What do you say? The SSD in Prague doesn't know Voldemort or the bakery
The new SSD councillors in the capital will reportedly have to overcome the obvious language barriers for the members of the Prague Board and the City Council to understand their renowned SDG comrades.
Aktuáln.cz "examinated" the members of the new SSSD Council to verify their knowledge of the marked slang language that has emerged in recent years in the Council, when the current members of the coalition ruled Prague.
The encrypted dictionary using Prague's political flower and cream during the previous period by Mayor Pavel Bém includes some of the most well-acquainting characters, situations and causes of the metropolis.
Unsurprisingly, it has been proven that the new councillors barely comprehend these well-known concepts.
At the very least, that's what they say.
"Who is Voldemort?"
"I really don't know".
"Rather, I'm a rookie in pr-gay politics," culture councillor Luká Kauck , said during the "colleaser" terminology examination.
And as far as the veteran politician is concerned, he reacted the same as Karel Beezina.
Maybe you'll know the "Golf hole" concept.
"Possibly it's the only expression of everyone you've asked me I use from time to time."
"Yeah I don't, I don't usually use slang".
From the glossary, whose definitions were published by the Aktuáln.cz newspaper last June prior to the SDG voting congress, the wording chose six examples, in particular those that are especially related to the municipal authorities in Prague.
The socialists had to explain the concepts "Voldemort," "Most golf hole in Prague," "going to the bakery," "True," "treasure of the capital" and "beautiful."
Three councillors agreed to submit to the test and a fourth, Antonín Weinert, who in the future will be responsible for education in Prague, initially agreed, but did not pick up the phone again.
Coroner Petr Dolínek directly refused to do so.
"I don't get involved in these things and I don't want to appreciate how civic Democrats have fun."
"What I don't care about is how we'll understand each other in the implementation of our programs," he said.
As has been seen, Councilwoman Luká Kaucka allegedly would not have realised that the previous title is an expression about an influential entrepreneur and a friend of the former Mayor Bém, Roman Janou-ek.
The word "Voldemort" doesn't say anything; the only term he hit in the exam is "the biggest golf hole in Prague."
"It's about the Blanka Tunnel," says Kauck, who immediately adds: "But I rather know from the news."
"I'm gonna have to aim for a language course," she jokes.
The Blanka Tunnel isn't the only one who recognizes in the golf hole the Blanka Tunnel, but also the Councillor of Environment Luká Placha.
Afterwards, both socio-democrats know who Voldemort and Luká Placha really even knows where to find that character; moreover, he suspects the meaning of "going to the bakery."
"I think it means to go to a company in Prague"; it will soon be unveiled to Placha that it is the headquarters of the Janouek companies.
Like Bezina, he indicates that such knowledge was obtained from the news, but that he does not use such a vocabulary.
According to the participants in the test, there is no such family glossary of colloquial terms.
"I asked my classmates, but it really doesn't look like we've got anything.'
"Rather, among us we use nicknames, sometimes abbreviations or we make punches with the names," says the president of the Ppraguense SSD, Petr Hulínsk, whose party companions call Hulda.
"At the school they called me Bíza, but I don't know if my classmates also call me that," he confesses his own nickname Karel Boezina.
The Aktuáln.cz newspaper tested the name and it works.
For his partymates, Weinert is Duda (similar to "yayo") or, less amicable, Dodek ("old"), representative Miroslav Poche, with whom they associated pre-election deceptions during party sponsorship, receives the nickname "Igrá-ek" ("playmobil") by some of his companions.
The nicknames, too, are the only expression Democrats tolerate in speech within the party.
He doesn't like to be called Hulda, but the Aktuáln.cz newspaper discovered even other nicknames for this pr-government president.
"The doctor of the rights of batons" apparently remembers that Petr Hulínská obtained his doctorate in law at the Bratislava police academy.
The government wants to limit the torture of "Witches," as published in a pamphlet
Women regarded as witches in Burkina Faso share certain characteristics.
They tend to be of old age, they can't work, for example for health reasons, they're unmarried and they don't have children.
Women who "return" whists are sent to an isolated area so they don't harm the rest of the town.
"Mills rarely suffer such else."
"Healthy young women don't isolate themselves either, because they help in work and have children," explains a nun who, in the capital of the West African state of Burkina Faso in a special residence of more than a hundred women expelled from their communities.
The local government has decided to oppose this unusual "social measure."
He issued an information pamphlet that should help prevent witch-hunting.
This, however, has a glue.
"More than 70 per cent of adults in Burkina Faso don't want to be able to read," the Die Standard newspaper warns about the downside of written awareness.
Accusations of witchcraft are also common in other African countries.
Ammy Hemmah, a 72-year-year-old Ammy Hemmah died last week when three women and two men torturing her, covered her with gasoline and burned it.
The news appeared on the BBC.
The authors claim that it was due to an unhappy accumulation of circumstances.
According to her version, the woman was placed in the oil that is used for rhyming.
It caught fire when the group wanted to "expose the evil spirits" of the woman.
The isolation of "Wizards" is supported by people at all educational levels.
The Der Standard newspaper quoted a 42-year-old entrepreneur from Burkina Faso: "Devourers of human souls always represented a danger," he said.
While in Burkina Faso the government tries to appease witch hunt, the president was still involved in the Gambia last year.
"The Gambian President Jammeh, after the death of his aunt, launched an extensive witch hunter who had allegedly been linked to his death and had tried to kill the president himself through black magic."
"To help him he brought shamans from the nearby Guinea who, protected by police, military and the president's personal escort, forcibly took men and women randomly chosen among the Gambian peoples for the president's property in Kanilai," Amnesty International said in its report.
On the farm, the people arrested had to drink a sheiable grass with hallucinogenic effects that, in addition, led to intestinal and kidney problems.
Two of those arrested didn't survive the stay at the estate.
After the international pressure, they finally released about a thousand people arrested.
Bakala's tenant: heated with charcoal stoves and signs higher rent
Elderly older and reticent man walked through the doors of the customer service center of RPG Byty of "charcoal magnate" Zdeek Bakala, the largest private landlord of apartments for rent in the Czech Republic.
Beneath his arm he wore documents with the new three-storey rental contracts: his, that of his, that of his daughter and that of his son, who is serving a sentence.
All three of them have just been hit with the liberalisation of rents.
Rather than 3,500 Czech kronor a month, the child will pay 4,100 kronor from next year for a two-storey two-stay flat with separate second-class kitchen.
"Furthermore, you don't have any hot water and the heating is based on a solid-boulder boiler," the man shows with the finger in his son's new contract, on whose firm he has full powers.
"But I've signed it," he explains half an angry, half-resigned.
The civil association BytyOKD.cz, which described the new rental conditions as "incorrect and indecent," advises tenants not to sign the new contracts and to wait until December 7, when it publishes a guide reporting on how to proceed in these cases.
But in the face of this, the man only makes a gesture.
"Everyone says one thing," he says with a nice face.
Ostrava is becoming a major liberalisation testing lab.
After New Year's, there will be the vast majority of the flats that, in the Czech Republic, have so far been under a regulated rent.
That's why Aktuáln.cz decided to visit the "laboratory" in person.
RPG's mining waiting room is one of four in Ostrava and, at 9 p.m. on Tuesday, it was already full.
Right next to the gates are the security guards and next to him a machine to take number.
"Rental 2011," can be read on a bright button on your touch screen.
When you press it, a little paper comes out of the machine with a number.
barely gives time to read "157" and the number already appears on the big screen located at the top of the room.
In a second, you're in the tail.
Soon after, there comes an elderly marriage to ask, but the crowd in the living room isn't here because of the new leases.
Most of the people come to solve the usual problems, such as in the past: requests for settlements, insurance issues and similar issues.
Letters with the new rental contracts will arrive in Ostrava at tenants of most of the 44,000 RPG-storey apartments around mid-November.
RPG Byty proposes a rent increase of between 15 and 3 per cent on the letters.
On 18 November a special window is opened in the customer service center for the new rentals.
"It's been running for a week, so it's just starting out," the workers who find themselves in it respond briefly when the Customer shows up as a journalist.
Leaving and old Bakala mining floors are the most sensitive issues in the area.
Prior to the elections, the Vice-President of the Parliament, originally from Ostrava, Lubomír Zaorálek, of the SSSD, and now appear continuously in the newspapers of the area.
"Twenty years later I want a new floor".
"They didn't even change my bloody dish from the bathroom," drowns the man mentioned in the introduction.
According to the workers, at the moment retirees come more than nothing to see if they receive a discount on the rent if they sign on time, as the RPG special offer promises.
They will also receive half of the discount on the February rent the remaining tenants who will sign the new contract before December 15.
However, the window of the mining "liberalization" certainly doesn't disguise the avalanche of doubts.
"Of the 360 customers who were here yesterday, they only asked about the rent in 2011 about forty to fifty people," estimates the window employee.
In the meantime, RPG proposes on the website and on its boards to hold individual meetings on contracts when necessary.
If the two parties do not reach an agreement on the lease, an independent judge will have to resolve the issue.
"This isn't a threat, it's about information about the only way to proceed possible and completely in accordance with the law," RPG warns on its pages.
"No one is going to be tried here," says one of RPG Byty's employees and a man standing on the street in front of the customer's service center, at least in part.
"I receive a pension of 13,000 and I have 5,000 left for food; I don't have the means to go to the courts," he says.
But immediately the employee sows the hesitation.
"We'll see".
"Mr. Zaorálek warns them in the media that they don't sign, so maybe people will wait," he says.
Rental regulation will no longer exist in most Czech Republic in early 2011 and, according to RPG Byty, its increase is an economic necessity.
"Current rentals still don't cover even the amounts necessary for the maintenance of the community fund," recalls Pavel Klime, director of the residential portfolio of the RPG Byty company, who adds that the careless state of the community fund that angers tenants is, for the landlord, the main reason to increase the rent.
While during the five years between 2005 and 2010 RPG he was able with rents to invest in apartments four billion, in the four years that will go from 2011 to 2014 the figure will increase to 5.2 billion.
The large screen hanging from the ceiling, in front of the seats of the waiting room, now repeats some data that stands out in red.
"The increase in rent in 2011 by 15 per cent and less applies to 31,200 apartments, i.e. more than 90 per cent of RPG's properties.
"In addition, it will always be a maximum of 7.50 Czech kronor a month per square meter," it is indicated on the screen.
The 400-floor rental of the RPG portfolio is only 38 per cent increased by 38 per cent.
"In such cases, it's an increase from extremely low amounts, of 19.66 kroner per square metre," RPG says.
At 2,600 apartments, the rent will remain unchanged next year.
RPG Byty vindicates that liberalisation will not suddenly affect rents, but will be scaled four to eight years.
In addition, it adds the following commitment: the company will invest all the money from the rent hike (wheat the graphic) again on the floors, mainly in savings in heating: new windows and heating.
Priority will be given to people who sign the rent hike agreement.
Architecs have understood that offices also need nursery
The Avriopoint administrative building in Brno is the new headquarters of the company Arch.Design.
No one would also say that he also hides inside a small nursery with everything to such facilities: walls of colors, creative furniture and even a miniature park on the outside terrace.
From October of last year, the children of Arch.Design's employees can come to her.
The architectural projects company Arch.Design, from which numerous known buildings of Brno came out, thought from the first sketches of the Avriopoint building to include a space for its mini-safe.
Around 90 people work at the company, mainly at the age of the baby boom, and they wanted to allow architects and delineators to be able to return to work soon after motherhood.
Along with the possibility of enjoying a reduced schedule, nursery is one of the benefits offered by Arch.
Builsurers and Architects in this way have the possibility to combine a demanding profession with the care of the children.
The mini-safety was an idea of Arch &apos; s own employees.Designu and, with the support of the management, managed to obtain funding from the European Union and the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs.
The company &apos; s mini-storage is financed through the European Social Fund under the Operational Programme for Human Resources and Employment, as well as the State budget of the Czech Republic.
Thanks to the financing granted (an endowment of more than CZK 2.8 million) they were able to purchase the equipment and, after two years, hire qualified staff and services.
When it exhausts the financing (two years old), the company Arch.Design has an obligation to keep the mini-safe in operation with its own funds for three years.
The preparation of the project lasted for one year and the submission of the financing application was supported by Economy Rating.
Children &apos; s care is in the hands of qualified personnel; a nurse and a teacher from the Ministry of Education are present throughout the day for their care. Nursery currently accommodates children without an age limit, although the majority of them are under three years of age.
Since it is a "mini-store," it can only simultaneously accommodate a maximum of five children, although with the shifts they come more during the day.
The functioning of the nursery is flexible to adapt to the needs of the parents and children of all workers have the same right to use the nursery, regardless of the hierarchy within the company.
Although it is an individual education and care, the contents of the mini-safety programme are comparable to the regular pre-school education programme.
Children not only play in it, but also receive quality teaching: from basic learning to preparation for the school, they teach to English.
The class not only has toys and board games, but also a computer for the children.
The presence of young children in the company itself will create an amicable and positive environment.
Parents can go to see their children at any time and avoid the stress of picking children late from school when they are away from their place of work.
Thus, the parents are more productive while they are working.
Unsurprisingly, this naturally generates a loyalty relationship between the worker and his or her employer.
In this way, Arch.Design is located among the companies they sign that they offer a great advantage for the admission of new workers: they support the family and, ultimately, this program strengthened the marketing of personnel.
Bloom house is still fascinating after 100 years. Observe Gaudí's indoors
The roof looks like the back of a prehistoric lizard, the stairs reminiscent of the dinosaur's column and the balconies look like the mouth of an open fish.
This is Casa Batlló, in Barcelona, of the Catalan architect Antoni Gaudí.
Due to her form similar to a skeleton, the premises nicknamed her "the house of the bones."
Its organic forms, its striking colors, the novel use of the stone, the fragments of tilts and mosaics, a facade reminiscent of flakes and ubiquitous gently curved lines, all this is typical of the secession of Antoni Gaudí.
The cover of the house plays with dreamy lines, delicate colors and textures removed from nature and fauna.
Paying more attention, there is a certain geometry, rhythm and a carefully thought-out order.
In the evening, the entire facade of the house seems even more impressive, since it is illuminated in such a way that the brightness of the colors is improved.
Serpent and the torsion of the forms continues to surprise us also within it.
Undulating wood copies the frames of the windows, the railings, the ornaments of the gates, the peephants and the signs.
Casa Batlló, which is located in the neighborhood of Eixample at number 43 of Passeig de Grácia, emerged from the remodelling of a house of 1877 by the architect Emilio Sala Cortés.
Reconstruction lasted two years and the house was completed in 1906.
The investor, the wealthy industrialist Josep Batlló, initially insisted on tearing down the previous building and starting from scratch, but he soon accepted Gaudí's idea of carrying out a novel and cheaper reconstruction.
However, Casa Batlló, made up of various apartments, was not designed by Antoni Gaudí alone.
The architect Josep María Jujol collaborated in the design.
In fact, in its plans of the project appeared ornaments for the representative apartment of the owner of the property and the mosaics used in the facade.
Other creators and performers are also workers on the facade.
Collaboration in Casa Batlló &apos; s proposals was the beginning of Jujol &apos; s successful collaboration with Gaudí.
Jujol participated in the construction of Casa Milá and Gaudí &apos;
Due to its organic form, the premises shortly after its completion nicknamed it "the house of the bones."
Others, due to the specific opening mechanism of the windows, began to call it "yawn yawn house."
It is also said that, at the top of the construction, the sword of San Jorge, skipper of Catalonia appears.
Other sculptural elements of construction hide religious motives.
Gaudí projected in the house a unique system for central heating season with ventilation vents and typical chimneys.
Your tilt of forty-five degrees may be odd.
During the construction, Gaudí did not fully respect the rules of construction of the moment and did not let himself be influenced by them.
Even so, the construction obtained the corresponding permit from the beginning, although obviously in it the data indicated on the planned reconstruction were so lazy that, two years later, at its completion, the city council was so shocked by the outcome that it requested to be corrected.
It bothered him, for example, that the construction included columns even to the public sidewalk.
The arrangements were never carried out, on the contrary: only fifteen days after the city council's request, Josep Batlló requested (and obtained) permission to rent the tallest two floors of the building.
The landlord himself was installed on the lower floors.
The second-floor gallery of the second floor was the lounge and on the third-floor bedrooms and the private part of the apartment.
The ground-floor was rented to a jewellery store.
The six-storey house is completely crossed by a staircase that leads to the terrace of the roof.
The basic elements of the reform consisted of breaking all angles and straight lines, covering them with curved walls.
Gaudí also collaborated with the modern concept of connecting the space.
The common part can be connected completely by opening the lounge doors and a larger group can communicate in this way without problems, without giving up the privacy needed for more intimate conversations.
The house, with the reconstruction of Gaudí, obtained not only a new facade, but also an unusual roof and two additional floors.
It's also a stunning how Gaudí plays with the light, white tiles and blue covering the inner patio and skylights.
Gambling with the two colors brings the maximum of light on all the floors of the house.
It was based simply on the principle that the white reflects more light than the dark colors, which on the contrary absorb sun rays.
Any person can visit Casa Batlló, which in 2005 was added to Unesco's world world heritage list.
On the first floor of the house there is the most spacious and most recharged apartment of those accessible to the public.
The landlords decided to open the doors to the public in 2002, on the occasion of the 150th anniversary of the famous creator's birth.
With the visits they confirmed, or undiscovered, that it is a very beneficial business and they expanded visits to the common area and the attic, including the terrace of the roof.
The price of the entrance to the monument is not insignificant, reaching 16.50 euros, although the students, retirees and the holders of tourist cards get a 20% discount.
The amount considerably exceeds the price of the entrance of Gaudí's other buildings in the city.
If you want to enjoy the real bed environment with your lizard towers, you should quickly befriended a resident of the neighbouring houses and visit him at home.
How the mattress is picked: women and men are looking for different things
None of a pompous frame for the bed; what really confers its quality to a good bed is the lying surface, i.e. the mattress and the somier.
Above all, they should be perfectly complemented so that they adapt to specific users.
Numerous elements come into play during the election, including the weight, height, state of health and sex of the owner.
It is also important the type of indoor in which the mattress and somier are located.
Typical example?
For example, in a weekend cottage that doesn't usually have heating, latex mattresses shouldn't be used because they "heat" more slowly.
In addition, they tend to weigh considerably more than the usual polyurethane mattresses.
It is therefore necessary to turn them around more frequently.
If you don't duly air the channels of the center and, on top, are located on a firm foundation, keep the humid humidity inside.
When it comes to choosing a bed, customs also influence.
Whoever has spent a lot of time abroad, often uses a very tall mattress with a soft layer and will have trouble feeling good on a lower and tougher surface.
"Almost every hockey player who came back from the NHL was complaining they didn't slept just as well."
"By his crushing shoulders, heels and hips needed mattresses with a soothing layer of viscoelatica."
"These types of mattresses are also recommended for people with diabetes who have had problems of the nervous system for a time, so they receive bad information from the joints and the skin," explains Sylva Hadamcziková, who specializes in padded furniture.
Lots of hockey players, as well as other clients, have long preferring viscoelastic mattresses.
"Today there's no problem anymore, 'because they've got these proper Tempur and the Italian Magniflex, but also the Czech Tropico brand.'
You don't already have to be an NHL player to be able to appreciate viscoelatica.
This "miracle" works very easily.
Optimize its stiffness and shape depending on absorbed heat or adapts to your body heat.
There's nothing puts pressure; during the night, it won't spin like after a tough game.
Simply, he sleeps better.
Viscoelastic also functions as protection against sores.
Choosing the size of the mattress, add at its height the length of its feet, i.e. between 21 and 34 cm.
A man or woman with a height of about 180 cm will need a bed between 210 and 220cm.
The minimum bread width for a person should be, in a double bedroom, of a total of 180 cm.
In the case of the single rooms, for example singles, it is recommended at least between 120 and 140 cm.
To make a possible tour also comfortable.
"If you don't, you can apply a simple standard to calculate the width: by lying face up, you should have your hands comfortably shrouded towards the elbows," advises Engineer Helena Prokopová, an expert at Cechu "alalouní" a dekoratér, who has been dealing for many years with the problem of healthy sleep and the ergonomics of furniture, a subject she teaches in specialized schools.
In the Double Rooms, everyone needs their own sleeper and mattress, so during the purchase you have to pay attention to combine the height of mattresses.
On the contrary, it will be necessary to mount the somieres at different heights, which is not always possible in all beds.
Foam mattresses should have a minimum height of 14 cm, models with dock interior a minimum of 15 cm, of which at least three centimeters are for the layer that adapts to the forms and softens.
"By touching it with your hand or by settling on the mattress, you certainly shouldn't notice the docks," warns Helena Prokopová.
Each mattress requires a certain type of somier.
The completely padded bedrooms, in which the mattress and the base are like Siamese, don't make sense in the bedroom.
Mattresses breath badly and you cann't wash your holster, which you don't often do today.
And, since every night we sweater between 0.2 and 0.75 of sweat, it is clear that after about five or seven years the mattress will begin to remind us of a large (and congested) sieve.
For this reason, the specialists reject the slogans that speak of a mattress life of up to 25 years of age or even a guarantee for life.
Everything's got, though.
That's why the sellers give a tip: on mattresses with a width of 120 or 140cm, carefully take care of the cleaning and washing of the clue, but better add a mattress protector that can be easily removed and washed.
Although mattress covers with zip closure are currently manufactured throughout their perimeter, so when they are opened they remain in two parts (something very important for washing and the size of the washing machine), their manipulation in the larger models (and for a single person) when lifting the mattress can look like a fight between a fly weight and a heavyweight.
In bed, instead of a somier, do you have a firm board?
Never.
At best for beds designed to spend one night visiting.
However, you must (if you don't already) ventilation holes on the board and it should be used with a mattress with docks on the inside and a minimum height of 15 cm with docks in bags (such as German Taschenfederkern) and a soft layer.
Polyurethane mattresses should not be used on such bases.
Latex mattresses (e.g. a combination of latex and coconut fibula and coconut fibulars) can be used for non-rangulated foil somieres and coconut fiber), although it's always better a somier with a larger number of foils, 28 or more, in which the foils in the lumbar area are reinforced and their rigidity can be regulated.
Arrive foil somieres should not be worn with a dock mattress, as it does not react to reclining.
In addition to the stiffness of the foil adjustment, the shoulder protection can also be used.
It is a special fixation of the foils that allows you to press or even tilt the foils in such a way that they "surround" the shoulders perfectly during the sleep.
It's a very important thing for the men who have very wide shoulders, it's a very important thing.
They are even rendezvoxed by mattress manufacturers who, in the "masculinos," began to use a softer area precisely around their shoulders.
They also remember the women, for whom they put a softer area around their hips.
For the most demanding customers, there are somieres with cross or tablet structure with the so-called multi-stortion cushion.
The pressure is distributed between different points and the tension is lowered.
You should also bear in mind that, even if you use the appropriate mattress and somier, if you use a bad pillow (big and "mullida"), most of the advantages will disappear.
The basic standard is simple: the pillow must hold the head and the corners can only reach the shoulders, so that the muscles of them can be relaxed.
Pre-size recommended size is 50 x 60cm, somewhat normal for example in Scandinavia.
lying on the side, the pillow must be at the same level (when it is combined with shoulder-builders with shoulder reinforcement or the softest area of the mattress) than the shoulder height.
Modern mattresses also have something to do with original mattresses filled with straw.
Manufacturers continue to increasingly often use natural materials or at least mixtures.
In fact, biological mattresses are fashionable.
Oil-based raw materials, with which polyurethane foam is manufactured, are filled with natural oils, such as ripper or soybeans.
In fact, virtually all known manufacturers use soybeans.
"Biological, in the case of mattresses, it means that the center is only made of materials of natural origin," explains Petr Va'í-ek of the Tropico Plus company.
Natural substances are also used during the manufacture of mattress cover, one of the first aloe vera.
But you can also choose microcapsules, which release a fresh aroma of forest or lavender.
There are also differences for both men and women.
While the men "refice" with breezy vegetable extracts, so the mattress case absorbs the heat and refreshes the body, women prefer to "heat" the blade.
Red pepper extract improves the circulation of the blood, so that women feel more warmth.
Favourites today?
Mattresses made of cold foam, that are more porous, with an adapted cell structure, which makes them more permeable than normal polyurethane and offer more permeability.
The combination of various cold and viscoelastic foam stiffs make mattresses from various areas that know perfectly well where the head, shoulders and heels are going.
A good-quality mattress will cost you 7,000 kronor up.
Those who perform a therapeutic function often have a lower VAT, since they are considered as sanitary means.
The first to gain this advantage in the Czech Republic was Gumotex.
It's hard to save on mattresses and somieres.
Offers can sometimes be approved from manufacturers or sellers, who sometimes offer two mattresses for the price of one.
For example, the Magniflex brand offers Duofreeze mattresses with a Memory memory foam attached to the case by 11,990 kroner.
The second one gets free of charge.
Viscoelastic era with an open cellular structuring and a high density began with Tempur "cosmic" material.
Initially it emerged as material for NASA, "because during astronaut knock-on take-offs they were subjected to a huge overload.
Tempur softens with the heat and hardens with the cold.
Therefore, the warm human body exerts a perfect effect and optimally adapts its pressure.
Therefore, it is ideal to prevent sores, but also to improve comfort during sleep.
Viscoelastic foam is used not only in mattresses, but also for sanitary products, for example for pillows that are placed beneath the legs and relieve them, as well as their circulation.
National manufacturers, for example, use Eucatherm C or Twinsaver foam.
More and more parents use people tracking devices to control their children
Fear for the children leads more and more people to think about controlling them by GPS.
The system, initially designed for the follow-up of vehicles, can also follow the movement of the people and thus contribute, for example, in the case of the disappearance of the nine-year-year-year-old Anika Janatková.
At the same time the device has the approximate size of a glimpse, but will soon be reduced to the size of a clock.
Many people were scared of the case of the missing Anieka and began to report on the means available to better protect their children.
Even at the cost of invading your privacy.
"We're all trying to tell each other that it's not about spying, it's not about spying, it's another way to reduce the chances of a tragedy," says Petr Pilin of Macro Weil, one supplier of tracking devices on the Czech market.
"I recognize that, in certain cases, you can abuse such devices, for example if they get into someone's purse without their knowledge, but we haven't run into such cases yet."
"Most customers actually buy it for security reasons," Pilin adds.
In his opinion, children should always know what the "cajita" is for and how it is used in an emergency.
Children's psychiatrist Dana Bosková warns of the fact that, if the child doesn't want to wear it, he'll just throw it away.
"Even the youngest children show great creativity when it comes to intentionally losing certain things if they want to prevent them from being controlled."
"Before, the parents relied heavily on the mobile phones, but they could be turned off as easily as the follow-up device," he says.
In his opinion, it is debatable whether such a device is really useful in the event of an attack.
"If it's a pre-prepared kidnap, the perpetrator will almost always check if the child wears some kind of electronic device that can reveal its location."
"Now it's the phone".
"According to extending the use of the follow-up devices, you will also look for them, since their sale will undoubtedly be carried out with a lot of publicity."
"In such cases, the criminals are always one step ahead of the police," the psychologist says.
However, it adds that such devices can help in the event of an impromptu attack by a paedophile.
According to Robert Eech of the RC Monitoring, which provides follow-up services for both vehicles and people, even in the event that, after an attack, the follow-up device is immediately destroyed, its presence can help.
"You can confide the device so that you regularly submit the position after a certain period."
"If he or she is harmed, it will at least facilitate the last position of the person in question."
"The most expensive devices have an inclination lyrter integrated, so if the person simply slips and stays immobile, the device will immediately emit a warning with the position in question," Eech states.
In his opinion, such a service can also be obtained from intelligent mobile phones with browsing, in which only special software should be installed.
The problem with current follow-up units is that at the moment they are devices similar to a match box or smaller mobile phones.
They can therefore be lost.
According to Macro Weil's Pilin, tests are currently being carried out with devices resembling a digital clock.
"Furthermore, they'll have the added advantage that a possible aggressor might not identify the device."
"Either identify it after a certain period of time, which can cause the police to gain time," he adds.
Although the follow-up devices are worn on the wrist or hanging, they will continue to use the same technology used to track vehicles.
It's about checking the position with a GPS system (which is used in the usual browsing in the car) combined with the function of a mobile phone that communicates the current position.
The army has plenty of wicks to surround the whole of the cottage, but it only lasts two years.
The Czech army bought 445 kilometers of ribbon to fire explosives.
Already last year the Ministry of Defence added 224 kilometers of fuse to its arsenal.
Therefore, they have stocks for 225 years.
Trouble's problem is that grind lifespan is two years, four at best.
The army bought the lynch for explosives from the STV Group for forty million kronor.
The general's personnel responded to MF DNES by justifying the purchase of such a huge amount of fuse, saying the soldiers are in need for the training maneuvers 60 kilometres a year.
According to the calculations of the pyrotechnics of the army, with whom MF DNES was contacted, it is decided, however, that such maneuvers should carry out such maneuvers with the frequent use of explosives each year also all generals, high-command officers and even secretaries and cleaners.
They wouldn't want either Defense Minister Alexandr Vondr and army captain Vlastimil Picek.
Only in this way could it be justified that the army spent sixty kilometers of fuse.
Such a length equates to 25,000 explosions.
Even if that were the case, the army would have enough stocks for more than ten years.
On the contrary, as the experts say, for the initial training of each new professional it arrives with a maximum of two meters of fuse, the army would with the repurchase (already adding the consumption of engineering or special units) sufficient stocks for the 225 indicated above.
Soldiers of the rapid intervention brigade complained personally recently to Minister Vondra because they say they barely have ammunition for various weapons, so they are limited even shooting practices over a matter of money.
And don't let's just talk about underwear.
"I also don't manage to understand that they repeatedly buy such amounts of fuse."
"But it's their decision and they'll certainly know why they're acting like this.'
"For us, the army is a good client whom, as a company, we are trying to satisfy," admits the commercial director of STV Group, Jan Duck.
The spokesman of the Ministry of Defense Jan Pejek defends the hundreds of kilometers of whale, saying he will also have stocks of this "munition" for at least ten years.
However, STV Group &apos; STV Group says the fuse has only a four-year shelf life and, in the case of pyrotechnic products for the two-year-old army or police.
"If it is stored in perfect conditions, it can last up to four," says one of the soldiers who, due to possible criticism of the army, does not want to give his name.
Upon the end of its useful life, each ammunition must be carefully checked to see if it remains reliable and will not harm the soldiers.
But these tests aren't free and require additional investment.
Another Ministry's argument is that by buying 445 kilometers of fuse, he obtained a discount.
"It cost us 60 per cent less than last year &apos; s request of 244 kilometers," says spokeswoman Pej-ek of the Ministry of Defence's procurement centre.
Only to compare: the annual gripping consumption of police pyrotechnics, which eliminate World War II ammunition or left by the Soviet army, has not already exceeded 1,500 meters for years.
In addition, the police always ask only for the right amount to exhaust the ammunition before its useful life ends.
It was chosen for safety reasons for pyrotechnics and, in addition, in the meantime you can obtain more modern and reliable types of fuse.
The Ministry already has clear difficulties in finishing tens of thousands of tons of ammunition at the time of the Warsaw Pact, when the Czechoslovak army of two hundred thousand people was preparing for a campaign on the Rhine.
According to the pyrotechnics MF DNES spoke to, after a couple of years there will be the following problem with the current one: you will have to remove hundreds of kilometers of old fuse, which is now being bought "cheap."
So far I'm still alive of the contacts I got thanks to the contest.
Upon winning the "Company of the Year" contest helped Luká Pytloun in the sale of the franchises of his hotel.
The last edition won the "Employment of the Year" competition by 28-year-old entrepreneur Luká Pytloun de Liberec.
His success story seems drawn from a film.
He loved to travel. Luká Pytloun loved to travel.
Abroad he noticed that in Liberec there was a need for quality accommodation at a good price.
With the savings he collected with seasonal jobs abroad and a bank loan, he bought a house that was half-destroyed five-floor.
Considering he was still studying his third year in college, he was really brave.
"Currently, the quality-to-price ratio of the accommodation in our area prompted me to do something about it," says Luká Pytloun.
He began renting the rebuilt first floor to foreign workers.
All the income he earned was reinvested in the building, which he gradually turned into a 54-bedroom pension.
Since then, in seven years in the business, he gradually managed to open in Liberec four pensions, two style restaurants and two hotels with a total capacity of around 300 in all accommodation categories.
The most comfortable of all was, until recently, the four-star Pytloun-student design hotel.
This year, however, it opened another of the same quality.
"We have just signed a contract with a hotel in Ostruv or Tisé, to which we have sold our franchise concept, and other people are showing an interest in a franchise from Pytloun," the number one entrepreneur said last year.
"To this sale of licenses for the opening of hotels it helped to a large extent by winning the 'Company of the Year' contest," Pytloun adds.
Victory increased its fame and popularity in both Liberec and the surrounding area.
"People recognized me in the street, they still congratulated me often and, I have to say, it was very pleasant," he recalls.
Undoubtedly, he advises anyone who wants to try a company that participates in the competition.
But he sees that it is not necessary to win, but that participation is already beneficial.
He states that, so far, he continues to live from contacts and acquaintances who, without the contest, might not have obtained.
"With some people that I met thanks to the contest I have started collaborating regularly."
Fame associated with success, in his opinion, also included in the employees,
in total about seventy.
"I've seen that they show greater loyalty and are proud to participate in something that earned general recognition."
"It's not, by any means, my own personal success, but it's the result of the hard work of the entire team."
"It's only my name that's on the front page," says Luká Pytloun modestly today.
To my successor I want a quieter year than we've had in Farmet.
The euphoria for victory soon dissipated because of the impact of the crisis.
Thanks to the glimpse of the glimpse, it's possible that Farmet's worst has already happened.
The owner of the company Farmet, Karel Eársk, received the first prize in the last edition of the Vodafone "Company of the Year" contest last year at the "Slava House" in Prague.
The jury valued his efforts, with which he managed to turn a small technician workshop into a firm with a turnover of hundreds of millions.
"I'm able to say the award not only cheered on me, but all my collaborators and improved our confidence to fight the competition," says last year's winner.
Farmet's company history begins with the desire to do something useful.
For this reason, when the State proposed to the father of Karel Eársk the rental of a small workshop as compensation for the seizure of properties during Communism, he did not hesitate too much; he relinquished his position in the company in which he worked and, with a couple of comrades, they founded the company with his father of plastic piles.
Soon after, they began to specialize in vegetable oil presses.
Eight years later they became the largest manufacturer of automatic presses in central Europe.
They also obtained a greater presence among the non-specialized audience after achieving success in the competition in the media.
"Not only did our Customers begin to regard the Farmet brand as considerably better, but foreign partners also began to value the collaboration with a company that achieved greater success in their country," says Karel ársk.
Last year perhaps it wasn't in Farmet just a sign of the euphoria of Victory.
It also gained recognition for the commercial results up to 2008 and, in part, from 2009.
And precisely 2009 was the toughest year for the sale of machinery.
The worsening of the crisis, which had not yet reached so many citizens, caused companies to stop their investment projects and begin to limit the purchase of new machines or technologies.
"Obvious, of course, we don't get rid of such a situation either."
"The revenues were reduced by 40%, we had to limit the production and we even had some layoffs," explains Karel ársk.
A viable company must, at the same time, even take uncharacteristic measures.
They, however, controlled the situation and maintained the company &apos; s profits, albeit at lower levels than before the crisis.
In spite of everything, where Farmet didn't slash expenses was in research and development.
"With the grants we have built a research and training center, reinforced the presence in the international market and obtained magnificent valuations at national fairs," he says.
Everything indicates that they have already left the crisis behind.
Gains are re-enhanced and, with them, the number of new employees.
"The new achievement expects a sense of victory to contribute, but in a calmer atmosphere," says Karel ársk.
A survey that unravels ridiculous rules
Absurdez's survey of the year is part of the business tournament for the fourth time.
He's already managed to eliminate some ridiculous rules.
The unfamiliar winner of the new edition of the Absurd of the Year survey was an obligation to bring to the administration documents that officials can access online.
The decision was made by more than a third of nearly 4,000 voters.
For example, the social security administration requested an excerpt from the commercial register, although its officials can obtain it with two mouse movements.
Thanks to the survey, this has changed.
"The Czech social security administration will no longer ask for an excerpt from the commercial register when it controls the insured person &apos; s haven't been in control of the insured person's impositions," SSZ spokeswoman Jana Buráová said.
The objective of the Abssurm of the Year survey is to consult the rules and regulations to be complied with by companies and entrepreneurs in the Czech Republic and to try to eliminate those that are absurd.
For the fourth time this year it forms part of the business tournament of the publication Hospodá-ské noviny and begins to create hobbies also among the general public.
While last year &apos; s edition had the participation of 3,000 voters, this year it almost reached 4,000.
And it'sn't surprising, since we still have several plenty-of-a-great legal obligations that must be respected simply to trade.
"Perhaps in the number of roles necessary for the tax return we will be in the first place worldwide," says Karel Havlí-ek of the Association of small and medium-sized enterprises and entrepreneurs of the Czech Republic.
Simplifying entrepreneurship rules have for years been a stated objective of all governments.
Compared to the rest of the world, however, the situation of improvement especially in the Czech Republic.
According to the World Bank, from the point of view of the conditions for having a company, the Czech Court is ranked 74th, i.e. eight places worse than last year.
The Absurdez of the Year competition took place for the first time in 2006 and every year is repeated in the same way.
Either can indicate some administrative or bureaucratic absurdity every year between April and the end of September, when the contest closes.
The proposals can be carried out on the website www.firmaroku.
He wins the absurdity that receives the most vows.
This year's survey presented more than a hundred, including 105 rules and regulations that make no sense.
Examples were sent by both entrepreneurs and professional associations and journalists.
Among the "finalists" of the contest was, for example, the requirement that vehicles for the maintenance of roads and motorways pay the toll or that entrepreneurs with twenty employees, two offices and two bathrooms paint the walls every two years.
This year &apos; s seventy-pronged list is published on the pages of the competition.
The proclamation of the contest, which is always held at the beginning of November in Prague's Wenceslas Square, is part of an event in which personalities from the political and social world usually participate.
This year, for example, Eva Svobodová, Director of the Association of Small and Medium-sized Enterprises and Entrepreneurs in the Czech Republic, or Petr Kuel, President of the Chamber of Commerce of the Republic, attended this event.
"Economic prosperity cannot be maintained if the state continually hinders entrepreneurs, so my objective is to eliminate every year a minimum of thirty paragraphs that currently unnecessarily complicate trade," said Minister of Industry and Trade Martin Kocourek.
Efforts to remove absurd regulations do not stay in drunken water.
In 2007, what annoyed people the most was the entrepreneur &apos; s obligation to communicate three days in advance to the business office of the permanent operating area, the intention to go with a post to a fair or exhibition.
This obligation was eliminated in the new industrial law.
The obligation that ended second in the number of votes in 2007, i.e. the communication to the labour office by its foreign workers of working trips of up to 30 days, was eliminated in the so-called green card law.
And in the third is the vanquished one.
The Absurdez of the year 2009 was the regulation for physiotherapists to have in their consultation a large mirror, a fridge and two soil scales.
Therefore, this year the Ministry of Health issued a communication stating that it had eliminated the obligation to dispose of a fridge.
A new category recognizes socially responsible companies
Eco-educative, humanitarian, educational or sports projects are valued.
The novelty in this fifth edition of the competition of Hospodá-ské noviny is the category called Responsible Company of the year 2010.
The task of this competition is to support responsible and fair companies.
"In this way we want to support the companies that deal with the impact of their activity on the environment and who support socially responsible activities," says Patrik Bartholdy, regional director of the mobile operator of Vodafone, organizer and sponsor of the competition, as in the case of the Rebel Degree of the year.
For this new special subcategoy of this year's edition of Vodafone's main competition Company of the Year 2010 companies were presented for which commercial success is not the only long-term objective.
The history of each of them is inspiring and would allow us to write an independent article.
Although the type of activities varies completely between them, the common denominator is the support and consideration they offer.
In one case, it is, for example, persons with disabilities, in other personal or employee health problems.
Fourteen finalists from all over the Republic were, for example, Et Netera's software company, which supports the formation of informal sports teams among its workers, who then participate in multi-sport tournaments.
Another example of company responsible for the Pardubice area is the company Isolit-Bravo, which has already been the main contributor of funds to sponsor disabled children for seven years.
There's a stationery that is particularly concerned about the health problems of its workers, since they make up more than 70 per cent of the workforce; that's why he received the title of Company responsible for the central area of the Czech Republic and in the Vodafone Company of the year competition ended up in third place in the region.
In Olomouc won accounting firm MiRyz Soft, which reduced working hours, even below the average of 35 hours a week, without reducing wages.
The title was also awarded because it mainly buys from Czech manufacturers, supports Community activities in the area and respects the environment.
"We are trying to recycle as long as possible so that our production and administration activities will have as much impact as possible on the environment," explains businesswoman Milu-e Ryznarová.
CEO TI Enlogit of the region of Estí also thinks about the health of its employees and, with each laptop, offered them an ergonomic chair and keyboards; in addition, it backs the work from home and in its business events it uses the catering services of a local protected company.
"Furthermore, we are supporting the Person in distress scholarship, which specifically assists the local Roma community," says the representative of the company.
They are also in a good place with a mention of the laundry company Praní a . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
"We provide benefits in food, transport, improvement of qualifications and leisure," says Josef Strapa, of the construction company of Bruntál Czasch, winner in the Moravia-Silesia region.
In the category of Responsible Business of the year, all the regional finalists of the Vodafone Company of the Year contest were automatically appointed to the questions of the registration form and sent it before August 15 to the organizers.
Organizers deleted from the evaluation the forms that were not complete or erroneous.
"If the representatives of the competing companies did not respond to the questions, they could not participate in the Responsible Business of the Year competition, but they are not disqualified from the "main" Vodafone Company of the Year contest."
"Indual applications for this competition are not permitted, as it is a subcategory of the main Business competition of the year," explains Karel Goldman of the Bison & Rose consultancy.
The police made arrests during a public auction at the Agricultural Fund building in Pilsen
Anti-Corruption police have made a number of arrests at the Agricultural Fund building in recent days.
The detectives handcuffed a local media entrepreneur and real estate entrepreneur.
It appears that this attempt to manipulate an on-the-going public auction for the restitution of the rights in such a way that the auctioned land was received by a specific interested party.
I was supposed to be paid for it to Lubo, half a million kronor.
"At the moment I can only say that the police are searching for a person for bribery during a public auction and public tender," Pilsen's chief of attorney-general Antonie Zelená told Právo, adding that at the moment she cannot divulge further details about the ongoing investigation.
After twenty-four hours, the 46-year-old businesswoman was released.
The way in which he was able to influence the public auction, given the lack of official information speculating, can only be speculated.
The spokeswoman for the Lubomíra Eerná Agricultural Fund responded to Právo &apos; shoes by saying that the police actually carried out the arrest at Pilsen &apos; s headquarters and it was during a public auction.
"It's about a client, not one of our workers," he said.
According to Esná, the public auction is called "Convener for the submission of proposals for the transfer of land in accordance with the law on the land" and is carried out when several interested parties appear in relation to the land offered.
"These persons, during the call, offer a value greater than that of their restitution, which must be transferred before the value of the land is announced."
"Depending on the offers, the order of the buyers is determined and the Agricultural Fund then transfers the land to the person who offers the largest offer," the Fund spokeswoman explained.
He had some information about who was going to be submitted to the convocation and, since at the same time he is an interested person because he acquired various restitution rights, he proposed to those concerned on the ground that he would not participate in the auction.
That's right, he's charged a commission.
This would explain that, at the time of his arrest, he had so much money.
He'd had special contacts at the Pilsen Fund for a long time.
It appeared in the case of the former PF boss Václav Sankot, who was accused of dispatching the replacement of land for which a construction decision had already been issued.
For example, Lubo-G. has just obtained from the Fund planned land for the construction of public infrastructurals, in particular where a large road should be built to the Borská pole industrial area.
Later, he changed them very beneficially with the magistrate for a house in the historic center of the city with a much higher value.
In this case, the city council had to intervene because, on the contrary, the blockaded land would avoid construction.
Brillies will have to pay an extra cost of several hundred kronor a month
Households will have to pay since January not only for all types of energy, but also for water and electricity, heating add-ons and, in some cases, higher rents.
As a result, families will have to remove several hundred krones a month more from the wallpaper from the portfolio than they are now.
The price of electricity rises by 4.6%, gas by about 2%, in some cities they will have to pay up to 10 per cent more for the water supply and many also increase the price of central heating.
Rising electricity for families living in apartments should amount to about 100 kronor more a year than it is now.
However, the main electricity suppliers reduced prices, as it has increased support for the production of energy from renewable sources, in particular photovoltaics.
However, the gas is impressed moderately, especially at the time when heating is most often used and, therefore, consumption is higher.
In this case, they must also have a couple of hundred more kronors a year.
While electricity and gas become more expensive throughout the country, the change in the price of central heating varies from place to place.
The price hike is mainly planned by the largest municipality.
For example, the homes of Pardubice or Hradec Králové will pay from January for the central heating about 50 kronor more a month, while Liberec's families will pay more than an additional 1,000 kronor a year.
Prague residents will also face a 9% increase in the price of water and heating, especially in the event that the city increases the rental of the infrastructure.
If the rent doesn't change, the Pairers will pay an additional 6.8 per cent.
Prices in Prague, however, will continue to be among the lowest across the country.
In the north of Bohemia, where current prices are among the highest, both rates will rise by 7.4 per cent.
Rental prices of 450,000 rented flats in Prague, where the regulation will be completed in January, can significantly increase.
For example, the company CPI Byty, which owns around 2,500 apartments in Estí nad Labem, will increase the rent from January by up to 100 per cent.
The company will bring to court those who do not agree with the new lease.
Cusported rentals, which benefit residents of about 300,000 flats, could increase next year by up to 10 per cent.
Young Czechs get drunk with Fernet and rum.
Fernet, rum, vodka and even nice whiskeys stolen from his parents.
These are the drinks that cause them to end up in junior medical stinking drinkers with an age of less than fifteen years.
Children who end up lying in the hospital continuously to get past the hangover are no longer an exception.
This stems from the experience of the doctors who encounter such cases in hospital pediatric admissions.
"Drinking beer or wine isn't fashionable anymore, 'cause it takes so long to get drunk.'
"Now fashion is strong alcoholic beverages," the head of pediatric pediatrics of the Hoovice Hospital in the center of Bohemia, Ludak Pelikán, told Právo.
"When they manage to catch a bottle of their laundry father, it can be whisky, but it's usually about Fernet and vodka."
"The vodka has the advantage of being lightened with juice and doesn't smell so much," he explains.
This is also corroborated by Motol University Hospital spokeswoman Eva Jurinová.
"Normally, it's usually Fernet or rum."
"That's modern liquor charcoal, particularly among schoolchildren."
"In a few cases, they use spirits that they took at home," Jurinová adds.
According to Chief Pelikán, it is drinkers of thirteen and fourteen who usually show up in hospitals.
Before Hoovice, where she has worked since this year, she worked for a decade in pediatrics in Motol, where ambulances took young people from the nearby areas.
"One girl had to come in maybe four times".
"She started at the age of twelve, when she was brought with 3.5 alcohol in her blood, in addition to a mixture with ecstasy," says the doctor of the department about this case.
"She'd be at the Holeovice fair at a dance party".
"What's interesting is that his mom didn't even be surprised that his daughter disappeared for 48 hours," the boss is surprising.
It is precisely the lack of interest of the parents or even their clear toleration of drinking by their children that is seen as the main cause of the increase in alcohol consumption among young people.
The experts point out that, in the case of the children, there can be no proper talk of dependence.
"It means an alcoholic to a person who has a chronic dependence on alcohol for several years for several years, with all the phenomena associated, which does not occur in children."
"In those who drank alcohol repeatedly, it's because of a systematically failed social or family environment," explains Chief Pelikán.
According to spokeswo spokeswoman Jurinová, forty drunken children were hospitalized this year at the Motol pediatric clinic until mid-November.
However, the stats only show those with severe intoxication and must remain in the hospital.
The young drinkers who, after the street medical examination, go home to the parents or the police, don't count. They don't.
In addition to the cases in which children simply gather after school and drunk warm-ups, for Pelikán there are three seasonal ups and downs to mention: witch day, New Year's Eve and the end of the school year.
"In such a circumstance, they drink for bad notes or, on the contrary, celebrate the beginning of the holidays."
"On witch day I've seen up to seven drunk-drown children at the same time in admissions," Pelikán adds.
Drinkers who spend the night in detox under the supervision of the doctors, paradoxically, have the advantage that the next day they don't suffer a terrible hangover at home.
Morning headache, caused by dehydration and a lack of glucose, is avoided with the injection of a sultry serum and the nutrient nutrients missing from the body.
"They're doing a lot better, "because after the hectic night, they wake up well and don't hurt anything."
"They're just confusing about where they are," Pelikán says, adding that patients often lose good mood at least when they see the expenses they have generated.
Ambulance transfers and hospitalization in a serious patient room comes to cost a dozen thousand kronor.
"I've often used the cost as a means to educate them, when on the tour I tell the children directly how much their drunkenness has cost and that they would have to spend the entire summer in a job for young people so you can earn enough to be paid that night," Pelikán adds.
Health insurance, in his opinion, perhaps as a regressive measure, which usually reimburses people for the costs of the treatment, does not do so in the case of children who drink.
Two L-159 jets are for the first time in the hands of others in Spain
Two L-159 aircraft out of a total of five, for which the Czech Ministry of Defence replaced the Casa C-295M aircraft departed on Tuesday for Sevilla, where aircraft manufacturer Casa - EADS is located.
It's the first time such a device has been in the hands of someone other than the Czech army.
At the moment, they were only abroad for internship or demonstration flights.
It's the two-seater version L-159T1, which so far the Czech army wore and was still in a position to float.
The remaining three aircraft are the L-159 single-seaters.
These are still preserved in Aer Vodochody and the intermediary of the sale, Omnipol, already took care of them last week.
Jan Pechak, of the defense press department, indicates that it is not yet known when Vodochody's planes will be physically withdrawn.
"The single-seater L-159 aircraft, on the other hand, were separated from long-prepared aircraft, with no investment or additional input from the Ministry."
"The two-seaters L-159T1 were traded in their current state," he told Právo Pejek.
He added that the army does not run out of two-seater aircraft, since in May 2009 the army signed with Aer Vodochody a contract amounting to 200 million kronor for the construction of four unnecessary L-159 fixtures for two-seaters.
One of the new jets was already received by the army in July, the other will be delivered at the end of the year.
According to Pexek, EADS pledged that the airplanes will be added to the equipment of some Allied countries.
"The contract clearly states that EADS is the one that withdraws the L-159 aircraft and that they will serve the practices of pilots from NATO countries."
"It's therefore up to the buyer to decide how it's going to use them in this framework," Pej-ek says.
For example, the Spanish army was not interested in the L-159.
The exchange of five L-159 for a Casa plane and the purchase of three other aircraft for 3.5 billion kronor were approved last year by the Mirek Topolánek government.
The Ministry of Defence did not convene a tender for the purchase and negotiated directly with EADS.
The current defense management says the exchange promotes the future export of L-159 abroad.
The Czech army acquired 72 aircraft from Aer and uses only 24, so it wants to sell the rest.
Purchase of Home planes may have judicial consequences.
The European Commission has already denounced the Czech Ministry of Defence before the European Court of Justice because it should choose the aircraft by conducting a European tender and not contacting a single manufacturer.
The army has already received three home planes and the last should reach Kbely Prague airport in fourteen days.
An index fin finger longer than annular indicates a lower risk of developing cancer
Guests who have the long-erfloating rate are subject to a lower risk of prostate cancer.
This is the surprising conclusion of a study published on Wednesday in a British cancer journal.
It could depend on the level of testosterone, the male hormone.
"Our results indicate that the relative length of the finger can be a simple test of the risk of prostate cancer, particularly for men aged up to 60 years of age," said study co-author Ros Eeles, a professor at the Institute for Cancer Research at the University of Warwick.
As a result, the finger could help determine which men should undergo a prostate cancer test, in particular if this characteristic is associated with a genetic risk or other harmful factors, such as family health problems, Eeles says.
During the period 1994-2009, he followed up with a British counterpart of more than 1,500 patients with prostate cancer and 3,000 healthy men simultaneously.
More than half of the participants in the study had the dial finger of the right-hander shorter than the annul.
They showed the same odds of prostate cancer as 19 per cent of those whose fingers were of the same length.
In the case of men with a longer-dister rate than an annular finger, this risk was reduced by 33 per cent; for men aged less than 60 per cent the reduction reached 87 per cent.
The relationship between the length of the two fingers in question, determined in the prenatal period, reflects different levels of hormones to which fetus is exposed in the womb.
A lower testosterone level corresponds to a longer index.
Previous studies have indicated that testosterone influences the development of prostate cancer.
The great Czech geneticist and former president of the Academy of Sciences, Professor Václav Pases, told TK that he knows the study, but that it does not seem to him that the relationship between finger length and cancer can be "demonstrated completely."
The Czech Republic under snow: the D1 and the D5 are closed
The Czech Republic, like most in Europe, dawned on snow-covered on Thursday.
The roads and sidewalks are mainly covered by compact snow and chitanieve machines circulated throughout the night in numerous areas.
In the morning the D5 road was closed between kilometers 89 and 93 towards Rozvadov due to several accidents of commercial vehicles and passenger cars, as well as the D1 in Brno in the direction of Prague.
The news was conveyed by the operator of the Automotokklubu (AMK).
At kilometre 91, a truck broke down, in '93 two commercial vehicles collided that caused injuries.
Also several cars ended up in the ditch.
There are also problems on the D1 motorway at kilometer 185 towards Prague.
Two lorries collided there.
Drivers must move slowly behind Brno at kilometer 190 or pass through Brno and be incorporated into kilometer 182 in Kovalce.
Several kilometre traffic jams have been formed there.
There has also been an accident on road D11, just near Prague.
Prague is draped by a multi-inch layer of snow and the thermometers indicate -7 oC.
