The Democratic Rust
Support for democracy in Latin America continues to increase, as well as support for private companies.
Censequence generates greater concern than unemployment.
Moreover, in much of the region, Brazil is seen as a more influential country than the United States.
These are some of the conclusions of the latest Latinobarometer survey carried out in 18 countries and published exclusively by The Economist.
Since the survey has been held regularly since 1995, it provides for a follow-up to changes in attitudes in the region.
The results of this year's survey, carried out in September and early October, highlight two related issues.
The first is the relatively animated mood reigning in Latin America.
The second is the increasing stability of attitudes towards democracy and its main institutions.
Support for democracy has increased significantly in a number of countries on South America &apos; s Pacific (see table 1).
For example, in Peru, where economic growth has reached an average of 6 per cent per year since 2002, support for democracy has increased from a low of 40 per cent in 2005 to 61 per cent this year.
It also grew in Mexico, where the economy has recovered after suffering a major decline in production last year.
However, only half of the respondents from Mexico and Brazil, the two most populous countries in the area, are convinced democrats, which lowers the regional average (see graph 2).
Around 44 per cent of those surveyed in the region were satisfied with the way in which democracy actually works in their country, the same figure as last year, but considerably higher than the 25 per cent recorded in 2001 (figure 3).
Around 34 per cent said they were trusting their country &apos; s congress and 45 per cent in their Government, the same amount as last year in both cases, but considerably higher compared to 2003 (17 per cent and 24 per cent, respectively).
A decade ago the economic turbulence in Latin America seriously affected support for democracy.
The fact that it has continued during the global financial crisis is probably due to the fact that many Latin American countries were only affected by a brief recession and enjoyed a strong recovery.
In addition, the existence of a stronger social safety net has helped to protect the poorest.
(The United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America reported this week that the proportion of Latin Americans living in poor conditions only increased marginally in 2009 and has fallen again this year, to 32 per cent.)
The mood, however, varies considerably from country to country (see graph 4).
The good economic returns of Brazil and the popularity of its outgoing president, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, is reflected in a general sense of progress.
This feeling is less common in Mexico and Central America, where the recession was more serious and violent crimes have increased.
For the second time since the surveys began, more respondents in Latin America mention crime, rather than unemployment, as the most serious problem their country faces (see graph 5).
Around 31 per cent say whether they or a close relative have been the victims of crime in the past year, down from 38 per per cent last year and is the smallest percentage recorded since 1995.
The most positive economic returns are reflected in greater support for private companies and the market economy: around 71% of respondents in the region say that private companies are indispensable for development, while in 2007 it was only 56% (see figure 6).
Around 58 per cent agree that the market economy is the only system that will allow their country to develop, up from 47 per cent last year.
It is interesting that 62% of those surveyed in Venezuela believe the same (and 80 per cent support private companies), although its leftist president, Hugo Chavez, is expropriating numerous private companies.
Brazil is the country with the greatest leadership in the region, according to 19% of respondents (compared to 18% last year), followed by the United States (9 per cent, the same figure as last year) and Venezuela (9 per cent versus 11 per cent a year ago).
However, the U.S. is the country considered most influential by respondents in Mexico and a large part of Central America, while Venezuela holds such a title in Ecuador, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua.
Wacraving elections
No one expected the general elections held on 28 November in Haiti to be perfect.
However, it happened in a much worse way than was feared by foreigners trying to help the country go back after their problems.
Votes were introduced in the polls, some people voted several times, ballots thrown in the sewers, destroyed polling stations and acts of intimidation.
Polling stations opened with hours of delay.
Numerous Haitians, including groups displaced by the January quake, roamed the area trying to find out where they needed to vote.
Some of them, in the end, gave up.
About a dozen presidential nominees, including several of the most popular, denounced fraud and called for the annulment of the elections.
Demonstrations, some of them violent, spread throughout the country.
But Edmond Mulet, Haiti's highest-ranked U.N. official, said it was "a relatively good election in many ways."
The observers of the Organization of American States and the Caribbean Community stated, with caution, that "we do not believe that these irregularities, however serious, necessarily invalidate the process."
In other words, the spectacle continues.
The electoral authority, which his opponents say is committed to René Préval, the unpopular outgoing president, and its candidate, Jude Célestin, should announce the preliminary results on December 7.
The tie is expected to take place in January.
Not all the hopes in relation to the elections have yet to be lost.
Opposition's two main candidates, Michel Martelly, a rapper and Mirlande Manigat, a former first lady, have withdrawn their initial petition calling for the vote to be invalidated.
But problems can still arise if the electoral authority declares that Mr Célestin barely has plenty of support, if he has any, has moved into the second round.
Given that the cholera epidemic sweeps across the country and more than a million survivors of the quake continue to live in tents, a big abstention is expected.
At the moment, many Haitians wanted to vote, but they did not succeed in doing so because of a lack of organization.
Around 250,000 identity documents, needed to vote, had not yet been circulated shortly before the election.
There's plenty at stake.
Aid donors see the election of a popular and legitimate government as an essential step in the reconstruction of Haiti.
As it has become clear, so do the Haitians themselves think.
Moving tiptoes towards the center
In the weeks that have elapsed since she lost her husband, Cristina Fernández, the president of Argentina, has enjoyed great sympathy among the public who have increased her popularity rate by 20 points, to more than 50%, the highest level she has registered since her election in 2007.
However, it could turn it into a successful campaign for a second term in next year's elections will depend on whether the clearly-stricken Mrs Fernandez leaves aside the intransigence of her defunct husband, her predecessor and political partner Néstor Kirchner.
At this point, the indications are inconclusive.
Mr. Kirchner, who continued to take charge of economic policy even after his wife succeeded him as president, had a habit of producing budgets that underestimated tax revenues, economic growth and inflation.
This allowed the presidential marriage to spend the extra income at its discretion.
In theory, Ms. Fernandez's government lost its majority in Congress last year.
However, the opposition is divided, a situation that has worsened following Kirchner's death from a heart attack on October 27.
Some dissenting members of the Peronist-to-wage movement have clashed with Ms Fernandez.
However, after chaotic sessions in which one deputy punches another in the face and other deputies accused the government of bribery, Congress decided to reject the 2011 budget. Congress decided to reject the 2011 budget.
"I won't argue," the president said.
The 2010 budget will therefore be implemented next year, which will give the president an even greater margin of action when it is spent.
The economy is improving considerably: it is likely to grow by up to 9 per cent this year.
The official inflation rate currently stands at 11.1 per cent, but numerous private calculations, in addition to the wage requirements of the trade unions, place about 30 per cent.
Mr. Kirchner, who took office in 2003 when Argentina was on the verge of economic collapse, made growth and poverty reduction his priorities, rather than control of inflation.
He refused to negotiate with the IMF, lobbied the private sector and caused INDEC, the stat institute, to adapt official inflation statistics.
Will President Fernandez now delay these policies?
It has convened meetings between the private sector and trade union leaders.
In addition, its officials say they want to limit wage increases next year to 18 per cent.
The economy has been boosted by the world's hefty prices for Argentina's agricultural exports.
Inflation, however, has limited the growth momentum achieved with a major devaluation in 2002.
Presumably for this reason Mrs Fernandez is showing signs of wanting to restore Argentina &apos; s financial ties to the rest of the world.
At the beginning of the year, the government reached an agreement with the bondholders who had refused a previous forced restructuring of the debt that Argentina had not paid in 2001.
At the moment, it appears that Mrs Fernandez is about to conclude an agreement with creditor governments grouped together at the Paris Club, to which she owes around $6.7 billion.
The Kirchners always insisted that this would not involve IMF supervision, as usual.
Last month, however Amado Boudou, the Minister of Economy, announced that the Fund will offer "technical assistance" to draw up a new national consumption price index, which appears to be the prelude to INDEC cleaning.
It's not clear whether President Fernandez wants to remain in politics when she finishes her term, considering that she no longer has her husband by his side.
If it does, you'll need to earn the support of the middle-class, much of which you've tired of Kirchnerism.
When the Argentinians return from their summer vacation in March, the wave of sympathy for her will have waned.
"If you want to win other elections, you will have to decide whether or not to repudiate many of your husband &apos; s policies.
Half trunk by China
Voters in Taiwan are slowly approaching a kind of closer union with the mainland.
What do you think about your great sibling on the other side of the strait?
More than 7.5 million voters, 70 per cent of those eligible, flocked on November 27 to elect mayors and councillors from five urban districts in what looks like a wide-ranging assessment of the president, Ma Ying-jeou.
Mr. Ma himself has become a proponent of feelings for the continent, forging innovative business ties, including a part-time free-to-trade pact, the biggest change in relations with China since 1949.
Mr. Ma passed the ordeal, but by a short time.
