Grammy Awards nominations: Michael Daugherty and Steve Mackey for Best Class Classic Album Address
Nominees in the field of classical music for the 53rd Grammy Awards include a mix of musicians and songwriters from around the world.
The main nominees were for Michael Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina," by piano and orchestra, of five movements, and Steve Mackey with "Dreamhouse," an eclectic three-model orchestra.
Among the nominees with ties to Southern California are the Estonian composer Arvo P?rt for his Symphony No 4, "Los Angeles," a piece commissioned by the Los Angeles Philharmonic and premiered at the Walt Disney Concert Hall in 2009.
Los Angeles-based songwriter Avner Dorman is also nominee for his Mandolin Concerto.
Classical music nominations are distributed into thirteen categories that cover orchestra, opera, choral and solo albums.
The classic producer category of the year includes five nominees: Blanton Alspaugh, David Frost, Tim Handley, Marina and Victor Ledin and James Mallinson.
The nominees for the classic music album are Bruckner for Symphonies No. 3 and No. 4, of the Orchestra Royal Concertgebouw; Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina" and "Metropolis Symphony" of the Nashville Symphony Orchess; Steven Mackey for "Dreamhouse" of the Boston Modern Orchestra Project and Synergy Vocals; "Sacrificium - the Art of the Castrati" of several artists; and Verdi's "Requiem" of the Chicago Symphony Orchestra and Chorus.
In the opera category, the nominees are Berg for "Lulu" of the Royal Opera House Orchestra; Adolf Hasse for "Marc Antonio e Cleopatra" by Ars Lyrica Houston; Kaija Saariaho by "L'Amour de Loin" by Deutsches Symphonie-Orchester Berlin; Rodion Shchedrin for "The Enchanted Wanderer" of the orchestra and choir at the Mariinsky Theatre; and Arthur Sullivan for "Ivanhoe" of the BBC National Orchestra of Wales.
Composers nominees nominated for a Grammy for contemporary compliments include Pert for Symphony No. 4, Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina," Hans Werner Henze for "Appassionately Plus," Magnus Lindberg for "Graffiti" and Shchedrin for "The Enchanted Wanderer."
Symphony No. 4 premiered at Disney Hall in January 2009 in a session recorded by the Los Angeles Philharmonic, directed by Esa-Pekka Salonen.
The roughly 30-minute piece nicknames "Los Angeles" and features former oil tycoon Mikhail Khodorkovsky, jailed in Russia.
Dorman's "Mandolin Concerto" is nominee for the instrumental solo performance with an orchestra.
The Los Angeles songwriter wrote the 15-minute piece specifically for the mandolin Avi Avital, who performed the New York-based Metropolis Ensemble concert with the Metropolis Ensemble.
"He composed it with sounds from the Middle East and numerous stopovers and rhythms of North Africa," Dorman said in a telephone interview.
The 85-year-old conductor Pierre Boulez was nominated in the Orchestra Function category for directing the Chicago Symphony Orchestra during a recording of Stravinsky works.
Nominated instrumental soloists include pianists Nelson Freire and Marc-André Hamelin; organist Paul Jacobs; violinists Julia Fischer; and harpist Sarah Schuster Ericsson.
The nominees such as vocal soloists are all women this year: Anne Sofie Von Otter, Cecilia Bartoli, Lucia Duchonová, Vivica Genaux and Measha Brueggergosman.
In the classic mixing category, soprano Jessye Norman was nominated for his recording of "Roots - My Life, My Song."
