An arsenic-resistant microbe raises the possibility of new ways of life on Earth... or elsewhere
The discovery of a strange bacterium that can use arsenic as one of its nutrients widens the possibility of finding new ways of life on Earth and possibly on other planets.
Although the researchers uncovered the curious bacteria here on Earth, they say it shows that there may be life forms without the main elements they considered essential.
"This organism has a Double capacity".
"It can grow with phosphorus or arsenic".
"That makes it a very peculiar thing, although it doesn't need to be a kind of really 'alienient' lifestyle," said Paul C. W. Davies of Arizona State University, co-author of the report that appears in Thursday's online edition of the journal Science.
For a long time, it is considered that there are six great essential elements for life: carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur.
However, the researchers have discovered that the bacterium, found in Mono Lake, Calif., may continue to grow after replacing phosphorus with arsenic.
"It's making you wonder what else it's possible," said Ariel D. Anbar of Arizona State University, co-author of the report.
As Anbar pointed out that the finding is important for the search for life on planets other than Earth, since researchers need to be able to recognize life, to know what it looks like.
The study focuses on a microbe discovered on Earth.
However, the convening of a press conference to discuss it, at which no details of the discovery were disseminated, generated widespread speculation on the Internet about the possibility that the report would indicate the discovery of extraterrestrial life.
He didn't.
What the discovery "yes shows is that in other planetarious environments, there could be organisms that use other elements to promote their biochemistry and that the "standard" set of "standard" elements that we consider indispensable to life may not be so fixed," said Charles Cockell, a professor at the Planetary and Space Sciences Research Institute, Open University in Milton Keynes, United Kingdom.
Cockell wasn't part of the investigation team.
"This work is novel because it demonstrates the substitution of one element with another in biochemistry and basic biochemical structure," Cockell added.
Disagreement did not happen by coincidence.
Felisa Wolfe-Simon of NASA's Astrobiology Institute, who led the study, focused on Monkey Lake because it has elevated arsenic levels.
Arsenic and phosphorus are similar from a chemical point of view, so he speculated that a microbe exposed to both could replace each other.
"Arsenic is toxic mainly because its chemical behavior is similar to phosphorus."
"It can therefore be difficult for agencies to differentiate them."
"However, arsenic is different enough that it doesn't work as well as phosphorus, so it enters and, to put it in some way, the biochemistry machinery creeps," Anbar explains.
Investigators surveyed the bacteria known as GFAJ-1 and exposed them to increasing concentrations of arsenic, to which they managed to adapt and used to grow.
The microbial grows better with phosphorus, but proving that you can live with arsenic in its place raises the possibility that arsenic-ushea-using form may exist naturally, whether in other areas of the Earth or on another planet or moon where arsenic is most common.
Jamie S. Foster, assistant professor of microbiology at the University of Florida, pointed out that the idea that arsenic could be replaced with phosphorus is not new, but there had never been an example to prove that it works.
Arsenic was more common in the early ages of Earth, he noted, so researchers speculated that the first life forms may have been used.
"Yes, it suggests that there could be other means of generating life, not just the way life formed on Earth in the past," said Foster, who wasn't part of Wolfe-Simon's research team.
The research was supported by NASA, the Department of Energy and the National Institutes of Health.
