The lion is poised to roar again, as MGM reshuffle gets final court approval
After 18 months of turmoil and turmoil, MGM has finally gained the green light to start again.
On Thursday, a federal bankruptcy judge in New York officially approved the "pre-arming" plan by creditors to wipe out about $5 billion in debt, paving the way for the 86-year-old entertainment company to focus its efforts on the production and release of films and television shows.
Thursday's ruling confirms that the plan fulfills the demands of the U.S. bankruptcy code, so there are no other legal hurdles for MGM to relaunch under the founders of Spyglass Entertainment, Gary Barber and Roger Birnbaum, who will become the chief executives of what will be a reduced study.
The staff will be reduced from 400 to approximately 320 members.
In fact, MGM won't leave Chapter 11 until JPMorgan Chase closes a planned $500-billion-dollar credit facility to finance the operations and production of the new MGM.
The investment bank is currently syndicating credit, which on its own is contributing $75 million, and is expected to be funded in mid-December.
The restructuring process of the study began in May 2009, when investment bank Moelis and Co. was tasked with advising on the management needed to order its finances as it struggled to make $300 million-a-year interest payments.
In August, director Harry Sloan was replaced by Stephen Cooper, a former Enron boss and bankrupt business recovery expert, known for his work at Krispy Kreme Doughnuts.
Attempts by Moelis and Cooper to resolve MGM's financial truffles and satisfy the studio's more than 140 creditors required much longer than expected.
After several months of discussions at the end of 2009, the parties agreed to put the company up for sale.
The auction, however, lured few bidders and none willing to pay for the more than $2 billion the creditors were looking for.
Earlier in the spring of this year, he returned to the lay-out table as MGM's team began working on a restructuring plan that, after months of debate and internal disagreement, was finally backed by creditors in late October.
When MGM is officially bankrupt in the next few weeks, Moelis and Cooper companies will receive multi-billion-dollar bonuses for their work.
Moelis and Co., which has already received $1.2 million, will get a $9.5 million payout.
Cooper's companies, which have been raising $375,000 a month for their company management services, will receive "successful savings" of $4 million.
Barber and Birnbaum are planning to resume production at MGM, which premiered only one film this year, and collected an annual slate of seven or eight films that will be released in 2012.
They also plan to co-finance two "The Hobbit" films, along with Warner Bros.
The studio needs to arrange a separate $265-$ 275 million loan to finance its share of the two films, which will be directed by Peter Jackson.
Investigator Carl Icahn, who owns about 18 per cent of MGM's debt, had been attempting to arrange a merger of the studio with Lions Gate Entertainment, in which he is the largest shareholder.
However, recent talks between the two companies did not result in an agreement.
