He wanted the feminine members of Chengguan, the corpulsy police of the urban order of China, feared and despised by their whimsical strong measures and their inclination to violence, to communicate a certain impression to a handful of spectators.
"Filers".
"Look up".
"Show them," he whispered, "How are the pretty girls?"
Four girls who barely spent adolescence in white glosses and identical olive green jackets and tight pants to draw attention.
Four pairs of perfectly aligned black shoes.
Four elegant hats perfectly placed on top of the hair straddled with buns in blue and white striped buns.
"Personally, I don't think they have a normal semblance like it," Mr. Tang said surprisingly.
"Motions are nice".
More than one government has been trying to clean up the image of China's urban inspectors.
A city has ordered that all new recruits have a bachelor &apos; s degree title.
Guangdong Province switched the greyish-green color from the uniforms to a supposedly more attractive blur.
Wuhan in central China replaced the use of force with intimidating glimpses: in 2009, according to a report, 50 officers surrounded a crazy snacks cart, and watched him with his frown constantly frowned for half an hour, until the street vendor picked up his stuff and walked away.
Xindu, an urban district of 680,000 in Chengdu, has chosen a radical makeover.
Since 2003, the district has added to its urban police 13 women, expressly chosen because of their appearance, proportion and youth.
The idea is to give stiff cop a softer, more feminine side.
Sadly, until Scarlett Johansson would have to fight to increase China's tiny respect for these squads in the city.
"And for a good reason," the critics would argue.
Contrary to the police, these officers are only authorized to enforce city ordinances, imposing fines and other administrative penalties.
But the Chinese news media routinely portray a different reality.
In January 2008, Hubei province inspectors beat a passer-by to death after he used his cellphone to shoot them while dissolving a protest against a waste dump.
Last year, a training manual for inspectors in Beijing was shouted and posted on the internet, describing it as effectively plaguing criminals without making them bleed.
Earlier this year, a wandering Shanghai watermelon saleswoman suffered brain damage after a scramble with five officers.
A violence-packed video game, available to download online, features Chinese-trained inspectors attacking street vendors.
"Chengguan has left a scar in the government," lamented China Daily, a national publication, last year after another controversy over tactic.
The diary demanded "really meticulous cleansing."
Sceptics say that the current approach is a long way from this.
After the district requested eight new female recruits through a notice in October, an publisher in The Beijing Evening News wondered if women had royal duties or were simply a stubborn distraction.
The answer seems to be a bit of both.
The district's warning called for female candidates ages 18 to 22 with a good figure "and the five harmonious facial traits."
They had to have an above-average height, greater than 1.6 meters or 5 meters, 2 1/2 in inches.
Retirement at the age of 26 is mandatory.
Officials said the work was physically too arduous for women over 25 years of age.
"His image is the important thing," an anonymous district official told Rednet.com, a quasi-government website.
"First, the external qualities of the candidates will determine if they meet the requirements, such as height, weight, facial traits, etc."
Then comes tempera "and indoor qualities."
Feminal chengguans are like vases, he said, adding, "in addition to being vases, they will have other responsibilities."
Zheng Lihua, deputy director of the district city's administration office, isn't eager to endorse that description.
But he noted that high-altitude demands were standard in many Chinese job warnings for both sexes.
Then there's the demand for harmonious facial traits.
"If this means being handsome, it's a matter of debate among the Chinese.
Probably the disabled or disfigured must not be presented.
"We can't be able to allow a lave person or a hunchback to come to work here," Mr. Zheng said.
"His image wouldn't be okay".
Liu Yi, who patrols Baoguang Square near a monastery, is 22 years old, with his cheeks and subtly curved mouth.
"She doesn't think the acccentuation in her appearance is sexist," she said.
"Do you think I look sexy in this uniform?" he asked with a sardonic gaze.
His co-worker with dimples, Xu Yang, 21, said "Our work must present the image of the city."
They also don't object to their work on time, they said, because they harbor older career ambitions that simply scare away vendors in the alleyways where they are supposed to circumscribe their business.
Every morning, the squadruple faces about a dozen street vendors who launch themselves on foot or by bike, trying to sell as many buns or bowls as possible before being kicked out.
"Lord Wang, you have to leave".
"We've told him many times," Ms. Xu said as a saleswoman slipped on foot, temporarily abandoning her bike cart with noodle gadgets.
The officers describe his duties as more monotonous than exhausting.
"That's pretty much the same every day," said Huang Jing, 20-year-old Huang Jing, who studies marketing in his spare hours.
"Very rutire".
One reason is that female officers lacked the power of their male counterparts to seize vendor merchandise.
They can only threaten to report violators to their male supervisors.
This tends to protect them from sudden public demonstrations of animosity against the bureaucracy, which are common in all parts of China.
Earlier this year, Hundreds of citizens in Kunming, the provincial capital of Yunnan, mutined after the fake rumour spread that Chechen officers had murdered a saleswoman.
More than a dozen Chechen police or officers were injured in the evening episode; 14 government vehicles were overwhelmed or caught on fire.
At the moment Xindu has avoided such violence.
Calm is barely guaranteed.
With just two blocks from peaceful Baoguang Square, where female officers patrolled that morning, more than 50 people gathered in a corner.
The officers had confiscated a motorbike that was being repaired on the sidewalk rather than in a workshop, as required by the regulations.
The motorbike owner shouted rudely.
The situation stagnated for 15 minutes before the bad-faced officers made its way to the elbows to their vehicles and left quickly with the motorbike and its owner.
Li Xuedong, 40, a co-ordinator assigned to the men's squad, stayed behind, with his white fountain turned around to hide his name.
Like female officers, co-ordinators, men ages 40 and older, play a purely supportive role.
Contrary to them, they aren't taught to keep an elegant image.
"Sometimes we fight verbally".
"Sometimes we fight physically," Mr. Li said naturally.
"Most of all the time it's the public that starts the brawl".
Prosecutors won't bring charges against Senator Ensign
The senator's lawyers announced on Wednesday that Justice Department prosecutors have exonerated Sen. John Ensign of the criminal charges that stemmed from his affair with a former campaign aide and his efforts to secure lobbying work for the woman's husband.
"The decision ends the most serious legal threat faced by Ensign, the Nevada Republican who was seen as a possible Republican presidential nominee for 2012, until the scandal about his affair made national headlines last year and damaged his career.
Ensign's office said he was "saferly pleased" with the decision, which came after a full-year investigation.
The senator could still face disciplinary action from the Senate ethics committee.
Ensign's lawyers said the Justice Department had "informed that Senator Ensign is no longer an objective of his investigation and that he has no plans to bring charges against him on this issue."
The U.S. Justice Department declined to comment on the case.
The announcement came as a surprise to many ethics lawyers outside the Justice Department, who said there appeared to be significant evidence to indicate that Ensign may have confided in violating a federal criminal ban on lobbying.
Intermediates, e-mail and other documents that were first released in October 2009 in the New York Times indicated that Ensign had helped Douglas Hampton, who was one of his aides, to obtain lucrative work by lobby, to secure the silence of Hampton and his wife, Cynthia, about the senator's affair with her.
Ms. Hampton once worked as treasurer of an Ensign campaign.
He contacted a number of political backers to find Mr. Hampton's job and pleaded on their behalf after Mr Hampton had hired Mr Hampton to locate for them, according to the documents and interviews.
Since Hampton had recently been an aide to Ensign, he was excluded under the lobbying law for his ex-employee or the Senate for a year.
Politication experts said the unusual arrangement between Ensign and Hampton, who were close friends before the affair, came at occupancy with the so-called "revolving lobbying ban," which seeks to restrict ex-state employees from using their connections to do personal business.
Of the evidence that has been made public, it "appeared to be a prima facie case of a conspiration to substantially violate the revolving statute," said Stanley Brand, a Washington lawyer who specializes in defending clients investigated by the government.
While Justice Department officials refused to explain the reasoning behind the decision in the Ensign case, Mr. Brand and other ethics lawyers said the department appeared wary of accepting political prosecutions after several major setbacks for its public integrity section, including last year's decision to drop out of prosecution against former Senator Ted Stevens, amid charges of malproportional misconduct.
The federal prosecutors "are clearly cowardly," said Melanie Sloan, Executive Director of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington, an advocacy group that brought several complaints against Ensign in the case.
"That basically means high-ranking government officials can now suppose the laws don't apply to them," he added.
Paul Coggins, Ensign's lead lawyer, said in an interview that he received a phone call from a Justice Department prosecutor on Monday and another call the next day, telling him that the department didn't want to bring charges against his client.
Ensign &apos; s defense team had been co-operating with prosecutors in trying to persuade the senator, as his office reiterated in a statement on Wednesday, "acted in accordance with the law."
Coggins declined to elaborate on the legal arguments he gave to prosecutors.
"I don't feel comfortable going into details about my discussions with the Justice Department, I can only say that we think they made the right decision," he said.
Last month, the Federal Elections Commission decided to change an investigation into a separate complaint by the ethics group, which alleges that a $96,000 payment from Ensign's parents to the Hampons violated the federal electoral law.
The electoral commission said it didn't have enough evidence to prove that the money was nothing more than a gift.
NV Energy is an important Nevada company that had long supported the senator, which got a lobbying job thanks to the help Ensign gave to Hampton.
An e-mail sent in December 2008 showed that after the company accepted Hampton as a lobbyist, Ensign's boss-in-chief John Lopez communicated with Hampton and said he was "shocking" the Home Office on a disputed issue.
Ensign also attended a Lunch with Hampton and another Nevada client, Allegiant Air, and called the transportation secretary to set up a meeting on behalf of the airline after he had hired Hampton to lobby.
When news of the affair first surfaced last year, Ensign apologized to his constituents and renounced his position as the fourth Republican leader in the Senate.
After maintaining a low profile for many months, two weeks ago Ensign told reporters that he planned to run for a third term in the Senate.
When Ensign spokeswoman Jennifer Cooper announced that Ensign would no longer be the subject of a criminal investigation, he said the senator hoped the ethics committee would quickly follow the example of the Justice Department and conclude its investigation.
"Senator Ensign is eager to continue his tough work on behalf of the people of Nevada," he said.
Cheshire's appeal will point to Twitter
There were half a dozen of them "kissing" in the ranks of the courtroom.
Minimal by minute, they sent reports of the appalling testimonial via Twitter.
Ultimately, the defense says, there were more than 140,000 Twitter messages about the process that addressed the rape and the children strapped to their beds in a house that was set on fire.
On Thursday, the widely-contaminated trial of Steven J. Hayes, who was convicted of the murder of three members of a family in Cheshire, Connecticut, is poised to reach an official end, with the death penalty judge cast by the jury.
But Mr. Hayes's lawyers have filed lawsuits in the court delineating the arguments of the appeals they are likely to occupy the courts for years.
One of the most provocative claims that intense reports of the process, including the widespread use of Twitter by mass media journalists, created "a circus atmosphere," and such widespread and immediate saturation with exalting details caused public passions to exert an inappropriate influence on the jury.
The outcry could force accommodation judges to grapple with the question of whether the new technology requires new rules in the courtroom.
"According to what I experienced during this process, I think there's a real danger that the inclusion of the new technology in the courtroom is affecting the rights of the individual to have a fair trial," said Thomas J. Ullmann, lead defense attorney for Mr. Hayes.
U.S. Chief Justice Jon C. Blue of the State Superior Court latched the lawsuits in a ruling last week, saying the news media were "certainly controlled" and that there was no evidence that the jury had been led away by passion.
But it probably won't be the last word on the subject.
For decades, the appellate courts have conceded that coverage of successful cases can raise questions about the impartiality of a process.
In a historic 1966 ruling, the U.S. Supreme Court overturned the conviction of a Cleveland osteopath, Dr. Sam Sheppard, for the murder of his wife, saying in his decision in the Sheppard vs. Maxwell case that there was "a carnival atmosphere in the process" due to the media.
It's believed that the crime had been inspired by the television show and the film "The Fugitive."
Some of the lawyers said the appellate courts, which examined Mr. Hayes's conviction half a century later, could be intrigued by the argument that new rules might be necessary to control the form of communication from courtrooms, something unimaginable at the time of Dr. Sheppard's case.
"There's a general cause for concern about the impact of new media" on high-profile processes, said John J. Donohue III, a Stanford Law School professor who studied the Connecticut death penalty and wrote a report saying it was arbitrarily imposed.
Professor Donohue said some appellate judges may be intrigued by the idea that limiting immediate communication from the court chambers could make trials fairer.
The question of Twitter is just one of many of the issues outlined by Hayes's lawyers, which are likely to be discussed for years, delaying an execution.
Defensive lawyers also claim, for example, that Hayes may have been harmed when Judge Blue refused to allow them to test the reactions of potential jurors, displaying creepy photographs of the crime scene during jury selection.
Jurors have said they were appalled by photographs of the victims, Jennifer Hawke-Petit, 48, and their daughters, Hayley, 17 and Michaela, 11.
U.S. defense attorneys say they will also question the constitutionality of the death penalty and argue that the judge incorrectly removed a juror who had criticized prosecutors, and had incorrectly pushed for a verdict for his bizarre decision to order the jury to deliberate over the weekend.
Jurors were not kidnapped.
But their arguments about the effect of the intense coverage of the process can attract as much of the interest.
They noted in a recusal of the verdict filed last month that the case dominated the news in Connecticut and was debated during the election campaign by candidates for governor and the U.S. Senate.
They argued that the media continually thrashed the blaze of ecmotion about the case.
"Daily agglomerations of media tweets, cameras and reporters outside the courtroom," the lawyers wrote, "it was something never seen here in New Haven and maybe across the state."
His focus on Tweeter's coverage is controversial.
In some interviews, some reporters said the complaint that the immediate news was more important than reports delivered more slowly were a ludite vision that would make the court's policy on controlling coverage weak in future proceedings.
But some lawyers said appellate courts were often lured because of arguments about adaptation that was required in court due to social and technological changes.
Hayes' lawyers are pushing for that claim.
In his brief, they referred to the Supreme Supreme Court's 1966 decision, which overturns Dr. Sheppard &apos; s conviction because of the coverage of the process, and made it clear that they would demand that the new media would require new rules.
"That case," they wrote, "represents the Sheppard v. Maxwell case of the 21st century."
Can Cicratic build a bridge to unite the divisions?
After a campaign notable for invective and negativity this week unleashed some climinism in Washington.
Is it a harbinger of genuine bipartisan future cooperation or an illusory moment before a rapid return to partisan combat in the capital?
Tuesday's meeting between President Obama and congressional leaders produced no progress on the major issues to be addressed during the outgoing session.
But from what everyone says, it was at least a polite discussion about the differences between the two sides, as well as an acknowledgment of their shared responsibilities to govern, rather than being another empty episode to gain partisan points.
Wednesday's public meeting of the president &apos; s deficit committee was equally civilized, despite the remaining political differences between members.
It seems clear that close interaction over a period of months generated mutual respect that transcends the ideological divide that still separates the members.
That's barely enough to state that a new era of co-operation has arrived.
Both sides are now in a probationary period, probing each other to identify signs of co-cooperation and commitment, while trying to assess the boundaries of public opinion and voter patience for renewed partisan warfare.
Reason why Tuesday &apos; s meeting and subsequent evaluations of it by the participants were made with a minimum of demands or accusations.
The philosophical differences between Obama and the Republicans are too great and are too deeply entrenched to suggest any rapid climate changes that have existed over the past two years.
Either it's about taxes, expenses, health care or social assistance, the two sides are at opposite poles.
Anyway, the November election has softened mood in Washington, at least in the short term.
"There's no doubt that things have changed with regard to the way things are said," said Pete Wehner, a Bush administration official now at the Ethics and Public Policy Center.
"They are inevitable after the elections.
Polls awaken partisan comfy passions, which are then extinct.
That's nice".
On a number of issues, particularly those on the agenda of the out-of-the-separation, Republicans and Democrats have no choice but to find a solution.
Bush's tax cuts expire at the end-of-the-year, and no one's in favor of this.
Both parties want to find a way to preserve some or all reductions, permanently or temporarily.
Likewise, there is likely to be a compromise to expand unemployment benefits, probably because Republicans can't make them believe they are in favor of tax cuts for the wealthy, denieting unemployment benefits for unemployed workers.
After Tuesday's meeting at the White House, there's greater optimism for a possible deal on these issues during the out-of-the-outgoing session, coupled with the ratification of the new START (Strategic Arms Reduction Treaty).
But William Galston, of the Brookings Institution, warns that successful co-examination on the pressing issues of the outgoing session will not necessarily lead to continued bipartisanship when the new 112th Congress meets in January.
On the contrary, he stated that it is very likely and even inevitable that a period of disagreement will begin.
"After the things that need to be carried out, in the short-term psychology will revert the situation to one of confrontation," he said.
Wehner also expressed skepticism (which is widely shared) about the fact that civility can lead to true cooperation.
"I see most of what will happen between now and now and 2012 as a rehearsal for the main event of 2012," he said.
Reasons have plenty to do with the formation of the new Republican majority in the House and Tea Party voters, who contributed decisively to its creation.
Regardless as Republican leaders have about the dangers of exaggerating their position, they know that they must try to fulfill their promises to slash the state's spending and nullify the new health care law.
These partisan lines have already been clearly seen in the initial backlash against the deficit commission's recommendations, which include the reform of the tax code and the increase of some taxes and the retirement age, and the spending cuts.
Co-Chairs Alan Simpson and Erskine Bowles have taken care of one of the toughest jobs in Washington and, according to their own assessment, have forcibly introduced the issue of the deficit on the public agenda in a way that cannot be ignored.
But they need 14 votes on the 18-member committee to publish their plan as an official recommendation, and have been struggling to get close to that figure.
The greatest resistance lies among the elected officials on the panel, suggesting that in fiscal matters there are, at least now, few bases or desires for compromise.
Galston predicts two phases in the battles between White House Republicans and those of Congress.
The first will happen next year in the form of partisan clash, when Republicans defy Obama about the budget, taxes and health care, then followed by a period of increased bipartisan co-operation.
What'd the change bring about?
It's public opinion.
There's plenty of evidence that, despite partisanship of the recent election, a majority of Americans still want to see the two parties working together.
"If the confrontation leads to a stalemate, as happened in 1995, then both parties will carefully observe people's reaction.
Whoever thinks he's losing will probably change course and seek compromise.
However, this can be an optimistic vision.
Partisanship and polarization are currently greater than they were in the mid-1990s, and there seems to be a larger ideological hole between Obama and congressional Republicans between President Bill Clinton and Republicans at the time.
At the same time, Activists of both parties see 2010 as the mere prelude to a more momentous election in 2012.
Given this reality, the civilism shown this week may be welcomed by many Americans.
But this will require a lot more effort from both sides to find points in common on the issues that still divide them.
Learned U.S. document portrays Moscow as the paradise of corruption
Control of the city is in the hands of "kleptocracy," and it leaves some of the bribes and protection money it collects directly to the Kremlin, the U.S. embassy in Russia reported in February.
It was reported that both the police and the Federal Security Service operate huge blackmail systems, which helps explain the high cost of living in Moscow.
They raise money not only of legitimate businesses, but also from organized criminal groups.
Each stratum of the bureaucracy, what Prime Minister Vladimir Putin has called the "vertical authority," takes its share as the money rises on the ladder.
The note, which was shipped to Washington under the name of Ambassador John Beyrle, was published by WikiLeaks on its website on Wednesday.
The note is based on sources whose names have been censored.
It was written while Yuri Luzhkov was still the mayor of Moscow, and blames him for much of the corruption.
President Dmitry Medvedev sacked him in October, but there has been little evidence of cleanliness ever since.
Luzhkov and his wife, Yelena Baturina, who owns a major construction company, have routinely denied allegations of corruption over the years, and he has won several libel lawsuits over such accusations.
In September, when he was asked on a Russian television channel about a documentary that suggested he was corrupt, he said: "It's crazy, a kick, an annoyance."
According to the polls, the Russians overwhelmingly believe that their society is corrupt.
But it's uncommon to find the details explained in detail, since they were in the embassy report, and it's unheard-of that it's unheard-maker for the diplomats of a foreign country to do that.
"The direct links of the Moscow city government with criminality have led some to call it 'dysfunctional', and to affirm that the government functions more like a kleptocracy than as a government," the note states.
"Criminal elements enjoy an 'krysha' (a terminus of the criminal and mafia world that literally means "roof" or protection) passed between the police, the Federal Security Service (SFS), the Ministry of Internal Affairs (MVD), and the prosecutor's office, as well as all sectors of the Moscow city government bureaucracy.
Analysts identify a three-story structure in Moscow's crime world.
"Luzhkov is at the top".
"The SFS, the MVD and the militia are on the second tier".
"Ultimately ordinary criminals and corrupt inspectors are at the lowest level".
"This is an ineffective system in which criminal groups fill a void in some areas because the city doesn't provide some services."
The report says the SFS collects the money from the largest companies, and that the police are targeting small businesses.
A source, the report said, "explained that the commercial owners of Moscow understand that it is best to achieve the protection of the MVD and SFS (rather than organized crime groups) since they not only have more weapons, resources and power than criminal groups, but are also protected by the law."
"That's why protection against criminal gangs isn't more of a priority."
The note notes that, while the collection of money is complete, the protection can be irregular; even those who pay are subject to arrest.
Those who don't pay quickly find their businesses closed by one pretext or another.
Medvedev has spoken several times about the fight against corruption, but has confessed that he has made little progress.
The embassy's memo informs men taking suitcases, probably full of cash, inside the Kremlin itself.
"In his fight against corruption, Medvedev must rely on bureaucrats," Georgy Satarov, director of Indem's Moscow expert group, said in a recent interview.
"But he is a part of this bureaucracy."
"He doesn't belong to a political class, because there's no longer a political class in Russia."
Without politics, without an opponent, without a separation of powers, he said, corruption is inevitable.
Nationwide, Indem estimates that corruption costs Russia more than $300-billion a year.
In a recent survey of global perceptions of corruption carried out by the non-governmental organization Transparency International, the country was ranked 154 (countries are ranked as at least the most corrupt).
The revelation of the U.S. embassy &apos; s note, which didn't happen until late at night Moscow time, will surely cause displeasure within the Russian government, and although Luzhkov can be blamed for Moscow's weaknesses, could be an opportunity for the Kremlin to argue that it is solving the problem.
Interviewed for CNN by Larry King, Putin suggested the WikiLeaks documents could be lying and reacted angrily by a revelation that U.S. diplomats named him Batman and Medvedev Robin.
"The truth of the matter is that this relates to our interaction, which is an important factor in domestic politics in this country," he said.
"But to be honest with you, we don't suspect that this would have been done with such arrogance, with such pressure and, you know, it was done in such an immoral way."
Veterans of the Khmer Rouge forced to face the past
In a distinctly enlightened classroom with smeasured and jacking walls, the director of Anlong Veng High School reminded the man who had built it, the last leader of the Khmer Rouge Ta Mok.
"Here everyone loves Ta Mok".
"He was a good leader and he cared for his people," said Sreng Kor Ma, 42.
Known as "the Butcher" because of his brutality during the Khmer Rouge government, the commander is still popular in this remote former Khmer Rouge fortress in north-west Cambodia, where he built hospitals, bridges and other infrastructure, where thousands of former soldiers of the organization still live.
"But this year, 12 years after the Khmer Rouge surrendered to the government, long-sustained allegiance is finally being defigued by Anlong Veng.
In April, a local forum for truth and reconciliation allowed the victims to publicly confront the people who had participated in the regime.
In June, the government distributed a high-principle textbook here that for the first time teaches the story of the Khmer Rouge to the children of their former soldiers.
And in July, a joint U.N.-Cambodian court issued the first conviction of a former member of the Khmer Rouge, condemning the former boss of the notorious Tuol Sleng torture center Kaing Khek Iev, best known as Duch, to 35 years in prison.
With each of those events, anxiety has grown among Anlong Reg's Khmer Rouge veterans, complicating reconciliation efforts and their attempts to reintegrate into Cambodian society.
"There's resentment and scary among the former Khmer Rouge, but they can't do anything," said Chhang Youk, director of the Cambodia Documentation Center, an independent organization that investigates the regime.
"For them, life under the Khmer Rouge was glorious, but the regime has become a symbol of evil."
"It's generating divisions within families."
During Cambodia's Khmer Rouge government, between 1975 and 1979, about 1.7 million people were executed or died of starvation, illness or overwork.
When in 1979 the Vietnamese invaded and toppled the Pol Pot-led government, the remnants of the regime and its military fled to Cambodia's border with Thailand.
There they launched an insurrection that lasted until the last movement that surrendered in December 1998.
Because Pol Pot, Nuon Chea and Ieng Sary led guerrilla warfare from their bases in the mountains and jungles of western Cambodia, Ta Mok cultivated followers at Anlong Veng.
But in the mid-1990s, after a U.N.-sponsored peace agreement led to the country's first democratic elections in 1993, large groups of Khmer Rouge fighters began defecting to the government, culminating in Ieng Sary's rhydes in 1996, and Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan in 1998.
Pol Pot died of natural causes in 1998, and Ta Mok, who had resisted, was captured by government forces in 1999 in the nearby Dangrek Mountains.
He died in a Phnom Penh prison in 2006 as he awaited the trial.
Without the support of his benefactors, the oldest fighters of the Khmer Rouge fell into absolute misery that is common in rural Cambodia, and many feel nostalgia for the movement.
Although a few elite Khmer Rouge officials retained their local administrative positions in exchange for laying down their weapons, soldiers remain poor and unskilled farmers.
"These people have made little profit after surrender," said Sok Leang of the Center for Justice and Reconciliation, which offers public forums across Cambodia, including in Anlong Veng.
"They've incorporated the utypical agrarian ideology of the regime."
"They were bred without any configuring to do business".
Sister Lim, 55, who teamed the Khmer Rouge when he was a teenager in 1974 wrestled in the jungle near Anlong Veng before dedicating himself to life as a poor rice farmer in 1998.
"Life with the Khmer Rouge was OK," he said.
"Ta Mok floated everyone".
"But now life is difficult because we have to self-supply".
The current Khmer Rouge court has also caused worries here.
Earlier next year, the court is expected to begin prosecuting Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sary and his wife, Ieng Thirith, another former Khmer Rouge minister.
The court &apos; s mandate is to prosecute the main leaders and those of greater responsibility for the crimes, but it has not said whether it will pursue cases in addition to those four.
This doesn't do much to allay fears at Anlong Veng.
Recent media speculation has centered on Im Chaem, 64, who was a district leader in Banteay Meanchey province during the Khmer Rouge government in the late 1970s.
In 2007, she told researchers at the Cambodia Documentation Center that she had overseen the construction of the Trapeang Thma Dam, a project in which thousands of forced workers are believed to have died.
On a recent embarrassing afternoon, Im Chaem returned from working in the fields to his house on wooden piles on a dirt road on the outskirts of Anlong Veng.
When the sun threw long sweeps over the dry grass, Im Chaem scrambling to talk about his past in the Khmer Rouge.
She stated that if the court summoned her, she would not appear.
"Cambodia is peaceful and stable," he said.
"If there's more prosecutions, there'll be war".
Prime Minister Hun Sen, who deserted the Khmer Rouge in 1978, has also repeatedly warned that there will be instability if the court chasses more suspects, although experts and historians question it.
"Cambodia must dig a hole and bury the past," he said.
"But facing the past is exactly what Cambodia needs to do to move forward, said Chhang Youk of the documentation center.
"Reconciliation, according to them, is to rejoin the shattered pieces," he said.
"It's our obligation to gather these broken pieces so we can understand."
The center produced the first government-approved textbook about the Khmer Rouge, called the 75-page "A History of Democratic Kampuchea."
It was distributed in Anlong Veng in June as a supplement to the Ministry of Education's high-studded high-studded textbook, containing less than four pages about the Khmer Rouge.
As with most of Cambodia, the young people of Anlong Veng know few details about the Khmer Rouge, in spite of the city's bond with the regime.
Toque Valeak, 19, a student at Anlong Veng High School, said the new textbook helped students understand an important part of their history.
But his parents reacted skeptically when he took the book home to study.
"My family rejects many parts of the textbook and the court," he said.
"They have suspicion because they're unsure about how many people the court will process."
"Such resistance has made the reconciliation process complex and difficult," Sok Leang said.
But public forums, the textbook and the court are beginning to make an impact, he said.
Anyway, the Khmer Rouge here retain a mighty charm.
In the Dangrek Mountains, a road with too many weeds and covered in discarded plastic bags leads to a rectangle of black soot covered by a rusty lentil roof.
Pol Pot's body was burned here on a pile of tires after his death in 1998.
Nuom Sothea, 31, a cellular saleswoman on the side of the road, said she didn't know very much about the man who was cremated there.
"But he has a lofty spirit and a lot of local residents go there to pray," he said.
It was Nuom Sothea's birthday, and later that day he decided to walk to the site of Pol Pot's last break, where he would leave a bunch of ripe bananas in the hope of having good luck.
Belarus agrees to relinquish its stockpile of weapons-qualified uranium
In a surprise victory for President Obama's campaign to protect nuclear material around the world, the Belarus government announced on Wednesday that it will relinquish its highly-enriched uranium stockpile, a critical component of nuclear weapons.
The deal was unveiled in a joint statement released after Foreign Minister Hillary Rodham Clinton met with Belarusian Foreign Minister Sergei Martynov on the sidelines of a security summit.
For years, the U.S. government had been trying to persuade Belarus to relinquish its highly-enriched Uranium and it looked like they'd reached a point with no return last spring.
But U. U.S. officials told the Washington Post that a pair of classified operations with Belarus over the past two months to remove 84.82 pounds of weapons-qualified uranium from a Belarusian research facility.
This paved the way for Wednesday's agreement to remove the rest of the material, or about 227 kg, according to an official, who spoke on condition of anonymity due to the confidentiality of the matter.
The agreement appeared to be one of the most important outcomes of the 47-nation Nuclear Security Summit chaired by Obama in April that excluded Belarus.
And apparently it was a good achievement to secure the ex-Soviet republic's invitation to the next summit in 2012.
"I was really fascinated by the fact that they wanted a place at the 2012 Nuclear Security Summit."
"That's showing that this process is working," said Ken Luongo, president of the Society for Global Security, a group that promotes nuclear safety.
Clinton said the agreement is "a very significant and important step" taken by Belarus.
In April 2009 Obama vowed in Prague to protect all vulnerable nuclear material around the world over the next four years.
Since then, the U.S. government has helped six countries get rid of all their highly-enriched uranium.
Bielorusia would be the seventh.
About a dozen countries did so under the George W. Bush administration.
At the time of Obama's Nuclear Security Summit, Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko had stated that the nation would never give up its uranium.
"We've retained highly enriched uranium (hundreds of kilograms of what's basically uranium rated for weaponry) and low-enund enrichment uranium," Lukashenko said at the time.
'That's our raw material... we're not gonna make dirty bombs, and we're not going to sell it to anyone.'
"We're using it for research purposes, that's it".
However, Belarus approached the United States a few months ago, indicating interest in reaching an agreement.
Officials said the first task of removing uranium from Belarus in October was particularly tricky, as the material had been slightly irradiated.
Officials said he was loaded onto a special train that traveled more than 2,000 miles to a Russian storage and disposal facility.
According to the officials, the second operation was completed on Monday and was done by plane.
The U.S. government spent $13 million on tasks, and most of the money was used to provide Belarus with the low-enriched uranium research facility, which is unsuitable for nuclear weapons.
Andrew Bieniawski, an official at the Department of Energy who is working on Global Threat Reduction, said "this was co-ordinated and orchestrated with great care to strengthen the trust" that led to Clinton's agreement.
This year, the James Martin Center for Nonproliferation Studies estimated that Belarus possessed at least 40 kilograms of weapons-qualified uranium, as well as hundreds of kilograms of highly enriched uranium.
Belarus will seek to eliminate its reserve by 2012, according to the joint statement.
Relations between Belarus and the United States have been tense for years, mainly due to Lukashenko's rough repression of human rights.
But Wednesday's statement suggested a potential detente.
"While progress on these global security issues is being welcomed, the United States and Belarus recognized that greater respect for democracy and human rights in Belarus remains a central theme for improving bilateral relations and is essential for the progress of the country and its citizens."
The Spanish government made no qualms about  CIA flights
The Spanish government gave no objection to  CIA flights with Taliban and Al -Qaeda prisoners headed to Guantanamo by Spanish territory, the U.S. ambassador to Madrid said in a telegram.
This is reflected in the leaked (cables) documents by Wikileaks to the newspaper El País, which adds that the then first vice-president of the government, María Teresa Fernández de la Vega, requested that the Spanish Executive be informed to "be able to demonstrate that they exercised proper vigilance over the foreign planes passing through Spain."
In June 2006, following a report by the Council of Europe accusing a dozen countries of complicity in practices contrary to human rights, including Spain, a meeting took place between former American Ambassador Eduardo Aguirre and Fernández de la Vega.
The vice-president acknowledged that the report of the Council "had taken the Zapatero government out of the game" and insisted that Spain had nothing to hide in this matter.
"De la Vega stressed that Spain has no objection to intelligence flights through Spanish territory."
"They simply want to be informed" to avoid being "sacrificing stacks," Aguirre says on a cable.
Also the then holder of Foreign Affairs, Miguel Ángel Moratinos, told the ambassador that the Spanish Executive "would to give this matter the lowest profile possible, although, being in the hands of the judges, the government had a limited capacity for influence."
For his part, in January 2007, prosecutor Vicente Gonzalez conveyed to the embassy that he would not try to block the judge's initiative to request the declassification of the documents of the National Intelligence Center (CNI) and the Ministry of Defense on CIA flights.
"These reports don't contain any incriminating elements or sensitive information," the embassy said after speaking with the prosecutor.
However, the U.S. expressed its concern about "apparent coordination between the Spanish judge and the German prosecutors," rather than the declassified information of the CNI or the Ministry of Defense.
"This co-ordination between independent researchers will complicate our efforts to handle the case discreetly between governments," Aguirre laments.
In March 2007 at a lunch between the U.S. ambassador and Interior Minister Alfredo Pérez Rubalcaba, he told him that "although the Spanish judges are rabidly jealous of their independence and will do whatever they deem appropriate," he had instructed his collaborators "to avoid comments of inflamed rhetoric, in an attempt to lower the temperature."
The ambassador also expresses his concern about the PP &apos; s actions in this matter.
"Despite its pro-American general orientation, our experience suggests that the PP will not hesitate to capitalize on any indication that the Zapatero government tolerated or deliberately ignored U.S. actions that could be interpreted as possible violations of human rights, even if these revelations have negative consequences for the U.S. government," he says.
