The people would win 4 seats in Congress and the PSC would lose 9
With the results of 28-N, the PP could be decisive in the municipalities of Lérida, Tarragona and Barcelona
After the significant increase of votes and deputies experienced in the recent autonomous elections, the Catalan PP already has its sights on the general elections because, there is progress or not, the prospects are more than positive, since the number of votes that this formation obtains in these elections usually doubles that it receives in the Catalans.
Thus the popular Catalans obtained 6,10,473 votes in the general elections of 2008, while in the 2006 autonomies the figure amounted to 316,222.
Last Sunday the CFP won 384,091 votes.
From maintaining this upward trend, which has been nourished by a transfer of votes from the PSC and the fidelization of the PP electorate, analysts of this formation have calculated that, if general elections were held, the CFP could go from 8 to 12 deputies in Congress, while CiU also won four (now it has 10) and the PSC would lose no less than 9 deputies (it has 25).
These calculations would verify that the Catalan square is fundamental for PP and PSOE.
According to popular analyses, ERC would lose a deputy - it now has three - while ICV would stay with a deputy or win one.
However, if the electoral calendar is maintained, the most immediate meeting will take place in May 2011 with the municipal elections, where the people can also be decisive in the end of socialist hegemony in the four provincial capitals and in their relay at the head of the Diputation of Barcelona, one of the administrations that manages the most budget - 661 million euros by 2011.
Both nationalists and people agree that the PSC will lose the municipalities of Barcelona, Tarragona, Lérida and Gerona, where the socialists would not obtain a majority or possibility to turn to their traditional partners, ERC and ICV.
Given the Republican debacle, it wouldn't be crazy if this formation loses its representation in the Barcelona Consistory - it is necessary to obtain 5% of the votes.
The PP has increased its number of votes in the four capitals and it could be a decisive force in Barcelona, Lérida and Tarragona.
The nationalists are convinced that they will be able to lead the turnaround in the Tarragona capital, where they ruled with the PP before the current socialist mayor, Josep Félix Ballesteros.
In Lérida, the PP has placed only 500 votes from the PSC, whose mayor Angel Ros maintains very good relations with Mariano Rajoy - during this last campaign, they met on several occasions.
The popular have also grown in other important municipalities such as Badalona, Castelldefels or Reus.
The PP is currently present in 34 municipal governments in Catalonia and aspires to form lists in the maximum number of populations.
the Catalan cell provided thousands of passports to Al Qaeda
In addition to the seven detainees in Barcelona, in Thailand there were three
The seven Pakistanis and Nigerians arrested by agents of the General Police Information Station in Barcelona formed a jihadist cell that, led from Thailand, provided passports to different Al-Qaida groups, including "The Tigers for the Liberation of the Tamil Land" (LTTE) and "Laskar e Taiba" (Let), the latest perpetrator of the Mumbai attacks that killed 106 people in 2008.
The Ministry of the Interior has attached great importance to these investigations, to the point of ensuring that with the " Kampai operation" Al Qaida's forgery apparatus at the international level and, "therefore, its operational capacity."
Investigators estimate that the cell, in the two years that has been active in Catalonia, has been able to send thousands of passports to Thailand, where they were forged and then distributed to groups that make up the Al Qaeda criminal galaxy so that its "fighters" can cross European and other Western borders with total impunity.
The dismantled cell is not the only one that the jihadist network had operation in Europe, hence the investigations have participated, in addition to the Thai services, other European police units, so it is not ruled out that blows similar to that carried out last Tuesday night in Barcelona in the next few days.
In coordination with this operation (Pakistanis Junaid Humayun, Atiqur Rehman, Jabran Asghar, Malik Iftikhar Ahmad, Mohammad Saddique Khan Begum, Tanveer Arshad and the Nigerian Babatunde Agunbiade were arrested in Thailand another with the capture of the Pakistanis Muhammad Athar Butt and Zeeshan Ehsan Butt and the Thai Sirikanlaya Kijbumrung.
These were the ones who ran from Bangkok the cell settled in Spain and in other European countries.
The Catalan network - part of it was beaten in 2009 - mainly stole from tourists who met the requirements of the so-called "World Islamic Front."
That is, the documents had to be in the name of males between the ages of 25 and 45, with a validity of several years and without stamped visas.
It is emphasized in the midst of the investigation that, for the time being, there has not been any information linking those arrested in Catalonia to the Bombay attack, despite the fact that the perpetrator of this attack, "Laskar e Taiba," has been one of the groups receiving the stolen passports in Spain and then falsificated in Thailand.
However, it should be remembered that days before this attack was committed, from Catalonia about a thousand dollars were sent through a transfer agency that were "invested" in the purchase of mobile cards that were then used by the members of the "command" that ended the lives of 106 people.
The police have seized plenty of documentation.
The  PP requires Mas to park the tax concert
The leader of the PPC, Alicia Sánchez-Camacho, yesterday had the opportunity to personally expose to Artur Mas his conditions to support his investiture as president of the Generalitat, identical to those already raised during the campaign and which is summed up in giving priority to the recovery of the economy and dispense with filo-soberarist adventures, such as the economic concert that the nationalist leader wants to propose in the equator of his mandate.
The president of the popular Catalans went to Artur Mas's meeting with the security of 18 deputies in last Sunday's elections, a figure more than enough to be loved, since CiU's candidate needs that at least one of the formations that the opposition will exercise to abstain in the investiture vote.
Future alliances that the nationalists want to maintain in Congress when general elections are being held depends on a possible agreement with the PP when general elections are held, but, for the time being, the conversations Mas maintains these days with the rest of the political leaders are taking place in Catalan key.
With regard to the president of the PP, to support an investiture "you will have to park all the sovereignty and pro-independent speeches," as well as the economic concert - similar to that of the Basque Country - that the convergent leader tries to even submit to a referendum.
Both leaders do have in common their commitment to thin the political apparatus of a very inflated autonomous administration and the elimination of the inheritance and donation tax.
The details of the negotiation on the investiture of Mas will be addressed by the PPC, Jordi Cornet and Dolors Montserrat.
But he has already met with Joaquim Nadal (PSC) - who promised a tough opposition - Joan Herrera (ICV) and today he'll do it with Joan Puigcercós (ERC), Albert Rivera (Ciutadans) and Joan Laporta (Solidaritat Catalana).
The geriatrician claims the deaths were not due to "lack of measures"
The owners of the Olot center don't rule out that their warden killed more than eleven elders
La Caritat de Olot (Gerona's manager) yesterday offered a press conference to highlight the damage caused to the institution the criminal behavior of Joan Vila Dilmé, the nurse who while he was there, killed at least eleven elderly people in his care.
The director of the residence, Joan Sala, appeared accompanied by the lawyer of the center, the lawyer Joan Cañada, and announced that "La Caritat's desire would be able to carry out the accusation against the person responsible" of the deaths but "the legality and the responsibility force us to assume the procedural situation of civil officials."
And in addition to the social discredit that the entity may suffer because it has not detected the criminal activities of the celador in time, the great threat to La Caritat now takes the form of substantial compensation that the legal representatives of the relatives of the families of the victims are already calculating.
Joan Vila Dilmé has confessed that the first crime dates back to August 2009 and has since not stopped killing.
The judge has already pointed out in the order ordering the exhumation of eight bodies - former geriatric patients - who suspected that in many cases no doctor personally scouts the deceased but, according to the age and pathologies of the patients, the procedure was dispatched by telephone.
La Caritat, through its director, categorically denied this point yesterday and pointed out that "the lack of measures cannot at any time have been the cause of what has happened."
La Caritat &apos; s attorney added that "there is not a single hint of reckless action on the part of anyone other than the defendant himself."
Already baptized as "Angel of the Death" he confessed the day before yesterday 11 murders and detailed that the first crime was committed on August 29, 2009 until, a month and a half later, to kill a second grandmother.
In this case he caused the death by puncturing an overdose of insulin, as opposed to the first crime he committed by helping with a mixture of sedative pills.
La Caritat's director points out that it's hard to control that the center's personnel have access to the medications.
Insulin punctures and sedative cocktail would have been the mechanism used in six of the nine crimes that the nurse, in his eagerness to alleviate the suffering of the inmates, used to kill them.
But it will be the analyses that are being carried out in the lab that will have to clarify whether Vila does not lie and sought for his first victims a less cruel death or if, on the contrary, he scorched them inside giving them bleach as he has already confessed that he did with the three women he killed between 12 and 17 October.
In La Caritat they don't give credence to the warden's demonstrations, not even the number of confessed murders, which they don't rule out being superior, and they justify their attitude in the different versions that Vila has given in his visits to the court and in the little sense that he has changed his way of proceeding after having successfully committed eight murders.
That is why they say that they are made available to justice to clarify the scope of this event.
The ECB threatens
Investors reacted abruptly yesterday to Trichet's threat yesterday warning "Markets shouldn't underestimate our ability to react."
Peripheral spreads have narrowly closed and shares of European banks have risen by more than 5 per cent.
However, we must not underestimate the ECB's ability to err and the euro has barely rebounded which is a troubling sign.
What has happened yesterday is typical of an illiquid market that as it plumes recovers but motivated by closing short and very speculative positions.
The institutional investor will soon lose his fear.
A month ago the ECB summoned us today to withdraw liquidity measures and it will have to be retracted tomorrow for the fourth time in two years and those that remain.
The Bundesbank demanded to complete the public debt-buying plan as ineffective and to eliminate all extraordinary forward liquidity measures and leaving only unlimited access per week.
In the aftermath of the Irish crisis, we expect them to extend the current liquidity measures "at least until March" and maintain the quarterly auction with unlimited access at the end of the quarter to ensure liquidity at least until at least June.
We also hope that they will continue their bond-buying program and will even give the signal of intensifying it.
If they don't meet the expectations generated they will reverse the excellent reaction of the markets yesterday and if they remain obsessed in removing liquidity measures, may God assist us.
Just as at their May meeting in which they stated that they did not either contemplate the purchase of bonds and collapsed the markets, history could be repeated tomorrow.
The ECB must be credible and persistent.
In May it announced bazuca on Monday and on Wednesday it said it had already bought a lot, triggering a further collapse of the market.
Yesterday the one-year-old letter auction in Portugal has been very poor.
The treasury only issued $500 million to 5.28%, versus $750 million to 4.81% of the previous auction.
Even so the rates in Portugal fell 30 basis points throughout the curve, clearly mirroring a change of market sentiment behind Trichet's words.
Allied and Bank of Ireland hole figures have been made public in Ireland.
The estimated figures are about $10 billion in the short term and an additional 15,000 potential and have until February to obtain private capital or will be nationalized.
Although Ireland does not have any credibility after failing to detect the problem in July with the stress tests, the figures are assumeable within the Help Plan and help reduce uncertainty.
However, next Tuesday, the Government will have to obtain parliamentary approval for its new budget and it does not yet have sufficient support, so Ireland remains a source of uncertainty.
Angela Merkel has moderated the tone of her speech to relax the tension ahead of the upcoming Summit of Heads of State and Government on December 14, in which there is a need for a political commitment to demonstrate that we are a union and that we all play the same score.
Trouble is that Germany wants to play Wagner and the rest the anthem of Beethoven's Joy.
In Spain, the president had the gift of the opportunity when he presented yesterday the plan of measures to be adopted on Friday by the Council of Ministers.
Pension reform, transparency in autonomous communities and financial institutions, deficit reduction, liberalization, privatization, measures to re-activate employment ring in the ears of investors such as Vivaldi's four stations.
Double Effect Trichet and ZP Explain that the Spanish Stock Exchange has risen by 4.5%, twice as much as the rest of the European Stock Exchange.
Unlike in February, the reaction of the Government and the Bank of Spain has been swift and brilliant and we are benefiting from the surprise effect.
Today there's a key auction of a three-year bond that will benefit from the Trichet effect so we hope it will be resolved with strong demand and help dissolve doubts about our refinancing capacity.
For Spain to ask for an Aid Plan would be self-immolating.
They would give us a liquidity line that we don't need, the Treasury has more than $40 billion in liquidity available three times as much as in March, to very expensive rates and closes access to the non-day markets.
The strategy, however, must remain Caesar &apos; s wives, "in addition, it seems so."
Cheer up, we can.
The president of Eulen will refute Vega Sicilia's board requested by his children
The storm doesn't abate the open conflict in the Alvarez family, owners of Eulen and El Enebro.
David Alvarez, founder of the group, will challenge, considering it "full-fledged," the judicial board of El Enebro on the 16th, requested by five of his seven children and in which they will try to assert its majority and take over the management bodies of the firm head of Bodegas Vega Sicilia.
David Alvarez and his children Jesús David and María José are tuning the legal battery for the new judicial chapter in their litigation for control of the part of the group.
The founder of the company's largest business services holding company doesn't appear willing to give in to its litigation to be present at the governing bodies of El Enebro.
From his board of directors he was knocked out by five of his seven sons who were appointed administrators at the shareholders' meeting of January 18 of this year and whose challenge is recorded in the Commercial Registry.
The judicial board of El Enebro for the 16th, convened by Madrid's Soldier's Soldier No. 2, has again opened the thorn box within the Alvarez family.
The appeal to the judge to establish the assembly is, until the conflict is resolved, the only legal mechanism that guarantees the convening of the board.
The last movement of Juan Carlos, Pablo, Emilio, Marta and Elvira Álvarez Mezquiriz, is described by David Álvarez as a "desperate resource of the applicants their five sons in order, through a procedural trickery, in a clear fraud of the Law and abuse of rights, to try to convalidate and heal the radically nil meeting on January 18," according to its official spokespersons.
Such an argument shall be the basis that, once the board has been held, it will proceed with its challenge on the grounds that it is "full-full nullity."
The same protagonists, who maintain a discreet silence, already unsuccessfully tried for the magistrate to convene a meeting in June.
In El Enebro, a heritage company and owner of Bodegas Vega Sicilia, among other assets, the seven children of David Alvarez control 100 per cent of the capital.
The founder of the services group, however, claims the usufruct on 51% of the capital, according to family protocols.
This matter is the substantive question to be decided by the judge once the hearing is held on the request for a challenge, for which there is still no prescribed date.
Red Bull triumph in F1 with secret Siemens weapon
Software designs real-time automotive improvements.
Ever since Red Bull burst into the world of Formula 1 in 2004, its history in this select velocity universe has been marked by surprise and success.
At the very beginning no one took into account the options of a newly-accommodating team and, despite the upward trajectory of the last seasons, last November, in the final straight of the championship all bet on the triumph of Fernando Alonso Ferrari.
Undeserved the skill as driver of the eventual champion, the German Sebastian Vettel, nor from the capacity of the Renault engine that propels his car, in the world of Formula 1 the "secret weapon" that the multinational Siemens has placed in the hands of Red Bull to turn him into a winning team.
It is in fact a powerful process simulation software that the German placed on the market in 2007 very far away from high automotive racing.
Denominated PLM, this powerful computer tool has been used to design assembly strings of manufacturing factories around the world, power plants, high-precision instruments.
The objective is to reproducing, faithfully simulate the product or the process before building it in a real way, thus avoiding preventable flasks and expenses.
PLM has facilitated the development of Canon's photographic cameras, Sukhoi jets or Nissan &apos; stricest utility cars.
Siegfried Russwurm, in charge of Siemens's industrial division, explains that the use of this computer instrument in its Formula 1 factory has allowed Red Bull to design and implement "in a few hours, almost in real time," the modifications, changes and improvements that are detected during racing and testing.
Mr Russwurm explains that the teams in 1992 had an average of 10 techni technicians in his team who were able to introduce a maximum of three or four vehicle improvements during each season.
The Red Bull team now employs 180 young high-skilled professionals who, equipped with powerful computer equipment, analyse and project all sorts of changes in the fireballs.
Siemens' manager explained that the PLM software is able to analyze and improve all the elements of Red Bull's fireballs with the exception of the engine, which is of Renault's sole responsibility.
The materials, fuel, aerodynamics and safety elements are modified.
The changes can be taken from the simulator's virtual reality to reality in less less than 24 hours, thanks to advanced laser production systems and high-tech autoclaves.
As the sole limitation of this technological improvement craze is the $40 million that each Formula 1 team can spend maximum on each season to facilitate competition.
Red Bull has assembled the high-tech factory to design and produce its fireballs in a town about 50 kilometers from London.
These are two buildings in which visits are restricted, especially for journalists.
Everything that's happening there is considered "high secret."
There are 580 people, including its two riders, Champion Vettel and Mark Webber.
The hero of Red Bull's Formula One, no doubt, is recent champion Sebastian Vetell.
Among the immense technological resources that the energy beverage brand has placed at the service of its participation in the circuits, other protagonists are hiding whose contest is priceless and their null and void.
The factory's head of technology, Steve Nevey, claims that five years ago it could be said that R&D advances in cart cars contributed 50 per cent of victories and riders were responsible for the rest.
Today these percentages have varied and the technology adds up to 85 per cent of success, while pilots contribute 15 per cent of the added value.
Nevey acknowledges that the Vettels or Webbers will never be replaced by computing because they "are unpredictable gladiators."
HP will install in two years 20 own stores with local distributors
Hewlett-Packard (HP) inaugurated yesterday in Barcelona its first exclusive store in collaboration are its partner Pont Reyes.
This will be the first in the network of 20 stores of between 80 and 100 square meters that the multinational plans to install in Spain for the next two years.
The company will reconcile this initiative with the 16 areas already present in multi-brand centers such as PC City, Carrefour, Saturn and El Corte Inglés.
The vice-president of HP España, Helena Herrero, gave as an example the remodelled store located in the Gran Vía Barcelonaa, owned by Pont Reyes, with whom she has collaborated for 30 years.
The U.S. group has prepared a plan to locate 200 brand sales points in Europe in 2013, a strategy that includes the 20 Spanish establishments.
The first to open were Bucharest (Rumanía) and Belgrade (Serbia) last week, with Barcelona being the third.
Herrero did not want to unravel the following possible openings, although he acknowledged that Madrid could be the next destination.
The director of HP personal systems also assured that the philosophy of installing the stores will always be the same, relying on a local distributor with experience and knowledge of the sector.
The U.S. group advises and is in charge of the exclusive furniture design of the stores (with products that can be played interactively), which in Europe will have an identical image.
In addition, HP covers the training of establishment staff.
Helena Herrero denied that it was a franchise system and stressed that the exclusive network of shops aims to "improve the service to customers and SMEs in the purchase, care, after sale and even packaging."
The Barcelona store includes a space with a world-picked pilot program that is responsible for solving the possible problems of HP computer equipment under guarantee.
An online service connects with a technical center located in Maastricht (Netherlands).
"So in 10 minutes we can solve problems that can sometimes take up to two days."
"This system is specially designed for SMEs and users, since large companies already have contracted technical support services," Helena Herrero said yesterday.
Sabadell bets on 'stealing' a million customers from its rivals in three years
More optimistic than other directors, the CEO of Banco Sabadell, Jaime Guardiola, believes that 2011 will be a tough year for the banks, but initially rules out a new liquidity crisis and predicts the end of the liability war.
In spite of this, it foresees a collapse of interest margins, which will seek to compensate in part with increases in commissions and the price of credit.
Sabadell is hoping to attract in three years a million private clients, its pending subject.
Sabadell has found a way to explode over the next three years and thus be able to survive in a very complicated economic environment.
The bank will launch in 2011 a new master plan that will be valid for three years.
The objective, in addition to surviving without the need to resort to FROB's public aid, is to gain market share with the recruitment of one million new private customers - 700,000 if those who can leave the bench are discounted - its great outstanding subject, since so far it had pampered SMEs and companies more than the retail customer.
This year it has attracted 250,000 private clients and 50,000 companies.
Its market share in individuals is 3.5%, while in companies it exceeds 10 per cent.
Another of the foundations of the 2011-2013 plan is the bet on productivity, or what's the same, of austerity, with a cost reduction as an objective, and whose challenges the bank will be exposed when it presents the results of the financial year.
Few banks now dare to set targets and make them public, as the financial crisis has disrupted all the plans of global banking.
Sabadell, in fact, will close this year its Optima master plan, fixed four years ago, although it was initially for three years, but the market situation meant it will extend it one more exercise.
Customers who let the savings banks slide will be their main target.
At a breakfast organized by APIE, Guardiola insisted that 2011 is going to be a "difficult and momentous year."
The executive, in fact, believes that leaving the crisis in Spain "would be slow."
In addition, in two or three years it will be "very difficult to create jobs," something fundamental to the survival of the banking business, he insisted.
And, while he believes that the liability war - which penalizes margins - is shrinking, the price of the risk premiums that banks and banks are paying for their issues to obtain liquidity are already shifting to the price of credit to compensate for the fall in margins.
"The issue of liquidity and financing is structural, and it's going to be a pressure factor on credit."
"We haven't skyrocketed, but the differentials are higher," Guardiola acknowledged.
Banesto on Thursday ordered his network to limit long-term credit and raise the price of differentials - see CincoDays on November 22 and 27.
He also acknowledged that the banking trend now "is to raise commissions," but "there is a competitive framework."
Sabadell &apos; s management plan is based on organic growth, although, as usual, the group does not rule out corporate operations.
Not in vain, he assures that in Spain "greater sizes will be required."
"Everything will be moved next year," he said.
He states that the capital needs are "up greatly" and does not rule out any Spanish financial institution going into red numbers.
And he points out that the FROB can supply the market in raising funds against new recapitalizations.
Assuring that Sabadell will be able to survive the temporary closure of the markets, he downplayed what some bankers are already tipping as a new liquidity crisis.
He points out that it cannot be issued, but contrary to what happened in June, short-term funding can be obtained through repos, which dampens the crisis.
The ECSC rules out a second wave of mergers in the boxes
The Director General of the ECSC, José Antonio Olavarrieta, yesterday ruled out that there will be a wave of mergers of savings banks and again insisted on the speed with which the restructuring of the sector is being carried out.
At the beginning of the process of concentration of the Spanish savings banks, there were several entities that assured that they would not participate in this first phase and that they would be reserved for the second round.
However, the Director General of the Spanish Confederation of Savings Banks (CECA), José Antonio Olavarrieta, ruled out yesterday that this new wave of operations will take place.
"I don't think the biggest part of the mergers has been made, the incorporation of Cajasol into the Civic Banking group you can't say that it's a second round, what's there's are."
"Most of it is completely done; instead of 17 we will go to 13 or 14, but all the great processes have already been done and protected by the Bank of Spain, which in that has collaborated a lot also," said the manager.
In statements during a breakfast of the Association of Directors and Executives of Aragon (ADEA), Olavarrieta has stated that the process of concentration is being undertaken "at the fastest speed possible, and we have to be very satisfied with how it is happening in the deadlines that in its day, not now, were marked in the business plans with the Bank of Spain," he emphasized.
"Moving from 45 boxes with an average of 28.5 billion active volumes to 17 with 75.5 billion is a process that is unparalleled in history, reducing the number of entities by 62% so that they are much larger to comply with Basel," he stressed.
Olavarrieta acknowledged that "there is a rather unlucky series of opinions about the slowness of the process" that demonstrate "a lack of seriousness and meaning," because "what the Bank of Spain does is to remember that we have to be in the deadline before the end of the year."
The Director General of the ECSC, José Antonio Olavarrieta, said yesterday that Spain has the most rigorous financial system in Europe and "probably" in the world, and attributed to those who call it a "disquit of knowledge."
The manager pointed out that, within the current economic situation, "we can be very calm" with the situation of the financial institutions and the savings banks, a system that has embarked on a "very important" concentration process and that "goes as quickly as possible."
He also recalled that the Spanish bench came out very well stopped from the stress tests carried out by the ECB in July.
Shoemaker and the German Red Line
Berlin has chosen to remain in the euro, but has established the conditions for the entire Union.
Tensions in the markets have provoked a dual government response.
On the one hand, José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero has advanced new adjustment measures, ranging from tax reductions to SMEs, the regulation of private placement agencies or the abolition of the 426 euros of aid to the unemployed without benefits, to the privatization of 49% of AENA, the management of the airports of Barajas and El Prat and 30% of State Lotteries.
On the other hand, Vice-President Elena Salgado, with the support of former President Felipe Gonzalez, has joined the criticism of Chancellor Angela Merkel for her responsibility to unleash the current crisis.
How do you have to interpret those moves?
It's ahead that Merkel's position isn't a mistake in his communicative policy.
On the contrary, it's another twist-twidth in the difficult process of re-dilution of the euro.
And here Germany has a radically new vision of what its future should be, which clash with that of maintaining the ancient traditions of a large part of its partners.
Faced with European resistors, in Germany there is beginning to speculate on the three possible exits to the crisis.
The first is a strong euro, made up of itself, Austria and the Netherlands, among other countries, while the south would leave the monetary zone.
The second is the recovery of the framework, even if it now resembles science fiction, with a very favorable stream of opinion, about 50% of the population.
The third, a redesign of the euro, in line with German proposals of greater macroeconomic control, budgetary stiffness, fines to transgressor countries, possible suspensions of payments of some of them and extension to the private sector of the costs of potential adjustments.
Germany's position is clear and it's been defining for a long time, so there's no excuses now.
First, in 2009, when both the IMF and the European Commission stimulated the deficits, the German government pulled a constitutional reform from its sleeve to prevent them starting in 2016.
This was the first sign of diverging between what Germany believed should be made (fiscal stiff-protective plans and stability in public finances), and what its European partners, including France, were practicing.
The second at the forehead was given by the Central European country when Merkel painfully elongated Greece's bailout decision.
Already then it was the subject of fierce criticism, but the truth is that the political consensus in Germany and public opinion and that published were very much against Greece's bailout.
Finally, in the last days of October, Merkel re-embarrassed with a clear position, demanding the reform of the Union's treaties, in order to make it possible to rescue a country and, at the same time, to force private lenders to run at some of the cost.
This position has been interpreted as anti-European as it has precipitated the Irish crisis and everything else.
But his proposal, although somewhat sweetened, has been approved and, in the coming months, we should assist in the process of reform of the Union that will enable those measures since 2013.
Germany has drawn a red line it doesn't intend to cross.
And you are telling us all that, even if we now have to take measures with which you may not agree very much, from that date the old operation of the euro will have been completed.
2013 will mean the birth of a new monetary area, in which infringing countries will be severely sanctioned, they can be forced to declare themselves suspended and, in that case, the private sector will also bear some of the cost.
Germany is pointing out to us that we must follow a severe growth path with high rates of savings and investment, moderation in consumption, improvements in productivity and competitiveness and positive external balances.
Another alternative based on debt and construction, with enormous holes in the balance of payments, such as the one that has presided over Spanish growth in the years of the bosom, they simply will not tolerate it.
Envite is clear.
And we'd do it wrong not to understand.
It isn't just a question of redirecting the government deficit to 3 per cent in 2013.
What they're demanding is much tougher.
And the worst thing in these circumstances we could do is deceive ourselves.
I hope the current turbulence will give way to greater reassurance.
And that we take advantage of the window of opportunity they have given us, until the spring of 2013.
It seems that President Zapatero has also understood this and his double manifesto (the reforms promised after last Saturday's meeting with the big companies and yesterday's announcement of new measures) suggests that, despite the criticisms of Merkel, the the thesis has been paved the way for putting order at home.
But the red line that Germany has charted there is, and it doesn't look like they'll move it.
And among the possibilities that are contemplated, to leave a monetary zone too crawling from unserious countries, it cannot be excluded.
Then Germany will always be able to tell us that it had already alerted us.
Sport and Fraud of Law
Trees such as Alonso and Ramos before the Ajax demonstrate wits but no willingness to escape a sanction.
Expulsion allegedly concoctuated by two Real Madrid players (Xabi Alonso and Sergio Ramos) in the November 23 match against Ajax in the Champions League has caused rivers of ink to be written about whether or not such behavior is unsportional and whether, eventually, both players had to be sanctioned by UEFA.
As it is well known, within a few minutes of finishing the game (that their team already clearly had won), the two players deliberately lost time when it came to getting foolish fouls to cause the referee to show them the second yellow card.
In this way, and since Real Madrid will already finish as the leader of their group, both players will serve the regulatory sanction the next match of the liguilla, against the Auxerre, and they will be able to pass clean of cards to the next phase.
Ultimately, UEFA's disciplinary committee has sanctioned the team's coach, José Mourinho with two matches, and the players involved.
The provocation of a yellow card in circumstances such as this or similar is not a new practice, but rather frequent in the world of football.
It's so widespread that it's part of the ethos, the unwritten rules, but followed, of this sport.
Part of the debate that arose in the related case is because it has been starred by a team of the entity of Real Madrid (which in its anthem boasts of nobility and honor, and which in recent times has fined its players for analogous acts) and the orchestrated dramatization, apparently, by Mourinho, who adds one more episode to its long history of histrionic technician and provocateur.
In any case, the debate revolves around whether this type of behaviour not expressly prohibited by the regulation contradicts the principles of the sport, i.e. if these actions commit fraud of the law; since, in fact, they are actions respectful of the sports regulations, but they seem to evade their meaning.
The issue involves examining the kind of damage they cause to the sporting spirit.
One of the assets that understands the sporting spirit is the physical integrity of the opposing players, an aspect that with these actions it was not endangered, i.e. there was no violent gambling.
Another of these goods referred to with fair play is to prevent the team of the offending players from taking unfair advantage of the opposing team, which did not happen either.
However, the paradox of the action of the players of Real Madrid is that it did not conform to what we normally characterize as cheater or fraud of the law, because their violation of the rules was not done hidden to try to avoid the sanction, but precisely his action tried to be the most evident in order to receive the punishment of the referee.
They waited and gladly accepted the sanction.
Another question is what kind of advantage offenders get in the wilful misdemeanours examined.
Here it seems necessary to note that it does not put the opponent at a disadvantage or benefit in the same match, since obviously the result of the action means leaving the team itself with fewer players.
And it's also not a useful strategy for the next game, because the club won't be able to count on those players.
The advantage, of course, is that the players will kick-start the next phase of the Champions League without cards that could threaten their participation in a future game.
But it is a long-term advantage, with a certain degree of indeterminacy, since the team can be eliminated at the beginning of change, and in addition with this performance the players fulfill a penalty game and move on to the second cycle of cards, in which the suspension for the accumulation of cards occurs with one less than in the first cycle.
So, if you go through rounds, it's not so clear that it's an advantage.
Ultimately, these actions do not go against the spirit of sport, of which it is a part to make strategic decisions that favor to make the most of what the rules allow.
On the assumption at hand, the players did not act to evade the application of the sanction (that would be a clear case of law fraud), but rather chose when to submit to it.
They made a strategic decision without skimming the punishment or escaping it.
Although the border between ingenuity and sterling is diffuse, on this occasion I think it was a clear case of ingenuity.
Controversy, it seems to me, arises by two factors: because they were two self-expulsions and the way in which the transmission of the guideline was carried out to provoke them.
The damage caused to the principles of the sport by these actions isn't as ethical as aesthetic.
Offensive of offers to capture Christmas group dinners in BCN
The dictatorship of the calendar this year concentrates an avalanche of reservations for the 16th and 17th.
Self-suffering celebrations by the employees, who want to spend between 20 and 30 euros, will proliferate.
As the nougat and the lottery, with the arrival of December the group dinners pre-Christmas also return the group dinners, which continue to act as an oxygen balloon for the restoration and leisure of Barcelona, but that because of the crisis they have led to an offensive of adjusted prices to be able to capture the majority customer: almost everyone wants to spend between 20 and 30 euros on dinners or meals, the entrepreneurs and associations of the sector interviewed agree.
