To its six group menus they have added one Christmas, with 30 euros or more as a star budget and the perception that there is a predisposition to spending 10% more than last year in exchange for demanding more details, such as cava.
They also see green shoots in some companies that again invite their employees and feel that the volume of the groups is somewhat higher.
On the part of the bosses Fecasarm (which includes entities such as the Association of Bars, Restaurants and Leisure of Barcelona), its vice president, Enric Gomo, hopes to begin to see the end of the tunnel and maintains that together the reserves are "encouraged," although customers "negotiate to the maximum."
Yes, the illusion of the celebrations has not descended, pay whoever pays, he says.
The Pakistani nuclear program generates great concern in the United States
Islamabad produces atomic weapons at "a faster rhythm than any other country."
Wikileaks leaks confirm the links between the Taliban and Pakistan.
The content of the correspondence between the U.S. embassy in Pakistan and the U.S. State Department leaked by the Wikileaks portal confirms the enormous concerns caused in Washington by the Pakistani nuclear program.
Apart from the enlightening and juicy details they offer about the content of the talks and the thoughts of U.S. diplomatic representatives accredited in Islamabad, the documents reveal that cooperation between the two countries on atomic security, rather than increasing, "decreases," and that the volatile Asian country produces nuclear weapons "at a faster pace than any other country in the world."
The peculiarity of the Pakistani nuclear program - that the weapons are not assembled but remains stored by components to make it difficult to destroy in the event of an attack - constitutes one of the main reasons for unveiling in Washington.
This is enumerated by the Ambassador to Pakistan, Anne W. Patterson, in a report prior to the visit of the then national security adviser, James Jones: "The proximity of some nuclear facilities to territory under Taliban attack, the rumored dispersion of nuclear materials and the vulnerability of the transport of atomic materials."
The high number of people involved in nuclear arsenals and missile programmes is seen not only by the U.S., but also by the United States, as proliferating and reason to keep their guard.
"Pakistan has had to recruit personnel with strict religious beliefs to protect nuclear facilities; given the fall in educational and cultural levels, extremist organizations have more opportunities to recruit people who work on nuclear or missile programs," Russian Foreign Ministry expert Yuri Korolev said at a meeting with their U.S. counterparts.
The newest documentation leaked by Julian Assange's portal is the dwindling cooperation between the U.S.-Pakistan on issues of nuclear proliferation and Pakistan's high rate of nuclear weapons production.
An embassy office in May qualifiers as "frustrating" communication with Pakistan argues: "Transparency is often non-existent; offers of help go unresponsive or are discarded."
In another message, Peter Lavoy, National Intelligence official for South Asia, notes that "despite the economic catastrophe, Pakistan produces nuclear weapons at a faster rate than any other country in the world."
Ambassador Patterson also asks Washington, during a visit by the head of the Army, General Ashfaq Kayani, to make it "very clear" to the person responsible that "the Pakistani Army must end tacit support for terrorist groups."
On the hunt for the Wikileaks boss
Obama appoints top counter-terrorism leader to avoid further leaks.
Interpol issues a red alert in order to follow the movements and locate Assange wherever he is.
Everything related to Wikileaks and its founder, Julian Assange, forms a complicated labyrinth where it's hard to have certainty about the facts.
Yesterday Interpol issued a "red warning" to "lopocate and follow Assange's movements."
However, this isn't a traditional arrest warrant.
In addition, it is not new, but dates back to November 20.
And it has nothing to do with the latest leak of more than a quarter of a million U.S. diplomatic documents but the allegations of sexual abuse that the Australian faces in Sweden.
In addition, Wikileaks creator lawyer Mark Stephens says that "the police know where and how to contact Mr Assange."
It was on the 20th that the international police force headquartered in Lón (France) issued the order at the request of Sweden, but it did not reveal it until yesterday, after receiving authorization from that country's authorities.
Thus, the 188 Interpol countries have indications of identifying or locating Assange "with a view to his pre-trial arrest and extradition."
Although Interpol is unable to order the arrest of an individual affected by a red alert, many of the Member States, as explained by the organization, do consider the so-called free-to-reset network for an arrest - which must be undertaken by the national police force - especially if they have a bilateral extradition agreement, such as in Sweden and England, where Assange is believed to be.
The move has outraged Assange's lawyers, who yesterday published a letter de lining the 39-year-year-old Wikileaks founder has been willing to respond by videoconference to questions from the Swedish authorities.
They also recall that Assange applied and obtained permission to leave Sweden the prosecutor of his case, which they compare to the mythical Lavrentiy Pavlovich Beria, Stalin's head of security.
"That sounds like a manhunt, not a legal persecution," the missive says.
As long as the world searches for him - and from Australia his mother defends his son's innocence - Assange remains unaccounted for, but not silent.
On Monday he gave from an undetermined location an interview with the director of Time magazine via Skype, a conversation in which he called for the resignation of U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton.
"If it can be shown he was responsible for ordering U.S. diplomats to spy at the U.N. in violation of international treaties that the U.S. has signed, he should resign," he said.
His opinion was despised yesterday by White House Press Secretary Robert Gibbs, who described the request for resignation as "ridiculous and absurd."
"I'mn't entirely sure why we should care about a guy's opinion with a website," he told CNN.
Obama himself has tasked a high-pronged counter-terrorism leader, Rusell Travers, to prevent further leaks of documents.
President Barack Obama &apos; s spokesman Barack Obama &apos; strengthen's attempt to downplay Assange contrasts with viewers these days proclaimed by conservative politicians and commentators.
Peter King, the congresswoman who from January will chair the National Security Committee in the lower house, has asked the State Department to consider including Wikileaks on the list of terrorist organizations "to intervene and their funds and pursue any contribution or help."
Further afield Tom Flanagan, an adviser to Canadian President Stephen Harper, who has had to recuse himself after saying Assange should "were assassinated."
"Obama should make it a target and use an unmanned aircraft or something".
Who didn't apologize was Mike Huckabee, a Baptist, former Arkansas governor and a possible presidential nominee.
"Whoever in the government leaked the information is guilty of treason and anything other than the execution would be too nice punishment," he said on Fox.
Puigcercós is looking for peace in ERC with winks to those related to Carod
The Republican front-runner will allow the party a secret vote on its continuity.
Huguet proposes a more pluralistic executive and the end of the assembly system.
Joan Puigcercós seems to have found the recipe to overcome the umpteenth internal revolt in the ranks of ERC, without north by the loss of 11 deputies and half of the votes.
The president of the party is aware that his regressiveness is insufficient for those related to former leader Josep Lluís Carod-Rovira, but he also knows that the influence of this sector is now as diluted as his own mentor and that at all it can cause him the headaches he had with other extinct currents.
For this reason, Esquerra's leader will try to beat the new critics with winks that will allow him to arm himself until the municipal elections.
To begin with, Puigcercós announced yesterday that the more than 200 ERC national councillors will be able to pronounce on their continuity in secret.
Using ballots, and not by hand raised.
