It keeps China aiming to reduce greenhouse gases
The representative of that country at COP16 stated that his country has made enormous efforts to reduce the carbon footprint and detailed that it maintains its offer to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions by between 40 and 45 per cent by 2020.
China maintains its offer to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions between 40 and 45 per cent by 2020, said Su Wei, that country's representative at COP16.
In his first public appearance at the Sixteenth Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (COP16), Su Wei said that his country has made enormous efforts to reduce the carbon footprint and to rebuke its industry.
In fact, he explained, over the past four years his country had invested $245 million in efforts to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
However, he made it clear that China will not change its objectives, but will continue to work jointly with the international community in this area.
He stated that China's climate change strategy is divided into four items: dialogue; capacity-building; institution-building and support for pilot programmes.
There has been progress in all these areas, including the adoption last year of a new Energy Law, which reflects the objectives set for reducing gas emissions, he explained.
On the other hand, he stressed that in China, the federal authorities have been working in a coordinated manner with the state and local authorities to achieve the objectives of energy savings, efficient energy use and emission reduction.
In addition, several multi-multi-year plans are under way or have already been finalized, both financed by the Chinese government and by governments of other countries.
For example, he referred to the Efficient Energy Usage Programme, which was implemented over the past five years and received investments from the United Nations (UN), government and private initiative.
A provincial climate change project is also underway, another for the exchange of incandescent hotspots for savers and one more mitigation and adaptation, he said.
At the same table, Martin Krol, a scientific adviser to that country's government on climate change, stated that China's efforts have been greater than is generally thought.
For example, he explained, the per capita emission of greenhouse gases in China is six tons per person, while in the U.S. it is 100 tons; China is responsible for 9 per cent of the historical contribution to pollution.
It has also made unprecedented efforts in the use of energy, such as increasing energy efficiency by 20 per per cent over the past five years and plans to do the same by 2020, he abounded.
Attached to Espino's legality expulsion: César Nava
The national leader of the PAN explained that the former Panista leader is in his own right to go to the National Order Commission to demand what suits his right.
PAN national leader César Nava Vázquez stated that the expulsion of Manuel Espino Barrientos was adhering to legality and that the former Panista leader is in his right to go to the National Order Commission to demand what suits his right.
In an interview prior to the protest of Gabino Cué Monteagudo as governor of the state, he said that in any case the expulsion of Espino is a decision taken at the local level.
"This is an autonomous decision of the Sound Order Commission."
"Lord Espino has his or her right, where appropriate, to address the Order Commission of the National Council, in such a way that it is a decision taken within the scope of a competent body," he noted.
On the other hand, he clarified that it is not time to think in 2012 and that an alliance for the presidential candidacy was never at the negotiating table.
Nava Vázquez said he was convinced that Acción Nacional will be strengthened from the internal election on December 4 and 5, in which his successor must be elected.
"That (an alliance in 2012) is an issue that was never at the negotiating table, at least not for the time being, it was an approach to the local election that was successful and fulfilled the goal," he said.
The Panista leader pointed out that the PAN is supported by 71 years of dialogue, so "we are not scared of internal competition; the conletarian "we enjoyed and strengthen us," he said.
He said the council will undoubtedly be able to choose the best or the best leader who requires National Action for the next three years, hence it will hand over the swindle "to whom the council determines."
"I've got that utter reassurance and openness to any of the five I'd be proud to be handed the party president's office."
He predicted that the repercussion recorded by the party in the last elections of July 4 will be reflected in the upcoming contests, although "it's not yet time to think in 2012."
"For the time being it's about accompanying Gov. Gabino Cué on this task".
In this context, he stressed that the three governments emanating from alliances in Oaxaca, Puebla and Sinaloa will be the flag and will be grounds for observation throughout the country, hence "many hopes, longings are placed in them, and I am sure Gabino will live up to expectations."
When questioned about PRI criticism of President Felipe Calderón for his speech in the framework of the four years of his government, Cesar Nava called on the PRI to reflect on the six-year terms of former Presidents Ernesto Zedillo and Carlos Salinas de Gortari.
"If the PRI thinks the Zedillo and Salinas governments were better than those of Fox and Calderon; let them begin to defend them; to come out of the closet," he said.
He agreed that the head of the federal executive can make expressions such as those he made in his speech in the National Auditorium, because in the country there is no gag or much less censorship.
Explores Obama on Twitter law in favor of undocumented students
"If you agree that passing the Dream Act is the right thing to do, call your senators to pass this legislation," the U.S. president tweeted on the popular social network.
President Barack Obama today joined the national mobilisation in support of the Dream Act, to legalize undocumented students, with a message on his personal social network Twitter account.
"If you agree that passing the Dream Act is the right thing, call your senators and ask them to pass this legislation," Obama tweeted.
His brief message keeps a cyber liaison open with a broader document that asks the public to immediately communicate with senators, as the legislation's vote is imminent.
"With a vow expected to happen soon, now is the time to have your voice heard," he says.
"The message was released after the 42 Republican senators threatened to block the vote on any legislation, unless the Bush-era tax cuts and government funding are approved.
Such a blockade, announced in a letter signed by the 42 Republican senators, could affect the Dream Act vote and other projects such as the ratification of the new START treaty with Russia against the Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons.
Discussed by the Republican blockade of the Dream Act and other projects, presidential spokesman Robert Gibbs trusted that once the fiscal issue has been resolved, a timetable can be set for the rest of the projects.
"I think there's plenty of time.
"We all agree that we have to resolve the tax matter before the end of the year..."
"I don't think we can reach an agreement at some point on that and then we can plan the sequence for the rest," he said.
Senate Democratic Majority Leader Harry Reid announced on Tuesday that he would call for a "developing debate vote" for the Dream Act, so the initiative to legalize undocumented students could be balloted starting next Thursday in the U.S., although it's more likely next week.
The senatorial version of the Dream Act or American Dream Act would legalize about 800 thousand young people who entered the United States before the age of 16 have remained in the country for the last five years and entered a higher education institution or the military.
But supporters require 60 votes to approve the initiative, which is why they require the requisition of a handful of Republicans, at a time when pro-prosying organizations of the project are heavily lobbying to secure the passage of the law.
Democrats, a majority of whom support the Dream Act, have 58 seats.
Yet at least one of them, Sen. Ben Nelson of Nebraska, has said he will vote against it, and only one Republican has expressed his support for the law, Richard Lugar of Indiana.
For this reason, Dream Act supporters need to join several Republicans in case other Democrats decide to turn their backs on the initiative.
Among the most polished Republicans are Maine senators Olympia Snowe and Susan Collins, as well as outspoken senators George Lemieux of Florida, who completed senator Sen. Mel Martinez.
Other Republicans who supported the Dream Act in the past are Orrin Hatch of Utah and Kay Bailey Hutchinson of Texas but have said they won't support it on another occasion.
Another Republican he supported, John McCain, hasn't defined his stance at this point.
Although the House of Representatives has a different version, analysts agree that the Dream Act faces the biggest hurdles in the Senate.
Literature they put their finger in the sore
The second round of Language Pleasures was a stroll where the attendees showed the narration
"Tex sex in the language I understand it but... the finger in the sore? what is expected of me?" asked the writer Luisa Valenzuela invited to open the second round of The Pleasures of the Language, a cycle that celebrates literature in Spanish and that this time used the subtitle of his question (at another fair the round table was dedicated to Sex in the language, she explained).
The finger in the sore... "puts us in trouble," the Argentinian writer listened sweetly to the audience, mostly of young people, who gathered at the International Book Fair of Guadalajara (FIL) to listen to this round table moderated by Marisol Shultz and where Valenzuela, Sergio Ramírez, Mayra Montero and Luis García Montero participated.
Luisa Valenzuela read a text that she dedicated to Gonzalo Celorio and that Shultz would later define as a great tale, with Luisa in the first person as the protagonist who in search of knowing what the "llaga," goes from doctor to doctor, of which he does not get answers although he does clue in the same narrative thread.
From the infectologist he is convinced: "Patience isn't my strength, maybe it's my sore."
Ultimately, after a frustrated rapprochement with the traumatologist who stole his breath in the middle of the aisle, but who clarifies that sore is not a scientific term; it is the psychoanalyst who reveals the mystery: "the sorrow is the real, the symbolic is the scar."
The protagonist shouted relieved and concluded "of the sore I only managed to know that it is the real open and that it does not exist," but convinced that the pleasures and trappings of the language must be very intertwined.
Valenzuela, the Writer, played with both of them.
The Nicaraguan Sergio Ramírez also exhibited a delicious narration that titled Praise of the Invention, where the finger in the sore is not named but at one point it is discovered.
With brushstrokes of magical realism, he tells of a famous sorceress, who also touched clouds, he brought back men who had gone a long way or repaired the virtues that a maid would have lost, a narration that he turned out to then speak America, the great novel he didn't lacked from the beginning his own chroniclers to tell her and cited Bernal Díaz del Castillo who criticizes the excesses of what the conquering captains said.
"Closing that invented us all (writers) mocked the feigned witnesses who appeared in the stories presented as real, but would otherwise never have come to tell the story of America with imagination that it lacked even modesty."
However, he pointed out "the crazies and the deceivers best imagine" and that is where reality and a lie converge, where it is no longer known that the narrated belongs to one side or another, where fictional literature reaches its heyday.
He's actually called the 'gavantine tradition.'
"By his inheritance we are what is told".
In her turn, the writer Mayra Montero limited and applauded the variety of interpretations that gave rise to the invitation of the FIL with her round table The finger in the sore.
The Cuban-Puertorriqueña pointed hers towards the great sores of the social violence that her country is going through - she shares Latin America - as a result of the disputes of drug trafficking and sales.
"I believe that the good literature, the good poetry, the good songs enclose a tacit commitment that of course is not premeditated," he said, citing that the reality of the literature is the communication and the dimension of the compromise that is established between author and readers, and that in a "chemical reaction" of the latter.
On the night it was closed by the Spanish poet Luis García Montero whose intervention also emphasized "of that finger on the sore of the writer's committed writer."
He considered that in a world that lives in a hurry, also in the intellectual customs and control of the consciences that results in dogmatisms, forgetting "that behind every affirmation there is a small no and behind every denial there is a small yes."
"In order to own our thoughts and to own the reality we must nuance: and nuance means many times putting your finger in the sore and annoying those people who are much more comfortable thinking of caricature, in black and white, in established ideas without nuisance and also nuisance the powers that prefer a world in black and white," he said, inviting to retrieve the sense of writing.
Romano goes for his first title.
Reubén Omar Romano has three defeats in the final, has had ups and downs in his career, but has already managed to reach the decisive series on three occasions.
With the duel of Santos Laguna and Monterrey in the final, they also face two different styles marked by their technicians, who find each other a contrast marked by the results.
Reubén Omar Romano has three defeats in the finals, has had ups and downs in his career, but he has already reached the decisive series on three occasions, although until now it has gone blank and that is why this occasion is very special for him.
The Argentinian has ended up conjoining the medal of the runners-up, has stayed a step behind the winners and curiously with the same team he has played consecutive finals.
The holy strategist arrived at his first finish as a coach in the 2002 Opening Tournament, when he wore the reins of the Morelia with which he fell globally of 4-2 to Toluca; then, in the 2003 Closing he also reached the decisive duel and he also lost it with global 3-1 and it was precisely before Rayados.
Already with Santos Laguna, in the 2010 Bicentennial Tournament again against the Devils, Rubén again suffered a setback, as his team ended up tied 2-2 against the choriceros after 210 minutes of play, but in the penaltis tank he took advantage by 3-1 with two chances to seal the title to finally three of his players failed from the 11 steps and suffered the 4-3 flip.
Rubén has already lost two finals with the Red Devils and one with Monterrey, his rival in turn, so he does not want to run the same luck, seeks to detach himself from the curse and be able to celebrate for the first time a title, although he affirms that for him the most important thing is the institution.
"I don't want to go champion, I don't want to break any statistic, that's because of a hobby that I owe and as a result the title will come for me, but the above is the most important thing," said the helmsman when he was questioned about the numbers he has against playing finals.
Very valid has the previous final, of which, he says, he has learned a lot, "the only lesson we can have is that until the referee whistles the end we can't celebrate, we're prepared in the streak, the players too, because the previous time, with two penalties for throwing we were ready to run out and I think that's a teaching, until the game is over we can celebrate."
To know that this series could be defined in criminal shots, something that doesn't bother him, because he trusts his players: "One is never looking to get to the batch of penalties, one seeks to end in regular time, but sometimes there is no other and you have to be prepared, in the previous tournament we were prepared, unfortunately it made us a bad move the fate, sometimes because of the nerves or the anxiety of having the title in our hands, but it left us, that left us something for this final that we hope we don't get to that instance."
Romano has in his hands to stop being a runners-up technician and taste for the first time the glory given by the titles.
U.S. Changes Rules to Get Visa
Persons must go to Visa Applicant Service Centres prior to their interview in the Consular Section; the cost will be lower as well as the waiting time.
From January 10, 2011, he will change the procedure of processing a visa at the U.S. embassy and consulates.
Under the new procedure, the majority of applicants will have to go to Visa Apparer Service (ASC) centres prior to their interview in the Consular Section.
ASC staff shall take the biometric information of the applicant, which will be reviewed by the Consular Section prior to the interview.
The new procedure will have certain advantages for applicants, including:
The total cost of processing the visa will be lower, it will no longer be necessary to make a payment to arrange the appointment, another payment to process the visa application and another for the courier service to deliver the document.
The applicant will only make a one-off payment that will cover the three services.
The current fee will remain unchanged: the equivalent of $140 for a tour visa, $150 for applied visa cases (including visas for temporary workers) and $390 for merchant visas and investors.
Persons who wish to renew their visa will not have to go to an interview with the Consular Officer, but will only go to the nearest ASC where they will present their documents and take their fingerprints.
Because the biometric information will be taken at the ASC, applicants who require a second appointment to be interviewed in the Consular Section will remain in it less time than in the past.
Likewise, those who apply for a visa at the Consulates in Ciudad Juárez, Monterrey and Nuevo Laredo will no longer pay the extra service fee of 26 dollars.
For additional information about the new visa application procedure, please visit http://mexico.usembassy.gov/boletines/sp101201-Visas-FAQs.html
Announce NASA finding of extraterrestrial life
The U.S. space agency summoned the press tomorrow to present a finding that will cause an impact in the search for living beings in space.
The U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) will unveil an astrobiological discovery tomorrow, Thursday, which could prove the theory that extraterrestrial life does exist.
The discovery will be presented at a press conference tomorrow Thursday at 1:00 p.m. at NASA headquarters in Washington by experts in astrobiology, science described as in charge of studying the origin, evolution, distribution and future of life in the Universe.
Upon citing the press, NASA stated that it will present "a finding that will have an impact on the search for extraterrestrial life," which aroused speculators of experts from other countries.
Discontinuation could demonstrate the theory that there are creatures alongside us and in hostile environments that were considered uninhabitable, and they would put at the table the possibility that they will become intelligent beings such as humans if they improve the conditions in which they live.
Apparently it's a microbe that was found at the bottom of Monkey Lake in Yosemite National Park, Calif., where he lives in an environment that was considered too poisonous for any form of life, as the lake is rich in arsenic.
Somehow the creature uses arsenic as a way to survive and this capacity increases the possibility that similar life could exist on other planets, which do not have our benevolent atmosphere.
According to astrobiologists, if these organisms use arsenic in their metabolism, it will be demonstrated that "there is another form of life, a second way of life, different from the one we know."
The space agency will unveil the true scope of the findings at the press conference tomorrow Thursday.
geobiologist Pamela Conrad, who studies the possibility of life on Mars; and biologist Steven Benner, one of the scientists who study Titan (Saturn's greatest moon), will participate in the conference, under the perspective that his chemistry is similar to that of the Earth.
Gesta PRD, PT, Convergence and Opponent 'block' Pattern
With reforms to the Organic Law of Congress, it leads to the arrival of 'bipartisanship', they denounce.
It did not install the 60th Legislature when an opposition bloc arose in Congress, headed by the deputies of the PRD, PT, Convergence and Jesús Manuel Patrón Montalvo, who declared himself independent, and the four questioned one thing: the arrival of 'bipartisanship' to the House.
Before starting the installation session of the 60th Legislature, Felipe Manzanarez Rodriguez of Convergence; Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of the PT; Javier Corvera Quevedo, of the PRD; and Patrón Montalvo 'threw' against the reform of the Organic Law, since he excluded them.
With this reform, they reiterated, it begins 'bipartisanship', because it will now be two people, of the same number of parties, who will control Congress.
These four deputies, together with Gloria Margarita Santos Aguilar, coordinator of the PRD, were the only ones who voted against the Board, headed by Panista Salvador López Brito and Luis Antonio Cárdenas Fonseca, in his capacity as vice president.
Corvera Quevedo detailed that these reforms institutionally constitute the political marginality on the part of the PRI and PAN.
He explained that in the next few days they will talk to the representatives of the PAN and PRI to modify the scheme, based on a political will, and if they don't have it, then they will be overwhelmed again by both parties and thus the opposition coalition ended.
"So far (the opposition coalition) arrived, it's what I'm watching, because we're excluded from any decision, they don't even have the political deference to consider the opinion of the rest."
Felipe Manzanarez Rodriguez of Convergence mentioned that with the PRD, PT and Patrón, there are coincidences and that is why they are forming a plural group.
It cannot be allowed, he continued, that Congress after being monopathic is now "duobolic."
"It's not the spirit of law-abiding that there's an eternal bipatidism in Congress."
To begin with, this group is beginning with 5 legislators, and they are conversing with other comrades to make them do more, the converter abounded.
Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of the PT explained that the Congress currently became a 'bootine' between two, allusion to the PAN and the PRI.
"Before it was just a game, now they're handing it out between two".
Patrón Montalvo, asked to respect the personality of the congresswoman, to be given his place at the negotiating tables.
"Let's not because of coupular agreements, 5 is left out that we're probably gonna make 9 deputies, right now the five who see us, separated in different fractions, we have decided to unify ourselves to look for a place at the negotiating table, that's the issue that in the reform they made that they agreed, we're out and we don't agree with that reform."
Learn $200 a thousand more for "supplying"
Mazatlan City of Mazatlan pays another check to the questioned owner of an electrical equipment company.
An additional check for just over 200,000 pesos in favor of Lorena Guadalupe Camacho Palazuelos was issued by the City Council.
With this, the amount that has been paid in this second semester to the owner of the electrical equipment company, amounts to 1 million 200 thousand pesos.
According to the transparency page of the Municipality, this last check number 9258447 was issued on November 16, in the amount of 20,144.56 pesos, under the concept of "liquidation of accounts to be paid."
Treasurer Luis Miguel Lopez Moreno had advanced that more money would come out of the Commune in favor of this supplier, which was observed by the Superior Audit of the State for selling at a higher price.
What hasn't been reported is the total that is still owed to it and, therefore, how many more checks are missing to be issued in favor of Camacho Palazuelos.
"You haven't bought anything, you're just being paid what you owe, just like the rest of the suppliers," he said in an interview.
The last checks "reappeared" in the last trimester of the year: September 24, two on October 15, October 19, October 29, October 29, on November 16.
Although the supplier has not yet been published in the Official Journal of the State, as set out by the Law when a company is questioned by the ASE, in order to prevent other governments from using its services, the Treasurer said they have not reused their services.
The above, after July 25, Northwest published a review of the checks issued by the City Council to its suppliers, in which it was revealed that, from March 2008 to July 9, 2010, in the triennium of Mayor Jorge Abel Lopez Sanchez, Camacho Palazuelos was paid the Municipality more than 10 million pesos, through 42 checks.
He also carried out transactions with Jumapam, where he received checks of more than 3 million pesos.
Urge to attend tourist sites
Tourist businessmen erupt foreign tourist accidents due to poor conditions of Glorieta Sánchez Taboada in Mazatlan.
After the fall of a foreign tourist in a plunge of Glorieta Sánchez Taboada, Mazatlan's tourism sector asked the authorities to pay attention to the areas and sites that are in poor condition and that project a bad image of the port.
Salvador Barraza Rubio, president of the Association of Traders of the Golden Zone, recalled that on different occasions they have denounced taking care of the image of the destination and reversing how bad tourists find with new activities or routes.
He said the poor condition of the streets and banquets has affected because tourists come for a walk and see the mess and turn him around to nearby businesses.
"Practitioners don't know what it's like to close a company one day and have no clue to the money being lost, or else they wouldn't do the works this way in so long and without proper planning."
Hotelier José Ramón Manguart Sanchez said that the tourist's fall exposed the lack of prevention and maintenance on the part of the City Council.
"The municipality will have to assume its responsibility and prevent it from happening again, how to do it because by giving preventive maintenance and not falling only into the corrective," he said.
"We are eager to enjoy a clean city, because it's not only because it's a tourist area you have maintenance, but as inhabitants that we deserve a clean place to live and the City Council must change its strategy to keep the city right."
There are other places that also look careless and incomplete, he said, such as the Shield and Mazatlan Venadito.
"Today, in order for the tourist to Come it does not depend solely on the promotion, there has to be a good product, a city that has cleaning on its beaches, on its avenues, permanent maintenance in what are the tourist attractions," he said.
'It hasn't been given due attention and these kinds of facts exposes something that's notorious for everyone, maintenance is needed.'
Raúl Llera Martinez, Undersecretary of Tourist Promotion, said there is an urgent need for the rescue of Mazatlan tourist sites, because they are not used correctly and project a bad image.
He talked about a number of places that must be put in line in conjunction with the municipality and being a priority to keep the city up to date, clean, orderly and that is attractive because it is of tourist vocation.
"Fortunately Mazatlan offers many historical and cultural sites and places that you will have to order first, clean and put us online in order to make them attractive to be able to add them in the inventory that we have to promote the destination."
Ask IP results to Congress
Eduardo Dávalos, president of the CCE, proposed that the deputies assume the great responsibility of being an example of looking for a better Sinaloa because of the various challenges it faces.
A  Legislature of dignified work free of agreements between parties and the state executive demands the Mazatlan business sector, because there is a convulsed Sinaloa that faces major problems.
Since yesterday the 60th legislature, made up of seven political forces, 19 PRI deputies, 13 from the PAN, three of the New Alliance Party, two of the PRD, one of the PT, one of Convergence and one more of the PVEM, which adds a total of 40 popular representatives.
Among the list of expectations of the new members of the Congress of the State is that they come to carry out a clean and commitment management with society and to the different sectors that require responsible legislative work and that the same patterns of partisanship or paternalism should not be repeated.
Eduardo Dávalos Zamora, president of the Business Coordinating Council, proposed that the newly inaugurated 40 deputies of the State Congress assume the great responsibility of being an example of seeing a better Sinaloa because of the various challenges it faces.
"We hope that the deputies will actually exercise their right, free of agreements between the parties because it is customary for them to make arrangements to ensure their own interests and become carnival comparsas of the rulers and the leaders of the parliamentary factions."
He therefore talked about certifying the commitment to establish the necessary bridges to bring the Governor forward in turn.
"Let's worry that they come to do a decent job and especially committed to society and to the sectors because it was the Sinaloans that gave them the vote of confidence in the last elections."
José Ramón Manguart Sánchez, president of the Association of Hotels Tres Islas, considered that one of the great challenges of the 40 popular representatives is to push and join forces in search of the economic growth of the state, because today the economic situation is very complicated.
"The most important thing is to boost economic growth, to generate incentives for investment and infrastructure and that will in some way encourage the generation of jobs."
With regard to the tourist activity, he demanded that the new legislators give him the weight within the state budget to strengthen the Secretary of State Tourism.
"I am aware that it is the second Secretariat in the state with the lowest budget and is inconsistent as an important economic activity that generates foreign exchange and investment and that it directly competes with the agricultural industry."
In addition, it is necessary to label resources on detonating tourism projects that help to give security to the state and in the update to the Alcohols Act that they have not been able to resolve in previous legislatures.
You're looking for a Wizard King
Doubts, cars and bikes, among other toys, are the yearning of the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family.
For his parents, the upcoming King's Day it will be difficult to please his illusions, since his home is made up of nothing more and no less than 12 people and a goat.
However, Adelaida, Isabel, Jesús, José Alfredo, María Guadalupe, Isaías, José Juan and Miguel do not lose the hope that Melchor, Gaspar and Baltazar will give them the toys and clothes they so much desire.
The family inhabits a humble house of the Colonia Salvador Allende and they live a day because the father does not have a fixed job.
"Sometimes we'll see it hard because my husband doesn't have chamba, but we sell mandarin and potato... already in the afternoon I go out with the 'fulks' to sell on the street," said Cayetana Valenzuela Andrade, mother of the little ones.
Adelaide, 4, wants a doll or bike, while her three-year-and-year-old sister Elizabeth would like a Barbie doll.
For his part, 10-year-old José Alfredo has long aspiring to have a 'monster' cart and for his 8-month-old brother Jesus, Cayetana suggests size clothes for a year.
"He told me (José Alfredo), 'I'd like this monster', but I said 'Let's see son', because it was very expensive," Cayetana recalled with sadness.
For the eldest children, those who were in school, clothing and shoes would be the ideal.
María Guadalupe, 11 years old, is size 12; Isaiah, 10 years old, is size 10, while Miguel, 8 years old, wears size eight.
José Juan, 14, lacks tennis, so a five-and-a-half-and-a-half-size-your-studded would be very helpful.
If you'd like to help realize the dream of the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family you can participate in the Wizard King campaign, by donating a toy or cash at the Northwest Reception.
Fourteen Suspected Drug Smuggler Arrested
A dozen persecutions were carried out on Wednesday in Estrie as part of the decommissioning of a Cannabis production and sales network.
Fourteteen people have been arrested who will face various charges.
Chasments have taken place in Sherbrooke, Orford and Stoke, mainly in places where the drug was produced.
"About 750 plants in Cannabis have been seized, four kilos of hashish, money and equipment destined for production," says Louis-Philippe Ruel, spokeswoman for Quebec Security.
In all, eleven men and three women have been arrested, including the two alleged leaders of the network, two Sherbrooke men.
Detainees had to appear in Sherbrooke's justice palace in the hours following their arrest.
They could deal with Cannabis's production charges, possession aimed at trafficking, plotting and the theft of services, among others.
These chases have taken place after a long investigation that has lasted nearly a year.
"It's the information provided by the public that has allowed us to carry out the arrests," Mr Ruel said.
This police operation involved about seventy officers from the Estrie investigation module of Quebec Security, the Sherbrooke Police Service and the Memphrémagog Police Department.
Unsecured clandestine migration can be combated
The Conservatives bill, which the government says should solve the problem of skirt-laden skirts, will surely face gripping strong opposition, as the three opposition parties announced on Wednesday that they couldn't support it.
"I rely mainly on the Charter."
"I am here with a party that has created the granite you step on," said liberal leader Michael Ignatieff, surrounded by deputies after the weekly caucus meeting.
The Liberals argue that the bill in its current form, which would make it possible to detain for up to a year anyone who comes from a ship identified as transporting clandestine immigrants, violates the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.
Quebec Bloc leader Gilles Duceppe also said he would not be able to support the bill because it targets the wrong target.
"We're up against it and it's apparently all the opposition parties are against the project, so we'll already look at what the government is doing," Mr Duceppe said.
Criticism of the NPD on immigration, Olivia Chow, has rightly rejected the criticism that her party is not firm enough with illegal immigrants.
"The New Democrats agree that action should be taken against those in charge and that's what we're aiming for with another bill, the C-35," Ms. Chow said.
"We don't support the Conservative bill because it attacks refugees and jails children for a minimum of a year," Ms Chow said.
Immigration Minister Jason Kenney has accused the parties of the position of playing politicians and disregarding the will of Canadians.
"The vast majority of Canadians tell MPs that they don't tolerate this kind of clandestine migration, these criminal operations against Canada and that they treat the country as if it were a doormat."
"They're waiting for Canada to take firm action," Minister Kenney said.
The government has not announced a vote of confidence on its bill, which could lead the country to an election as a result of its rejection.
However, a Conservative has informed the QMI Agency that the government would call for a vote so that every MP he or she is going to oppose will then explain to his or her constituents.
The new unit doesn't achieve unanimity.
Despite an announcement of the possible establishment of a permanent anti-Corruption and collusion unit by Jean Charest, numerous observers continue to call for the establishment of a public consultation committee on the issue.
Secretary of Public Security Robert Dutil supports that this idea hasn't been raised two days ago: Quebec would have consulted with the New York Department of Investigation since January last year.
The Metropolis has a permanent anti-Corruption unit since 1870.
As a result, Tuesday &apos; slipped more than one by surprise.
It should be said that, during a meeting of the Permanent Council on the Quebec police services held on 29 October, this measure was never debated despite the presence of Robert Dutil.
That was enough for the Quebec party's critic who deals with the issue of public security, Stéphane Bergeron, to describe this announcement as "a rabbit taken out of the joke" and as "a last-minute dirty solution."
Minister Dutil, for his part, has responded to those who fear that political power will have too much influence over possible future unity.
"You'll see the results when we announce it," he simply says.
"We're aware of this problem."
For the president of the CSQ, Réjean Parent, there's nothing that can replace the release of a public survey.
"The stubbornly Mr. Charest to avoid the creation of this commission of inquiry, the more people share our opinion, that is, he has something to hide," he explains.
"The only way to dispose of a commission of inquiry is with the resignation of Jean Charest," explains Yves Boisvert, professor of ethics and governance at the National Public Service School.
To get out of this stalemate, he suggests Quebec should grant a special mandate to the controller-general to carry out a survey on collusion and corruption.
"We must be able to extend the mandate of the general controller," he concludes.
It also believes that Quebec could give the controller the same powers as a commissioner, thus allowing it to force people to testify.
The creation of a permanent crew is not the only budding project in the Charest Government.
Quebec is also calling on Ottawa to amend the Criminal Code to better adapt to this type of crime.
This request seems to come from Operation Marteau's prosecutor, who has informed Minister of Justice Jean-Marc Fournier that jurisprudence is not explicit in this type of investigation.
The Federal Minister of Public Security, Vic Toews, for his part, has confirmed that his Justice counterpart, Rob Nicholson, is currently inclined to make certain amendments on this issue.
Thirteen 131 persons were arrested during Operation Spring 2001 on biker groups, even if the Criminal Code had not been adapted, 131 persons were arrested.
Later, Ottawa pushed for the adoption of the Law on gangsterism.
Rios under extreme surveillance
Various watercourses are being closely monitored due to heavy precipitation that has occurred in several regions of the province since Tuesday.
A landslide took place at Shawinigan on Wednesday afternoon in Mauricie.
As a pre-precaution, two houses have been evacuated.
In the evening, there was another dirty slide in Saint-Lin-Laurentides, in Lanaudiere.
A highway plunged.
From Tuesday to Wednesday night, several regions recorded heavy rainfall, between 40 and 70 millimeters of rain.
An additional 20 to 40 millimeters are also expected from now until Thursday night, depending on the region.
Sectors located to the north of the river and in the Outaouais valley were the hardest hit.
They could receive up to 100 millimetres of rain.
Since these precipitations are in addition to the melting of the snow that had already accumulated in certain sectors, it is feared that several rivers will break down.
On Wednesday night, Quebec's Civil Security unit issued alerts for the Ouareau and Achigan rivers in Lanaudiere.
The rivers of the north, in Saint-Jerome, in the Laurentids, and of the Chaene, in Lotbiniere, have already caused floods in the past and are under surveillance.
Heavy precipitation has caused some lakes to overwhelm in Sainte-Julienne, Sainte-Marcelline-de-Kildare and Rawdon.
In Saint-Calix, heavy rains have scrawled a temporary bridge.
As a preventive measure, ten residences have been evacuated.
In Outaouais, the overflow of certain lakes has led to the closure of a number of lanes, in particular in Mansfield-et-Pontefract municipality.
The civilian security unit is still on alert and monitors the situation closely, according to Annik Bouchard, a security adviser.
The Quebec Flood Fore Forecast Center, for its part, issued announcements on Wednesday communicating the strong floods of several rivers, in particular the Maskinongé, Assomption, Ouareau, Achigan, Batiscan and Loup.
Partial blasting Dupont
Quebec radio presenter Stéphane Dupont has suffered a certain setback to the Canadian Radio-to-television Standards (CCNR) Council for controversial statements in relation to social assistance and on Haitians, as well as their rude language on the radio.
The CCNR considers that Mr Dupont and his CHOI 98.1 FM team had the right to express their opinion, but certain statements contained "unfazed information about social assistance, unduly negative representation of Haitians and rude language that shouldn't be used in emissions released during the day."
The council has also indicated that discussions on suicide, including disclosing the names of two people who had committed suicide, do not violate broadcasting codes.
The CCNR had received a number of complaints in relation to comments made on broadcasts between 2008 and 2010.
The first decision was handed down following a complaint by the Common Front of People Receiving Social Assistance from Quebec.
The group was complaining that the amounts mentioned in the issue were not correct.
Presenters must communicate reliable data rather than deceiving.
In relation to the suicide, presenter Stéphane Dupont had stated that suicide was an act of cowardice.
Most members of the Committee have come to the conclusion that the message conveyed was that suicide should not be seen as a solution to the problems of life.
"Most of the Committee believes that the episodes called into question "Dupont le midi" have addressed a sensitive issue in an equitable and appropriate manner."
In this regard, therefore, any blame has been thrown away.
The latest litigation related to the statements made after the earthquake in Haiti.
At the time, Stéphane Dupont and Jean-René Dufort had starred in a media confrontation that had provoked numerous reactions.
The CHOI presenter stated that he had not made any donations to help Haitians because he couldn't know for sure whether those in need would actually receive the money.
The Committee considers that these comments are not abusive, but that their cumulative effect constitutes an unduly negative representation of Haitians.
"I've already served my three-day suspension upstairs."
"My file has been clean for a year and a half," the presenter said.
In all the decisions, the Committee denounces the issuance of rude language.
In any case, the CHOI-FM radio network has collaborated "even more than required."
There will be minimal consequences for the broadcaster, which must, among other things, announce the decisions once in the hours of the largest hearing within three days.
A Kung-fu King in Montreal
Arnezeddine Zemmal's bouquets of Algerian origin has had an extremely quirky tour.
The athle athle athlete, exemplifies in "sanda" (kung-fu "full contact"), kickboxing and thai box, goes upstairs to the tatamis and rings of the world in search of new challenges.
"I began practicing martial arts such as kung-fu and kickboxing 12 years ago in Algeria," explained Zemmal, who has lived in the metropolis since 2008 to advance at the professional level.
"In my case, I am looking for fights everywhere because, for those who don't enjoy the on-the-floor gambling involving mixed martial arts, there are few competitions in North America."
The athle athlete, who works as a security agent and as an employee of the UPS company, has recently given bad news to his rivals, winning two gold medals at the Amateur World Cup of the World Karate and Kickboxing Association (WKA), held from October 23-30 in Edinburgh, Scotland.
"I've won in the kickboxing full-to-cuff and thai box categories," he explained.
"Currently I'm getting ready to participate in the San Shou World Cup in China".
"They will invite athletes who have finished in the top four in the last world championship in each category."
"In 2009, I won the bronze medal in Toronto".
"In 2005, in Vietnam, I ended up in eighth place".
"In 2007, in China, I finalized the tournament in fifth place, but reached third place in Toronto in 2009," he added.
"What's interesting is that I've been progressing in every competition".
"At this step, I'll be the first this time or next year".
San shou or "sanda" is a discipline originally from China in which it is allowed to be beaten with fists and feet, as well as throw opponents on the ground.
Clashes originally take place on a platform and, less frequently, in a ring.
The most renowned pugilist in this type of combat is the American of Vietnamese origin Cung Le, widely recognized in the martial arts world.
Although Nacer Zemmal must only maintain a competition in his career, he will no doubt go to the great "King of Sanda" tournament, the kung-fu Super Bowl.
"This competition is the most notorious and prestigious in China," explains the sympathetic kid.
"It's like the equivalent of Lumpinee Stadium (Thailand's largest stadium) ."
"It's been an incredible experience".
Zemmal has won his first match, in the semi-finals of his category (80 kg), but lost in the final.
"Whoever beat me is a true professional," he says.
"There's four contenders per category (80 kg, 85kg, 90 kg and 90kg)."
"Semi-complete winners face each other in the final of their category."
"Nextreme the champions of each category are confronted to crown a single champion, the king of kung-fu."
$450 million in non-convenied contracts
Hydro-Québec has awarded $450 million direct contracts to engineering companies for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert hydroelectric project.
At the center of this avalanche of more than a hundred contracts is a consortium made up of the companies BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor, which has obtained without a pre-consumer tender the most important contract, that of the management of the project, for an amount of 295 million.
The consortium, called Energie gérance, was created in 2004 following Hydro-Québec &apos; s decision to entrust the private sector with its responsibility to manage this important $5 billion hydroelectric project.
At the beginning of 2005, the Energie geance consortium obtained the lucrative contract without a public tender being carried out in advance.
For the Quebec Party, recourse to consortia of companies poses a serious ethical problem that threatens the elimination of competition and leads to an increase in prices.
"This is a new method of collusion and removing the competition."
"In other words, the people, instead of doing it in secret, are now doing it openly," Quebec Party MP Sylvain Simard said yesterday at a press conference.
Earlier in the morning, Mr. Simard raised the issue in the National Assembly because he says he doesn't understand how the public's interest can benefit in this way.
Natural Resources Minister Nathalie Nordeau responded in the afternoon, firmly denying that there has been collusion with this contract.
"I'm getting tired," he protested, arguing that the PQ had acted "dishonestly" and "unrigorous."
He pointed out that "the minimum" would be for the PQ to apologize.
Consortia's formula is a common practice throughout the world.
Typically, it is an alliance with a time limit that allows two or more companies to pool their knowledge to respond to a tender for a complex project.
The Eastmain project presents a high level of complexity, taking into account the magnitude of the works.
The works have already begun and will last until 2012.
In any case, the contract has been granted without the entry into play of the competition; it has been a negotiation.
In addition, the consortia formula has ceased to be the exception to the norm in Quebec, even for relatively simple projects.
As Le Devoir published last last week, the consortia formula in the technical advisory sector is being investigated in the Government.
A quick glimpse of Quebec's business record allows more than 200 consortia to be identified, of which some appear permanent.
In this way, Dessau works in association with most of its main competitors, such as CIMA, Génivar, SNC-Lavalin, Roche et Tecsult and others.
Each combination, and there are dozens of them, leads to a single legal person, i.e. Dessau.
With regard to Consociation, Minister Normandeau has stressed that "James Bay's territory responds to a particular labor dynamic and Hydro-Québec responds specifically to it since the 1970s."
The great boss of the Eastmain project, Réal Laporte of the Société d'énergie de la Baie James (SEBJ), has given an explanation that has highlighted the fact that the management contract of the project has been directly granted.
"If you respond to a tender and there's a few entrants, your prices are going to be higher."
"You've got two options: you can get in touch with the players in the sector and tell them: we're going to negotiate a satisfying agreement and at a good price."
"However, the idea is that what matters isn't the price, it's the competition of people," Mr Laporte said.
The contract awarded to the Energie gérance consortium is part of a long list of contracts of more than $50,000 obtained by the Quebec Party.
The contracts were awarded by Hydro-Québec or SEBJ for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert project.
Upon verification, Le Devoir has identified 109 technical advisory contracts granted between 2002 and March 2010 to companies or their subsidiaries, in particular geotechnical laboratories.
SNC-Lavalin has obtained 22 contracts, Génivar obtained 16, Tecsult 14 and Dessau 10, without taking into account his participation in Energie gérance.
All contracts were negotiated.
A total of $49,783,344, inclusive of Energie &apos; s contract of $295,131,564, is $49,78344.
Sylvain Simard has hinted that the four companies that make up the consortium (BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor) are "good acquaintance of the Quebec Liberal Party."
Minister Normandeau has dropped that the PQ is also linked to these "funded" companies, before pointing out that it is more about their leaders.
Barometer of responsible consumption. "Green" tag doesn't inspire confidence
More ecological but deeply skeptical.
The Quebec consumer barely entrusts the actors of sustainable development, as well as fair-trade brands or ecological certificates to guide their choices.
According to the Barometer of responsible consumption, whose first results have been published yesterday, the situation is even worse. The consumer is immersed in a media universe through which messages that defend a more responsible consumption are swarming, but regrets the lack of reliable information and considers that it even curbs its commitment.
Even if the consumption of so-called "socially responsible" products has increased in 2010, Quebecers seem to give very little credit to those trying to sell them.
Two-thirds of them say, in fact, that they do not rely on the environmental commitment of the companies or environmental advertising, reveals the measurement tool developed by the Consumer Observatory responsible for the University of Sherbrooke.
Another conclusion: half of the consumers call into question the reliability of the official certificates that carry so-called fair products, as well as the credence of the products and trademarks that carry labels indicating that they are "responsible," the Barometer says.
Likewise, only 14.6% of Quebecers take the speeches of politicians on this issue seriously.
In any case, information on responsible consumption seems to be essential to stimulate this sector of consumption, currently supported by about a third of Quebec consumers.
In fact, loopholes and the dubious nature of the information cause 46% of consumers to discard this option.
It is the second obstacle identified by the Barometer, after the highest price of these products.
In addition, a third of Quebecers also say they distrust the supposed returns of the eco-friendly products or services they are trying to sell.
On the other hand, almost half of the consumers changed their habits in 2010 and, in particular, changed the brand of one or more products to respect their convictions.
Sixty-seven per cent have prioritised purchases in stores in their neighborhood and have preferred to buy food products cultivated in their region, the Barometre says.
Greener and more responsible (responsive consumption index has reached 64 points above a maximum of a hundred this year), Quebec's Homo consumus loves to take the position of the king of recycling that tries to reduce its impact on the environment by worrying about the protection of the fauna.
However, according to the Barometer, this social consciousness seems to be of gates (of their car) abroad: in 2010, only 28% of Quebecers indicated that they have used public transport or shared a car to defend their environmental principles.
In addition, 39.1% of the participants in this comprehensive behavioral survey, which has just completed its first edition, supports the use of bicycles and walking.
A loan for the state to retrieve eXcentris
The Québec Government, to which they have requested to make a financial commitment in the recovery of the eXcentris cinema complex posed by Société Angus and the Parallel, agrees to cooperate, but only through a loan.
The Société de développement Angus and the Parallé, which were expecting a donation of seven million dollars from the Ministry of Culture to repurchase from Daniel Langlois the eXcentris film complex on Saint-Laurent boulevard boulevard and to return its true typical vocation, have not obtained what they requested.
The Government has agreed to get involved in the matter, but on its own terms.
Instead of the expected donation, the Québec government is proposing a loan that would, in in fact, be less than initial expectations; it could constitute a viable solution on advantageous terms, at least in the opinion of certain public employees.
Quebec had already rejected last spring's first plan of action and a $12.8 million request, but applications have since been revised downwards.
The President of the Société de développement des entreprises culturelles (SODEC), Francois Macerola, who is leading the eXcentris file, submitted a proposal and an action plan to Minister St-Pierre.
He has submitted a final report about a dozen days ago.
SODEC, however, received a counter-prosecution from the cabinet on Tuesday.
The Parallel and Angus have the deadline until December 31 to respond.
In any case, it seems that the hypothesition of a donation from the State must be completely discarded.
Remember that the Association of Cinema Owners and Cinema Cinema Complexes in Quebec was opposed to direct investment by the state to support the recovery of eXcentris, arguing that the Ministry of Culture should not finance real estate projects, since it has not done so with other movie theaters.
On the other hand, various representatives of the sector do demand considerable financial assistance.
Daniel Langlois, its owner, has for ten years carried the burden of the author's cinema on its premises of the complex, after financing its own construction, which could justify a derogation by the Treasury Council.
The lack of rooms for independent films is evident in Montreal and eXcentris had been able to loyalize its clienteler.
The company Angus and Parallel, led by Christian Yaccarini, promote a private financing campaign, hoping to raise $700,000 to open its way to the acquisition.
Since the spring of 2009, only one of these rooms, the Parallel, continues to screen films on eXcentris.
The goal of the future buyers is to reopen the three rooms on February 27, screening Xavier Beauvois' beautiful film "Des hommes et des dieux."
However, Christian Yaccarini hasn't re-contact Le Devoir and we don't know what he and his group think of the downward proposals they've received.
Raising the veil of grocated Canada...
New U.S. diplomatic cables released by WikiLeaks unveil what the U.S. sees as Canada's compound with respect to Americans, but both reflect the latter &apos; s sense of superiority towards Canada.
U.S. diplomats are amazed that Canadian electors don't think about Canada-U. U.S. relations when they're going to vote and believe that President Obama's visit has helped the Conservative government get its budget approved in 2009.
The four new communications between the U.S. embassy of Ottawa and Washington that have been made public yesterday offer rather juicy details that are really contained.
A cable of September 22, 2008, i.e. during the middle of the federal electoral campaign, recounts to what extent the United States doesn't fall into the issues debated by political leaders.
This caused a big surprise the U.S. ambassador-in-cherrano to Ottawa, David Wilkins.
Mr. Wilkins offers a few explanations about this situation.
"Perhaps the political leaders are conditioned by a complex of inferiority practically inherent to Canadians in dealing with daily domestic issues, rather than leaving such a choice to the United States (as during the 1988 free trade campaign) ," he writes.
Therefore, he concludes, the United States is "the proverbial elephant of this choice: ubiquitous but potentially too dangerous to talk about it."
The ambassador has even more problems in understanding this "deensing silence" because the two countries maintain intense political and economic relations.
"Logically, he believes, the capacity of a candidate, of a party and, moreover, of the head of a party to manage these essential relations should be a key factor in which voters should think when it comes to voting."
You can also observe this sense of importance the cable sent on January 22, 2009 by Terry Breese, a business manager in Ottawa, to prepare President Barack Obama for his first visit to Ottawa.
"His journey will help to ensure the government's survival during the confidence vote in early February on the federal budget, by which Canada will record deficits for the first time in the last decade due to a recovery plan of between $30 billion and $40 billion," Mr. Breese writes.
There is no mention that the government had to extend parliament before Christmas to prevent it from replacing the coalition or that the opposition parties conditioned its survival to the quality of the proposed recovery plan.
Another communication from January 2008 signed by Ambassador Wilkins warns Washington about the ever-negative image transmitted by the drama series released in the U.S. CBC.
The ambassador worries the issuance of Border, Little Mosque on the Prairie, Intelligence and H2O, in which unsubstantial references to Maher Arar are made, "No-Fly-List" or even the kidnappings for the CIA to carry out energetic interrogations.
Another cable says that, during her first bilateral meeting, Hillary Clinton asked his French counterpart Bernard Kouchner if Europe would be willing to host some Guantanamo prisoners.
Mr. Kouchner said yes, but it depended "on every case."
At the end of the meeting, they wrote: "He has delivered a document to the Secretary of State concerning Omar Khadr, a 15-year-old Muslim of Canadian origin."
"The secretary agreed to review the file".
Ultimately, we can mention that the mood has been heated in relation to the WikiLeaks site.
Tom Flanagan, a long-running senior adviser to Stephen Harper, told CBC radio that the person responsible for this massive leak should be "murdered."
Mike Huckabee echoes Mike Huckabee in the U.S.
A Global Plan to Remote Enlightenment
Yesterday, the United Nations has proposed in Cancun a plan for the renewal of domestic, commercial and industrial lighting in a hundred countries to reduce greenhouse gases (GHGs) in an amount that corresponds to almost a third of the additional decrease that must be carried out to stabilize the world's climate by 2020.
This ambitious plan is framed in all major concrete projects that the United Nations hopes will be adopted in Cancun in order to compensate for the lack of binding reduction commitments in the industrialized countries.
According to the plan presented yesterday, if incandescent bulbs, which still monopolize between 50 and 70 per cent of the market, be replaced by fluocompact light bulbs or, even better, by electroluminescent diode bulb bulbs (DEL or "LED" in English), a large part of the 1.8 billion tons (Gt) of GHGs emitted on the planet would be avoided to illuminate humans.
Enlightenment is responsible for 8 per cent of global emissions.
It's the equivalent of 70 per cent of emissions from the planetarium car fleet.
In Copenhagen last year, major issuing countries, such as the United States, China, Europe and others, including Canada, committed to voluntary reduction plans to reduce global GHG emissions from 53 Gt to 49 Gt.
However, 5 Gt should still be reduced from this amount to stabilize, by 2020, the increase in temperatures by two degrees Celsius in order to prevent it from getting out of control.
In incandescent bulb bulbs, 95 per cent of energy dissipates in the form of heat and the rest, i.e. 5 per cent, produces light.
In the case of fluocompact bulb bulbs, energy expenditure is reduced by 75 per cent and with new LED bulb bulbs, energy consumption is still reduced twice as much.
Fluocompact bulb bulbs last an average of 6,000 to 10,000 hours.
The new LED bulbs, such as those launched by manufacturer Philips this week (a company that has also participated in the UN study), have a life expectancy of 25,000 hours and consuming 80 per cent less energy than an incandescent bulb, which allows us to get an idea of the savings that can be achieved with the replacement plan that would be implemented in a hundred countries.
These new bulbs can also work with redetects and emit very little heat.
On top of that, they don't contain any mercury.
A number of specialists have expressed their reservations in relation to fluocompact bulbs, since they contain mercury and must be disposed of as hazardous waste, which is not always possible in developing countries, unless manufacturers place instructions.
However, according to the report, the world's main source of mercury emission is not fluocompact bulbs, but coal thermal power plants, which emit a large amount of mercury to produce the electricity that is wasted with incandescent bulbs.
In the U.S. alone, these power plants emit 25,000 tons of mercury a year.
The report elaborates on the advantages that this international programme would offer to each country.
For example, Indonesia would thus save $1 billion a year in electricity and 8 million tons of CO2, i.e. the equivalent of 3.5 thermal power plants whose construction it would cost $2.5 billion.
Ivory Coast eagerly await the announcement of the electoral results
U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon has called for the "immediate" publication of the results of the presidential elections in the Ivory Coast, as the deadlines have expired.
"The Secretary-General stresses the need for the independent electoral commission to complete its work and announce the provisional results immediately," says a spokesman for the head of the United Nations.
"Ask all Ivory Coast parties and institutions to demonstrate their sense of responsibility and to refrain from any initiative until the provisional results are announced."
Despite the pressure exerted by some foreign governments, the electoral commission has left Wednesday's mid-afternoon deadline without the results being published, three days from Sunday's vote.
About forty minutes before the end of the deadline, the president of the CIS had told television that the commission "continued to be working" to announce the results.
Petitions for the CIS to finally proclaim the name of the second-round victor Laurent Gbagbo and his opponent Alassane Ouattara have multiplied around the world on Wednesday.
Gbagbo's side, however, has annulment by the Constitutional Council of "fraudulent" vows in the north, in the hands of the former Regression of the New Forces (FN) since the failed coup d'état in September 2002.
Pascal Affi N'Guessan, director of the Ivory Coast president's campaign, has stated that voter-bullying maneuvers carried out by the N.N. in the north of the country had deterred supporters of the outgoing president from going to vote.
The entire election could be overturned if the Constitutional Council accepted the complaints submitted by Gbagbo.
Gbagbo &apos; s opponents, however, consider the country &apos; s highest court not to be neutral, since it is led by Paul Yao N'Dré, a close political ally of Gbagbo.
Alassane Ouattara, a man from the north who denies any relationship with the former rebels, has tried "unacceptable" the delay in the publication of the results and has asked Gbagbo to respect them, but has not gone so far as to claim the victory.
His allies claim that Gbagbo is blocking the publication of the results because he knows that he has lost and that filing a complaint with the Constitutional Council after preventing the electoral commission from disseminating the results would be illegal.
The second round of the presidential elections, on Sunday, should have put the icing on the process of reunification and reconciliation of a country divided into two for years.
The U.N. on Wednesday warned Ivory Coast's political class they could be held responsible for possible acts of violence.
Laurent Gbagbo had finished in the first round, on 31 October, with 38% of the vote compared to 32 per cent from Alassane Ouattara, who publicly received the support of former President Henri Konan Bédié (25 per cent in the first round).
The unemployment rate remains stable in France in the third trimester
According to the International Labour Organization (ILO), France &apos; snack-free unemployment rate has remained stable at 9.3 per cent on the mainland (9.7 per cent taking into account the overseas departments), according to data corrected with provisional seasonal variations (CVE) published on Thursday.
The number of unemployed remains at 2,631,000 in mainland France.
Data for the second trimester have been confirmed, 9.3 per cent of the labour force on the mainland and 9.7 per cent with DOM.
Within a year, the unemployment rate has increased by 0.1 points.
In general, 3.4 million people in metropolitan France did not work in the third trimester, but they want to work, whether or not they are available in the two weeks to work and seek employment.
The situation of young people present in the labour market has continued to deteriorate, with a further increase in the unemployment rate to 24.2% in mainland France (0.9 points in three months, 0.5 points in one year), 25% including DOM.
During the third trimester, 637,000 young people between the ages of 15 and 24 were unemployed within the ILO region.
For its part, there has been a slight increase in unemployment among the elderly, up to 6.4% in mainland France (-0.2 points both in three months and one year), i.e. 482,000 people over 50 years of age, which amounts to 6.6% with DOM.
There has only been a stability of unemployment among people between the ages of 25 and 49, with 8.3% in mainland France (-0.1 points in three months, 0.1 points in one year) and 8.8% throughout France.
The Insee has not published the monthly unemployment rate since 2006, following a controversy over its calculation methods.
Eurostat, the European Union's statistics agency, is continuing to do so and on Tuesday announced a rate of 9.8 per cent for France (DOM included) in October, following 9.9 per cent in September.
Alexandre Guérini Investigated and Detained
Alexandre Guérini, a 53-year-old director of waste treatment companies and the brother of President PS of the General Council of Bouches-du-Rhene, is being investigated and has been imprisoned in Luynes prison near Aix-en-Provence as part of an investigation into allegedly fraudulent public contracts.
Alexandre Guérini is being investigated for the abuse of social property, embezzlement of funds and public property, cover-up, active corruption, influence trafficking and possession of ammunition, according to his lawyer.
"Alexandre Guérini is going to wrestle and answer every question the magistrate asks, not ghosts."
"It's Easy to chase him for a multitude of reprehensible acts, it's another thing to prove them," his lawyer, Florence Rault, told AFP before his client's appearance.
Mr. Guérini, Brother of the PS leader in the department, Jean-Noel Guérini, wanted to transmit through his lawyers to the judge, before his appearance, a document to be explained.
On Wednesday night they also began to investigate and imprison one of his people nearby, Philippe Rapezzi.
According to his lawyer, Mr. Lucien Simon, the only thing his client has done has been to deal with the dumps he must manage.
With regard to the allocation of the contracts or the destination of the funds related to their execution, he knows nothing about it, according to counsel Simon.
As part of this file, Daniel Pinna, former director general of Aubagne urban agglomeration services has also been investigated since Wednesday for embezzlement of funds and complicity, but he has been released on parole and is prohibited from meeting with the protagonists of the case.
"My client has been able to explain all the facts they have reproached."
"The investigating magistrate has taken into account the explanations and has released him," explained his lawyer, Mr Yann Arnoux.
From Tuesday to Wednesday they interrogated four other people and released them, under judicial control in the case of two of them, according to the Deputy Public Prosecutor, Michel Raffin.
Michel Karabadkhakian, deputy managing director of the Marseille Provence Métropole (MPM), has been accused of "traffic of influence and passive corruption."
Two officials of the agglomeration of Aubagne, Michale Duval and Christophe Bringuier, have been interrogated for "abstraction and embezzlement of public goods by a person dedicated to the public service" and for "complicity in the embezzlement of public funds," respectively.
Finally, Alexandre Guérini &apos; s partner Jeannie Peretti is being investigated for "disguising the abuse of social property."
Among those who have clashed with Mr. Guérini is Stéphane Brunengo-Girard, recently jailed in a cross-complete case for fraudulent public contracts involving Corsica.
Investigators suspect that their societies have served to launder the capital of great banditry.
The investigation is being carried out under a number of judicial instructions initiated against X in April 2009 following the receipt of an anonymous communications, of course fraud in the allocation of public contracts, in particular in the waste sector.
A source close to the investigation points out that the investigations are especially concentrated in the conditions of assignment and operation of a landfill in La Ciotat, which collects the waste of Aubagne, as well as from other cities in the urban community of Marseille (MPM).
Clotilde Reiss: The Elise has exaggerated the mediation of Syria
During more than ten months, between July 2009 and May 2010, Clotilde Reiss, the young French researcher detained in Iran for "espionage," has had the public in France in jeopardy, warned diplomacy and accentuated the deterioration of relations between Paris and Tehran.
Everything was attentive gadget of U.S. diplomats.
The U.S. documents obtained by WikiLeaks and consulted by Le Monde reveal certain unknown aspects of this crisis.
They describe how the Elite has explained its strategy to Americans to the point of giving them advice to manage their own "repeats" crises in Iran (a small group of hikers).
They show how French leaders tried to convince Washington that the release of the young Frenchwoman did not result in an exchange for the release of Majid Kakavand, an Iranian arrested in France and wanted by U.S. justice for trafficking in materials for the Iranian nuclear program.
Ultimately, they reveal the harsh reality of how the French presidency tried to manipulate the media to give the idea that Syrian President Bachar Al-Assad had played an essential role in Clotilde Reiss.
He didn't actually do anything.
A diplomatic telegram published on August 12, 2009 in Paris is titled "Iran: French comments on how to handle hostage-taking cases."
Elise's advisor for strategic affairs, Francois Richier, accompanied by the director of the Quai d'Orsay for North Africa and the Middle East, Patrice Paoli, talks with American diplomat Kathleen Allegrone.
They'll explain the French method.
U.S. officials are advised to do the following to repatriate their own citizens forcibly detained in Iran.
"Make loud," they say, "continuingly loose" in the media the words "fundamental human rights," "innocence," "immediateliberation."
Everything to embarrass the Iranian regime, caring about its image.
"Whether the Iranians ask you to stop, do it even more often".
Because, once attempts to negotiate discreet channels are exhausted, "silence won't solve anything," the Iranians are "experienced in dilatory maneuvers."
