From mid-afternoon, young Koreans can't gambling online
The South Korean government is going to adopt a law called "yellow" that will prevent teens from participating in online gambling after mid-afternoon by blocking their access, according to government officials.
In December, a bill will be submitted to Parliament to ask online gambling companies to block access after midnight games for young people under the age of 16, according to representatives of the Ministries of Culture and the Family.
"A lot of kids play all over the night and then they find it very difficult to study at school and lead a normal life during the day."
"We believe that this law is necessary to protect your health and your right to sleep," said AFP Jo Rin, ministerial leader in charge of the bill.
Online gadgets will again be available at 6 a.m.
The companies will have one year to prepare for the implementation of the law, he says.
The government also intends to ask the companies to limit the time of daily access to these games when the parents of young people so request, he added.
In addition, it will be made available to those who so request for free computer programs to limit the time of Internet connection.
South Korea is one of the most "connected" countries and the newspapers regularly give news about various facts linked to intensive use of the Internet.
The country is also the undisputed champion of world online gambling racing.
In November, South Korea garnered four gold medals at the World Cyber Games, where it faced 58 other countries.
World Cup 2018-2022: FIFA Appoints Organizing Countries
Amid the thunderstorm sparked by suspicions of corruption, FIFA's executive committee on Thursday appointed the organizing countries of the 2018 and 2022 World Cautions, a clearly political choice where influence struggles and lobbying can come to a primary over technical criteria.
One thing is clear, the 2018 edition of FIFA's competition will be celebrated in Europe, a traditional bastion of the discipline (four nominees: England, Russia, Spain-Portugal and the Netherlands-Belgium).
The 2022 edition will be much more exotic, given that the International Federation hopes to conquer new territories (Qatar, Australia), after a premiere on the African continent in 2010, or to continue its implementation in the United States or in the Far East (Japan, South Korea).
The list of personalities that were members of the delegations (David Cameron, Prince William, José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero, José Socrates, Bill Clinton, the Emir of Qatar, the Heads of the Belgian and Dutch Government, Zidane, Beckham, Cruyff) attests the economic and political interests that are at stake in a World Cup.
