According to FIFA &apos; s evaluation report, the Spanish-Portuguese (2018) candidacies (2022) have better arguments than their competitors.
However, these considerations will not have too much weight for the 22 members of the executive committee when it comes to voting.
The Sunday Times, responsible for the revelations that led to the suspension of corruption of two EC members (Reynald Temarii and Amos Adamu), could thus have spoiled the opportunities of his country, the cradle of soccer who only hosted the World Cup once, in 1966.
The BBC added firewood on Monday by broadcasting a documentary that has exposed three influential EC members, Ricardo Teixeira, Nicolas Leoz and Issa Hayatou.
Enough to disguse FIFA and embarrass English officials, who have thrown themselves into intense lobbying in recent weeks with David Cameron at the helm.
The Head of Government is hoping to re-emerge the successful coup by his predecessor, Tony Blair, who had won the organization of the JJ.OO. 2012.
This intense activity will be enough to regain England's brilliance before the EC?
It is not clear, since the Spain-Portugal duo also have numerous strengths thanks to their stadiums, already ready, their experience (Mundial of 82 in Spain, JJ.OO in Barcelona in 1992, European Cup in 2004 in Portugal) and their football culture.
But the bleak economic and financial perspectives of both countries could tear down their ambitions.
The possible reprisals against the English and the doubts about the solvency of Spain and Portugal all they manage is to give points to Russia, turned into a weightless outsider despite the loopholes in his candidacy (stadians, hotels, transport).
However, Prime Minister Vladimir Putin decided not to travel to Zurich and has denounced "unfair competition in the preparation of the vote," in a direct allusion to the British press's accusations against EC members.
"They're flashing their dirty rags, trying to compromise them," Putin said.
By 2022, the U.S., which has the experience of 1994, seems the most logical favorites.
World's top economic power and headquarters of FIFA's main sponsors, the country appears to take advantage to Japan and South Korea, which have separately developed an excellent project.
However, these new candidacies appear premature for two countries that have jointly organized the championship in 2002.
Another risk: the concession of the 2026 World Cup to China, fervently desired by FIFA, which would cause Asia to run out of chances by 2022.
Together with Qatar, which has presented its project based on futuristic stadiums, and Australia, FIFA would continue its expansion towards new horizons, but both countries present more risks than certainties.
Australia, like Japan and South Korea, could also be harmed by the weakness of paid TV rights (depending on the hourly difference) by European countries, the backbone of war for FIFA.
