Euro: the ECB is forced to maintain its acquisitions of State Letters
The last meeting of the Board of Governors of the European Central Bank (ECB) of the year should make it possible to specify the arrangements for the exit from the crisis or, at least, announce the interruption of a large number of non-conventional monetary policy operations which should be completed by the end of the year.
cause of the European sovereign debt crisis, the Frankfurt issuing institute certainly cannot be allowed to be so peremptory if it does not want to risk aggravate a more than complicated situation today in the European bond markets.
The political cost would be just as lofty.
Yesterday, during a press conference, French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde supported the active role played by the ECB in favour of resolving the debt crisis of European states.
He didn't want to say whether he believed that the central bank should do more, increasing its state bond-buying program, for example.
"I reserve my opinion for my European colleagues...."
The day before, in Brussels, during his hearing before the Committee on Economic and Monetary Affairs of the European Parliament, the President of the ECB, Jean-Claude Trichet, was no more eloquent in terms of his intentions, indicating only that the programme to purchase State Letters on the secondary market "continued" and that "we will see what we decided" during Thursday's meeting.
The only thing that becomes clear is that the ECB will keep its main rate unchanged at 1%, the lowest level in its history since May 2009.
In practice, the ECB's acquisition of treasury bonds has doubled last week to about 1.35 billion euros.
In addition, according to indiscreet market operators, yesterday it would have again purchased Irish and Portuguese securities.
In total, the central banks of the eurosystem have acquired securities amounting to EUR 67 billion since May 2010.
Among the operators on the market, there is little doubt that the purchases will follow (see page 29).
Economists also don't foresee any significant course changes.
Although loan operations for more than three months have already vanished from the ECB &apos; s arsenal, it still makes unlimited allocations of liquidity to beneficial conditions with durations ranging from a week to three months.
Economists are expecting this device to be extended today for a few months.
"I don't imagine it's going to significantly modify your show."
"If you wouldn't run the risk of causing new tensions in the short term," says Michael Schubert, an economist at Commerzbank.
"It seems more likely that there will be a progressive exit announced during the first trimester of 2011," anticipates Norbert Br?ms, an economist in Sal.
- Oppenheim.
With regard to interventions in the public debt market, "the ECB should maintain a purchase volume of those bonds so that the market remains stable," adds Norbert Brims.
Taking into account the importance of European bank exposures to euro area sovereign debt markets, the issuing institute has no choice but to maintain its presence on the market.
&quot; "It must compensate at any price for the lack of investors in the market and act in such a way as to prevent a marked increase in the returns paid by the most fragile states," a major bond manager said yesterday.
Because, if interest rates are put through the clouds, states will have even more trouble reducing their indebtedness, or even stabilizing it.
That's the price of protecting the euro.
In Italy the Berlusconi government craves the storm
After Italy began the week in a disturbing way, the situation improved slightly yesterday with the reflux of 10-year bond rates.
A recovery from precarious calm.
Upon leaving a meeting with the Minister of the Economy, the regional presidents have stated that they listened to Giulio Tremonti described the situation as "unsuccessful" and an "unpredictable" weekend was expected, statements later denied by the interested party.
In any case, on Tuesday the difference with Germany's long-term rates had reached a record 210 basis points and the price of bankrupt hedging contracts (CRQ) had skyrocketed to 263 points, triggering some uproar in Rome.
In the Council of Ministers, Silvio Berlusconi commented that, in Spain, the difference with German rates was greater than 400 basis points.
But his right-argued arm, Gianni Letta, was "deeply concerned" about "the turbulence in the markets" and a possible contagion from Ireland.
"The good news is that Italy, the third-largest economy in the euro area, is not like Greece or Ireland."
"The bad news is that it's like Portugal," says Irwin Stelzer, an economist at the Hudson Institute.
In fact, its problem is the lack of growth and the stagnant income associated with it, which causes the State to run the risk of not being able to meet its funding needs.
Brussels' latest forecasts have meant a jar of cool water for the government.
Italian growth is expected to reach 1.1 per cent in 2011 to 1.4 per cent in 2012, while the budget being scrutinised in the Senate is based on figures of 1.3 per cent and 2 per cent, respectively.
Under these conditions, according to the European Commission, the government deficit will represent 4.3% of GDP next year (compared to 3.9% foreseen by the government) and 3.5% in 2012 (rather than 2.7%).
Hence the rumor that new budgetary provisions can be implemented after the festivities, amounting to 7 billion euros.
&quot; If necessary, new austerity measures should be taken," says Commissioner for Economic Affairs Olli Rehn.
At the moment, Italy enjoys the support of the international institutions.
"The foundations of its economy are in order and its accounts in good condition, as we have been saying for a long time," says Arrigo Sadun, the Italian administrator at the International Monetary Fund responsible for Italy at the IMF.
"There's no immediate problem either in the short or long-term, because in spite of its considerable debt (119.2% of GDP in 2011, NDLR), the stabilization efforts requested from Italy are lower than those required of other countries," says Pier Carlo Padoan, chief economist at the OECD.
There remains the problem of political instability.
On December 14, Silvio Berlusconi will face a motion of censure in the Chamber of Deputies, after which elections could be anticipated at the end of March.
In that case, it would open an unfazed period for decision-making in the event of a serious crisis.
The Europeans reflect on the strengthening of mechanisms to combat the crisis
Christine Lagarde has already repeated it yesterday during a press conference in Paris: "I think we have already demonstrated on Sunday, at night and in the day, that Europeans are clearly supportive, determined, committed to the defense of their currency and its monetary area."
Although it was a relative, yesterday's relaxation in the rates of the obligations of the European states has boosted governments and the ECB.
However, we cannot yet speak of a principle of normalization.
Qualification agency Standard & Poor's announced that it could lower Portugal's debt rating, the most common target of speculation today.
"Like in May, there's a real lack of political clarity and transparency, which weighs significantly on banking systems," Exane BNP Paribas economists said.
Especially because "the political rhythm is much slower than the pace of the markets."
Conscious of this hiatus and the dangers it hides, the European countries are intensifying the discussions in order to strengthen the existing mechanisms of exit from the crisis.
Informal consultations between finance ministers who are most directly affected and, in general, with their G-20 counterparts are on the right track.
And this also taking into account the meetings of the Eurogroup and Ecofin at the beginning of next week.
The ECB is meeting today.
On the agenda the type of unconventional measures to support euro area countries in the market collimator (see above).
With regard to the European Financial Stability Fund (EFSF), its executive director, Klaus Regling, began a tour in Singapore on Tuesday in Singapore to inform investors in the Far East about developments in the crisis in Europe.
Although the objective of these visits is not to raise funds, it certainly prepares the ground.
First EF EF EF Title issues are expected to take place next month.
Yesterday, an official U.S. representative told Reuters that the U.S. would support a potential increase in funds mobilised through an additional direct commitment from the IMF.
With regard to the front-running countries, Portugal and Spain would be prepared to adopt structural reforms, in particular in relation to the organisation of the labour market.
These reforms are in addition to the austerity measures with a closer effect.
Lisbon and Madrid want to move along a different path than Greece and Ireland followed.
They both want to avoid applying for international aid at all costs before implementing their own budgetary corrections and deciding the structural reforms they consider essential to increase confidence in a lasting manner.
The capitalist partners of the European funds are finally considering the options available to accelerate the process of normalisation of public bond markets.
By way of Paris, on 24 November, Axel Weber was the first to launch the idea that, if the EUR 750,000 million mobilized by Europe and the International Monetary Fund were not enough, the Union would not hesitate to remove the portfolio again.
The economists at Barclays Capital, a leading player in the European public debt market, believe an amount of an additional .100 billion would be sufficient, "preferably" in the form of a direct injection of liquidity.
There's an instrument for this: the European financial stabilisation mechanism, currently endowed with EUR 60 billion.
A council that could certainly be examined in detail by the European capitals.
Especially if market conditions get worse.
Snow and Ice: 24 apartments on orange alert
Snowfalls continue to cause circulation difficulties, especially on the secondary network in Brittany, and can affect a number of economic activities.
Twenty-four departments are currently on orange alert due to the risk of snow and ice formation.
Eure, Eure-et-Loir, Deux-Sevres and Vienne have joined the twenty departments that were already on orange alert, eighteen since Wednesday at noon and two, Dordogne and Gironde, joined the group at night.
According to Météo France, the heavy snow-capped falls affect northern Gironde and Charentes, although it also smells slightly in the Isle of France.
Sengs of snow between 2 and 5 cm are expected punctually between 7 and 10 cm, in the affected departments, and between 1 and 3 cm, or even up to 5 cm, in the Parisian region.
The departments affected are Charente, Charente-Maritime, Cotes d'Armor, Finistare, Gironde, Dordogne, Poitou and the regions of Baja Normandía, Isle of France and Lemosin.
The snowfalls are strong enough to cause circulation difficulties and affect some economic activities, warns Météo France.
In the Cotes d'Armor, access to several departmental roads has been prohibited for heavy vehicles of more than 7.5 tons, including Guingamp-Lannion and Saint-Brieuc-Loudéac axes.
According to the gendarmes, circulation in Brittany &apos; s secondary network is tricky, so they recommend that it should not be conducted except in case of need and the movement of certain TER vehicles has been prohibited during the day.
On Thursday, school hauling in Picardy has been banned.
No specific measures had been foreseen on Wednesday night to limit the movement of heavy vehicles.
The prefect-of-prefect prefecture "requires all drivers to drive with the utmost prudence on the roads and to adapt their driving to the situation."
The Directorate-General for Civil Aviation (DGAC) asked airlines on Thursday to override 25 per per cent of their flights at Roiss and 10 per per cent in Orly because of planned savages.
DGAC advises air passengers to get in contact with their airline.
In Great Britain, Gatwick's airport, south of London, and Edinburgh Airport in Scotland are closed until Thursday morning.
Metro of the Grand Paris: early financial drafts
Since the debate on the modernisation of transport on the Isle of France has reached its peak, it rains billions.
At the very least, in the debate.
In particular, we're talking about the $23bn for the Grand Paris subway to be built by Société du Grand Paris.
To initiate this GSP, the State has announced an allocation of funds of 4 billion euros.
At the theoretical level, this sum will then allow the GSP to obtain loans on the markets to finance part of its double loop.
Today, at last, we've got the first bills.
The Amending Finance Act of 2010, examined in the National Assembly's finance committee this morning, contains the devices designed to find the first part of that money.
There are two modes: the creation of a special equipment tax and an increase in office tax on the Isle of France.
The first, as mentioned in article 15 of the text of the bill, is "for the benefit of the Société du Grand Paris public institution."
In addition, it is fixed in numbers: "Proceeds of this tax is 117 million euros per year."
The second is a modification of an office tax that already exists "to modernise it, increase its product and allocate this increase to Société du Grand Paris."
How much would you get with this method?
- Who knows.
There are no figures in the text.
But it's away from $4 billion.
"It is specified that the fiscal resources are in addition to the 4 billion euros of the allocation of funds to be mobilised by the State and which will be granted to Société du Grand Paris as the project progresses," we can read in the explanatory statement.
The SGP doesn't be surprised by this system.
"From the moment income is created (the two taxes, ndlr), we can cover the studios for three years and then receive the 4 billion later, we explain."
"It shall be recorded in the budget and will be used according to the requirements."
The SGP estimates that it will begin to receive 4 billion in 2013 for the 2014 budget.
This device, in which the 4 billion promised is not included in the credit, can provoke a slightly lit parliamentary debate.
The 1st public session will be on 7 December.
Avenging Hackers and Spectious Spies
The WikiLeaks case has a dual value.
On the one hand, it unravels an apparent scandal, a scandal that only seems to be a scandal in the face of the hypocrisy that governs relations between States, citizens and the press.
On the other hand, he announces profound changes at the international level and foresees a future dominated by regression.
But let's go step-by-step.
WikiLeak's first aspect is confirmation of the fact that each file created by a secret service (regardless of the nation to which it belongs) is made up exclusively of press clippings.
The "extraordinary" U.S. revelations about Berlusconi &apos; sexanishes only reflect what's been written for months in any newspaper (except Berlusconi) and Khadafi's sinsely ridiculing profile has long given ideas to cabaret artists to create their sketches.
The rule that the secret files should only be made up of news of public knowledge is essential for the dynamics of the secret services, and not only in this century.
If you visit a bookstore dedicated to esoteric publications, you'll see that each work repeats (on the grail, the mystery of Rennes-le-Chéteau, the Templarios or the Rosacruz) exactly what had been written in the previous works.
This isn't just because the authors of occult texts don't want to do unreleased research (or don't know where to seek novelties about the non-non-non-non-non-non-non-non-existent), but because those who engage in occultism don't just trust what they already know what they've already learned.
That's Dan Brown's coping mechanism.
The same applies to the secret files.
The informant is lazy, just as it is lazy (or has little craving) the head of the secret services (if it couldn't be, what do I know, editor of Libération), who only considers it true what he can recognize.
The top-ranked information about Berlusconi sending the U.S. embassy in Rome to the State Department was the same as published by Newsweek the previous week.
Then why did the revelations of these files cause such a scramble?
On the one hand, they only say what every cultivated person already knows, i.e. that embassies, at least since the Second World War and since the heads of states can call themselves on the phone or take the plane to meet for dinner, have lost their diplomatic function and, with the exception of some limited representation exercises, have been transformed into espionage centers.
Any viewer who watches research films knows it perfectly well, we just pretend we don't know because of hypocrisy.
In any case, repeating it publicly violates the duty of hypocrisy and gives a bad image of U.S. diplomacy.
Secondly, the idea that any hacker will be able to consult the most secretive secrets of the world's most powerful country significantly affects the prestige of the State Department.
Therefore, the scandal affects both the victims and "executioners."
"But let's get into the true nature of what's happened."
Previously, in the Orwell era, all power could be conceited as a Big Brother who controlled every gesture of his subjects.
Orwell's prophecy has become a reality ever since, being able to control every movement thanks to the phone, every transaction carried out, hotel visited, motorway taken, the citizen thus became a complete victim of the eye of power.
But when it is demonstrated, as it happens now, that not even chlamps of the secrets of power can't escape a hacker's control, the control relationship ceases to be unirectal and becomes circular.
The power controls every citizen, but every citizen (at least the hacker, chosen as the avenger of the citizens) can know all the secrets of power.
How can a power be endured that can no longer even preserve its own secrets?
It is true, as Georg Simmel has already said that a true secret is an empty secret (and an empty secret can never be unveiled); it is also true that every knowledge about Berlusconi's or Merkel's character is actually an empty secret, unblemished, since it comes from the public sphere; but unveiling, as WikiLeaks has done, that Hillary Clinton's secrets were empty secrets means removing all power.
WikiLeaks hasn't hurt Sarkozy or Merkel any harm, but, yeah, and a lot, to Clinton and Obama.
What will be the consequences of this wound caused to such a powerful power?
It is clear that, in the future, States will no longer be able to place any reserved information on the network, since it would be like publishing it on a poster in the middle of the street.
But it is also clear that, with current technologies, you can't expect to have confidential conversations on the phone.
There's nothing's easier than figuring out if and when a head of state has traveled a plane and contacted one of the counterparts.
How will they be able to maintain private and confidential relationships in the future?
I know perfectly well that, at the moment, my foresight is a matter of science fiction and, therefore, novel, but I imagine government agents discreetly moving in errands with uncontrollable itineraries, carrying messages that have been learned from memory or, at most, hiding some data written on the heels of their shoes.
This information is then preserved, in a single copy, in locked drawers: at the end of the day, the Watergate espionage attempt was less successful than WikiLeaks.
In the past, I've written that tech currently moves as a crab, i.e. backward.
A century after unwitting telegraph revolutionized communications, the Internet has re-created a telegraph with cables (telephonics).
Visual (analog) films had allowed film researchers to study a step-by-step film, advancing and retreating, discovering all the secrets of the montage, while now the CDs (digitals) only make it possible to advance or go back from chapter in chapter, that is, to enormous proportions.
With high-speed trains, we can go from Rome to Milan in three hours, while by plane, with the journeys involved, we need three-and-a-half hours.
It's therefore nothing out of the ordinary that politics and communication techniques take us back to horse cars.
One final comment.
In the past, the press was trying to comprehend what was being secretly plotted at the embassies.
Nowadays, it's embassies that ask the press for confidential information.
Climate: France's splashes on its funding pledges
A year after the Copenhagen Agreement, it is time to examine whether the rapidly unblocked financing promises (spounceased fast-show or early) made by developed countries have been respected.
The French splashes have been unveiled.
Under the Copenhagen Agreement, developed countries pledged to provide $30 billion between 2010 and 2012.
The European Union pledged to contribute 7.2 billion euros over three years and France 1.260 million euros (i.e. 420 million per year).
During the inauguration of the COP16, the EU has put on the table its best cards to convince its partners that its commitment to fast-start financing is true: a report detailing the measures of its Member States, a press conference, a major complementary event, the distribution of brochures...
Translator transparency is appreciable and appreciated for what is really valid: developing countries and civil society would want all developed countries to do the same and with the same level of precision.
The EU announces it has liberated EUR 2.2 billion in 2010, which puts it on the right track to meet its commitment of 7.2 billion in 2012.
But, if we study the situation in detail, things are more complicated.
According to the Copenhagen Agreement, the money must be "new and additional" to ensure that a complementary effort is made, without prejudice to official development assistance.
Unfortunately there has not been an agreement on what "new and additional" means.
When the European Commission proposed harmonisation, some Member States, including France, have blocked the procedure.
At the present time, everyone chooses the reference that best suits him.
For example, the European Commission has taken as a reference in 2009: international funding is maintained and $150 million is added in three years for fast-start financing.
Other countries have selected similar references in terms of the year or the level of official development assistance.
On the contrary, France sees that the financing of "all the new projects" of the year 2010 is "new and additional."
In other words, there is no reference is made to previous years &apos; expenditure or the total level of official development assistance.
In particular, the total amount of France's aid to developing countries can remain stable (or even decrease), while it aims to respect its commitment to "new and additional" funds..."
Another crucial element, the distribution between donations and loans.
Once again, there is no standard and everyone chooses the calculation method.
Some, such as France, consider that each borrowed euro amounts to a donated euro.
Evidently, it's difficult to justify: the effort is not comparable.
Although the use of loans may be justified for certain investments that are profitable (of course, projects related to the reduction of emissions that are based on more effective technologies), it is impossible to present a euro that will be replenished as the equivalent of a donated euro... At most, only the cost of the loan for the country that grants the loan (i.e. the equivalent of an interest rate should be computed (i.e. the equivalent of an interest rate).
But France doesn't find such reasoning useful: it "repeats" its 420-million-euro commitment by making 91.5 per cent loans (Euro389.8 million) and EUR 8 per 8 per cent of donations (EUR 36.1 million); refer to the EU document to see it through your own eyes.
It's the only country in the Union that so frequently uses the loan.
Germany uses it by 46%, while Denmark, the Netherlands or Slovakia, among others, respect its commitments with 100 per cent donations.
In other words, when the majority of the European Union contributes a euro to fulfil its commitment, France only contributes ten cents and considers that the effort is comparable.
A nice stunt.
Ultimately, in its fast-start funding, France carries out a double abuse: on the one hand, in relation to actions over time and, on the other, in relation to the real financial effort.
Spending as little as 36.1 million euros, he manages to give the impression that he is spending 420 million "new and additional," as he had promised.
The manipulation of the Ministry of Finance (because it seems that Bercy has dealt with arbitration) could have a twofold negative impact, both within the EU (why were the other Member States going to continue to donate when they can simply lend?) and in the negotiations, since the developing countries are not foolish...
The chapter on early funding is a relatively short-term minutiae in relation to the whole climate negotiations.
But it could be a grain of extra sand in the immense machinery that is the CMNUCC.
Let's hope it doesn't.
Either it is necessary to learn from this first year of early funding.
In the first place, France could announce a change in its method of calculation for the years 2011 and 2012, with the intention of respecting its commitments and in the same way as the remaining countries of the European Union.
Especially France could advance the debates on betting on long-term financing (another essential chapter currently under discussion): we will not get rid of the "additional nature" or the method of calculating loans.
France could change its position to become as progressive on this issue as it is with others.
We therefore have the hope of the new Minister for the Environment, Mrs Kosciusko-Morizet, who will arrive in Cancun next week.
Surprising announcement about France's fast-throfts and its vision of "additional nature" and the use of loans would help to improve the level of international confidence needed to advance the negotiations.
Warning about pesticides ingested by children
A 10-year-old boy ingests 81 different chemicals in one day, according to a study published on Wednesday by the Genérations Futures Association (the ancient movement for the rights and respect of future generations).
The association, which warns that its results "do not have a significant statistical value taking into account the limited number of samples analyzed, but they are illustrative of the problem treated," acquired in supermarkets of Oise and Paris between July and September the food usually consumed by a child of about ten in four meals and a snack a day.
These meals taken as a model, which comply with the official recommendations (five parts of fresh fruits and vegetables, three of dairy products and 1.5 litres of water a day), as well as treats, were screened in lanalaborators for chemicals, pesticides, heavy metals and other contaminants.
The dish's balance is overwhelming: 81 chemicals, of which 42 are classified as possible or probable carcinogens and five substances classified as safe carcinogenic, as well as 37 substances likely to act as endocrine disrupters (PEs), according to studies by various institutions such as the International Cancer Research Centre.
"Although, in virtually all cases, the legal limits are not exceeded for each chemicals individually, our study clearly shows that the exposure of consumers to potentially carcinogenic contaminants is worrying," the association says.
For break-a-break, butter and tea with tea alone contains more than a dozen possible carcinogenic residues and three considered to be safe carcinogenic, as well as about twenty waste that can harm the hormonal system.
The apple, imported from Brazil, planned as snack for the school, contains traces of six chemicals, including a fungicide banned in France.
The same applies to Kenya's green beans, included in food, where the study has detected traces of a powerful insect insecticide banned in the EU.
In addition, Russian steak, canned tuna and even the bread bar or gum were dressed with pesticides and other chemicals.
In the faucet water, the analyses undiscovered the presence of nitrates and chloroform.
At dinner, the salmon slice was the most "nutritive" with 34 chemical waste detected and even the analysis of the plastic dish used to heated food in the microwave wasn't completely clean.
The end-to-last risk these "pollutant cocktails" for the consumer "probably underestimated," the study says.
The Europe Ecology-Les Verts Alliance (EE-LV), for its part, has proposed "in the face of the seriousness of the situation" to implement a moratorium on the use of pesticides to curb "the increase in diseases associated with civilization."
In July, a study by the European Food Safety Agency (EFSA) indicated the presence of residues of 365 different pesticides identified in the fruit and vegetables consumed in Europe, of which 76 in cereals.
